      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>POULTRY PEN.</head>
        <p>POULTRY PEN.</p>
        <p>c,,,,, ar DOG BISCUITS &amp; POULTRY MEALS ARE THE BEST STAPLE DIET.</p>
        <p>AGENTS:</p>
        <p>CARTER &amp; SON Ltd.</p>
        <p>BRIDGE ST., WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, etc. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>CARE OF CHICKENS.</head>
        <p>CARE OF CHICKENS.</p>
        <p>Chickens should now be doing well, and make the place full of life, 'writes Clem Watson, in the "Feathered World." , On no account coddle youngsters, although 1 do not believe in letting them die, which will happen without precautions to help them to live. While not coddling, see they have every protection - necessary against storms, and even told winds. We may get some frost and snow yet, so be ready for any emergency. Another thing—do not let the .young ones out in long grass while it is very wet, or, after a very heavy dew. Keep the coops facing the morning sun, and though this means east, you will find that an hour first thing is worth three in the after- noon. We are not`likely to, get very rough winds from the east now, but should a storm arise, the coops can be turned rouhd in a few minutes. With chickens about there must be someone else handy, because feeding regularly is 4hecessary, and there are other things which need attention, and the moving of a few coops can be easily done. The backyard fancier is often troubled to get enough green food, but now he should begin to look round, and 'find out where they have small lawns which need cutting, and who does the work, because these clippings are very handy and good for the youngsters Sow rape, turnip, or lettuce seed, and use as advised above, for fowls of all ages mast have green food, and there is nothing better for young week-old chicks. -</p>
        <p>On the question of feeding we are all at fault. It is often a case lately of doing what I say, not what I do. Foods usually barred have been brought into use, and ,are now being used regularly. Still, with yagngsters there is nothing better than the best, and only these kinds of foods should be used if good results are expected. The cost is more than usual, but it is only enough to make us careful in their use, without any waste, either on the floor for rats oI• birds, or excess prepared, which goes sour, and must be thrown away. Keep all the coops clean, and disinfect occasionally, for you can only raise strong, healthy chicks when the con- ditions under which they have to live are healthy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>VARIATION IN FOODS AND FEEDING.</head>
        <p>VARIATION IN FOODS AND FEEDING.</p>
        <p>To be successful in the feeding of poultry it is necessary constantly to vary the diet. This applies to fowls generally, but perhaps to none so much as to young chickens. No matter how good the food may be, variation in the dietary will react upon the system to a wonderful degree. There is a great diversity of opinion as to the relative values of .the dry and the moist systems of feeding chickens: There is, however, one argument strongly in favour of dry feeding. and that is the variation contained in the usual mixture of seeds. If this dry feed mixture be combined with one or two feeds per day of mash, or chicken meal, the system is as near perfect as possible. For many seasons past com- parisons have been made as to the speed at which chickens grow under different methods of feeding, and it has in almost every case been found that where the food is varied, better results have been achieved. In many cases, however, broken wheat forms the chief—as a mater of fact, often, the only—grain, and -results have quite upset preconceived ideas. But invariably when this has been the case the chickens have been given full liberty after the first few days, and they could thus obtain so much natural food, and other necessary elements from the soil, ' that very largely counterbalanced this lack of variety. There must be variation in feeding any kind of fowls. It is the monotony of feeding the same food month after month that causes chickens to show the inevitable "flagging" when they reach a certain age. It is also very neces- sary for the parent stock and for the laying pullets to have plently- of variety in their diet. Sometimes pullets are hanging back, and not giving the return that might be expected. If, when this is noticed, a complete change in their diet be made, it will very often have the effect of bringing them on to lay. This shows the influence, and how the variation reacts on the pullet's system. - It must tie understood, of ecrurse, that while variation is absolutely neces- sary. there should be a method in the way of varying it. The object is to get a well-balanced iliet—that is, a proper proportion of flesh-form- ing matter together with the right ratio of fat formers and heat givers.—Middlesex, in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>FARMERS AND POULTRY RAISING.</head>
        <p>FARMERS AND POULTRY RAISING.</p>
        <p>The Board of Agriculture and Fisheries have issued a special leaflet, No. 4, on the subject of poultry as farm stock. The leaflet points out that the increased production of food supplies is a most important consideration at the present time, and among perishable foods eggs and poultry hold a leading position. Hitherto up- wards of 40 per cent. of the annual consump- tion of these products in the 'United Kingdom, valued at perhaps. A:25,000,000 yearly, have been imported. As these supplies are affected by war conditions the need is greater than ever for development of our own resources. The leaflet lays 'stress on egg prduetion as in most:eases the most profitable, :and in this connection farmers can render most important service, namely, by hatching and rearing birds suitable as breeding or laying stock for sale to small holders, allotment holders, and other poultry</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
