      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 17, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 17, 1915.</p>
        <p>WAR STORIES:</p>
        <p>NEUI E CHAPELLE TRIUMPH.</p>
        <p>Thrillinq Story Of A Great British</p>
        <p>Victory.</p>
        <p>BAYONETS' "CLEAN PASSAGE."</p>
        <p>We have received the following account of the battle of Neuve Chapelle from Rifleman T. Cunningham, who is to be congratulated upon his posier of observation, as well as his faculty for marshalling his facts:—</p>
        <p>"April 9th</p>
        <p>"I am sorry to see the casualties- of our 'Terriers,' as I know most of the boys pretty well. Some of their letters are very interesting. I saw Rifleman McDonald's letter in last Sat- urday's 'Examiner.' Ile was in my section in the 2nd Battalion Rifle Brigade.</p>
        <p>"I would like to tell you a few experiences of our battalion in the trenches, but instead I will give you a few outlines of the 'Battle of Neuve Chapelle.' The victory itself advanced our line about a mile.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>What The Victory Means.</head>
        <p>What The Victory Means.</p>
        <p>"It cost us heavily in dead and wounded, but it will live in history as one of our greatest triumphs. The victory does not rest in the ground we gained; that was not worth consid- ering for that matter, nor the heavy losses we inflicted on the enemy, but the marvellous staff</p>
        <p>Preparations which imade our victory assured. There must have been 80,000 combatants of all arms. It/was not the overwhelming masses of guns, nor our superiority in numbers, but the brilliant combination of all which gave us such a great triumph. About 11 o'clock on the night of March 9th we paraded to proceed to the place where the battle was to take place. Our billets were about seven miles from the _ _ _</p>
        <p>t-i;t:Cies. When we got half-way we halted by the roadside, had our supper, and rested there for about three hours. Then we proceeded to the trenches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>"What Was Expected of Us."</head>
        <p>"What Was Expected of Us."</p>
        <p>"It was a beautiful moonlight night, sug- gesting peace rather than the great battle which was to take place. Knowing what .we were expected to do, and all eager to do our best, we waited for the night to pass. our officers were as eager as us and felt quite satis- fied that we, would rise to the occasion. We</p>
        <p>had had a good stiff preparation and plenty of rehearsals, so we had a good idea what we had to do. Every regiment which took part in the battle had a certain part to do. The Royal Berkshires occupied the trenches in front of us, and we were in reserve on the road behind , barricades which we had built for the occa- sion. They were to rush the German trenches and we were to go through and take the vil- lage. The bombardment was to start at 7.30 and the advance at 8 o'clock. About 7 o'clock an aeroplane was seen to fly over our line., and we were given the order to take cover. There was much uneasiness about it. What was it? Had the Germans guessed what was happening? Had this thing in the air come to betray the secret which had been carefully- guarded un to the last minute? As the aeroplane neared us a sigh of relief went up as the red, white and blue design could be seen. It was one of ours and it had been to have a final look at the nemy. Shortly afterwards two biplanes were 'Seen to fly over the German lines, but they had fb fly very low on account of the mist which had gathered. A hail of machine gun and rifle fire immediately. broke from the Ger- mans, and one of them was forced to descend. I learned ,afterwards that both occupants had been hit. The time was drawing near, and at twenty-five minutes past seven we were given the order to take rover.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>"Hell Broke Loose."</head>
        <p>"Hell Broke Loose."</p>
        <p>"A couple of minutes later and 'Hell itself broke loose'; the guns spoke at once. It was just as though the ground was being torn from under us. The sound was deafening—it set every man's ears ringing, every nerve on -edge, just as though we were being driven mad. We: couldlinow safely -look over the barricaaes. and one could see the yellow smoke of lyddite mixed with the black smoke of shrapnel, and beneath it great clods of earth being torn up from the German trenches. But Worse than all was the maddening roar of each explosion. At eight o'clock the guns were lifted on Neuve Chapelle. We• saw the Berks climb the little ladders 'which had been previously put up, and clamber over the parapets. We then set out to 'scotch 'em up.' The order was to take Neuve Chapelle. "We went through our own trencYand raced like mad for the German trenches. We fought our way through the German trenches: wherever there was resistatice it was met by the bayonet, which quickly forced a clean pas- sage. As we drove the enemy from one trench so they fell back on another, prisonern being taken all the way. As the Berks fell so we took their places. It was a Pitiful sight, thousands of dead bodies strewn all over, the field, in some places three or four bodies lying across each other. But the most surprising thing_ of all was the enormous amount of food lying about</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>"Knocked Out !"</head>
        <p>"Knocked Out !"</p>
        <p>"When the German trenches were cleared we raced for the village, but about thirty yards from it I got 'knocked out of the betting.' It did not bring me down, hut as I looked down I saw a hole each side of my trousers. I had been shot right through the left thigh. I sat down and took my equipment off _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>I began to feel sick at the sight of it. There was a hole each side of my thigh about the size of half-a-crown. It seemed to have torn my leg open to force its way through, but I felt easy to know it had gone through. I bandaged myself tip and got up, but I fell down again. I was very weak. I lay there about- an hour. By this time the Germans had brought up their guns and shells began to drop "pretty thick, so I thought it was time I was making a move. I wriggled back through the German trenches on my stomach, and on to the road, a distance of about threequarters of a mile, stopping every few yards to get my strength back. When I reached the road I had about another half a mile to go to the first-aid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>German Prisoners As Bearers.</head>
        <p>German Prisoners As Bearers.</p>
        <p>'"I .sat down beside an old building and I caught -sight of some prisoners coming, escorted by the Indians, who had been fighting on our right. When they reached me the N.C.O. in charge, who was an Indian himself, came to me and asked what was the matter, in very good English. I explained to him and he brought two prisoners from the ranks and made them understand what he wanted to do. They stooped down; and picked me up, and under the N.C.O.'s directions carried me to the first-aid. All way going they kept saying to me, `Anglay Kamarand.' Oh, there's no one like the Eng- lish when we get the upper hand of them! I have heard a good many stories about the fight for the village, and I believe after a terrible hand-to-hand fight our boys reached the Ger- mans' stores, where they found a large quan- tity of ammunition and thousand of bombs, engineering tools, and all kinds of military tools. / I did not see it myself, so I can't tell yoU'exactly what took place. I was glad to get to England again, and have got on beautiful since I arrived here. lam expecting to bo home to see Chelsea and Sheffield United play the cut) final at Old Trafford. "I will Close now with best wishes to you and the readers of The Examiner,' from "RIFLEMAN T. CUNNINGHAM."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>GERMANS (REF FED UP!"</head>
        <p>GERMANS (REF FED UP!"</p>
        <p>And " Doti Rifles " hen " Charges.</p>
        <p>Writing fo his brother—Mr. J. Wright, of 93, Cartwright-street—Driver Stanley Wright, of the Army Service Corps, says: "I suppose you have heard of the recent great battle. . . . Our boys did some splendid work. They took about a mile and a half of trenches, and when the fighting was 'over there were thousands of the enemy lying dead or wounded, besides which many prisoners w9re made. . . . The Germans get 'fed up' when 'our boys get to close quarters. With the bayonet facing them they won't fight, but they just 'down rifles' and give themselves up. They are a lot of cowards."</p>
        <p>-Driver Wright adds that he gets the " Examiner " regularly and that it helps- to while away many a pleasant hour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>BY LETTER AND INTERVIEW.</head>
        <p>BY LETTER AND INTERVIEW.</p>
        <p>GEIIARN INNINGS CLOSING.</p>
        <p>Our Side That Will Bat On Any Kind</p>
        <p>Of Wicket.</p>
        <p>Sergeant C. Holt, of the Lancashire Fusiliers, Miller-street, Friars' Green, boot repairer, who is now in hospital in Liverpool, writing to Mr. W. Yates, 37, Slater-street, says "I now have time to inquire if you are still in the land of the living after all these months. I. remember that the cricket season is about to commence, and that it was at the end Of the season 1914 that the game com- menced in which we are interested still. Our opponents, having won the toss (doing the dirty by using a double-headed coin, no doubt) elected to take first knock, and, truth to tell, they have kept us pretty busy in the field up to the present, but our side is now getting the upper hand. With the advent of our new howler (titchener's Army) we hope to bring their • innings to a close. Our captain, who is a good one, named French, feels sure of our success, for the committee at Whitehall, with remarkable foresight, have placed a good batting-side at his disposal and Ile has a team for any sort of wicket. What price Gough and his merry Lancers for a fiery wicket, Smith-Dorrien and his brave lads for the sticky-wicket, and for batting in a bad light none can equal the Ghurks and Bengal Lancers. For a wet wicket we plump for Jellieoe and 'his invincibles; so you can easily imagine that' we do not fear the result. Of course, we already know the capabilities of their fast bowler, Jack Johnson. His pace is terrific, but his pitch is erratic, and all you have to do to score off him is to make a good duck.' It seems rather funny • that a series Of 'ducks' represent a good score, but such is the case in this game, however. Since August I have had the pleasure of a free trip on the Continent with the International team (and although the programme was not under the auspices of Cook's agency) yet the tour was very pleasant, though at times exciting. Fell Down a ()marry.</p>
        <p>"I had the misfortune whilst fielding to fall down a quarry and was most unfortunate to injure my knee (I was pleased to find my pipe intact), consequently. I have been com- pelled, for the time being, at any rate, to give up any idea I may have had of capturing his Satanic Majesty, Kaiser Bill. After a month in this hospital the doctors have per- suaded my knee-cap to leave the region of My left lung, where it had found a temporary home since my flight through space- (smells like aviating, doesn't it?), and to resume its place as advanced outpost to the back of my knee, and I am now coming along finely, and hope shortly to be ready for -another practice at the 'nets' in Flanders, feeling fit, right and merry as rain. Well-trained for the Trenches. _</p>
        <p>"Talking about rain—it was some pumpkins, as the Yankees say, and it was a common saying when one was relieved in the trenches to hear the .relief express the hope that you would' enjoy your 48 hours 'ashore.' Up to the knees, sometimes stomachs, for days on end, it was glorious! But we did not grumble, although it was inconvenient sometimes, espe- cially when one wanted to cut a dash at the opera—ahem! Of course, I was pretty well used to it myself—for had I not watched War- rington play football at Widnes and Runcorn? —and water did not present such terrors to me as otherwise it might have done. I felt sorry for those unfortunate chaps - who had only</p>
        <p>played cricket, the 'flannelled fools,' to be `Kiplingesque.' Being used to dry wickets only, it was very hard for them indeed, and they cut up rough on the soddened pitch, sometimes losing their wickets to that artful alow bowler named Rheumatics, and occasion- ally through being sniped, which is something like being yorked, stumped, run-out, 1.b.w., caught and bowled with a long-hop all at once. But you had this pleasure if the sniper had -not .disturbed your wicket much, just touching the 'bails,' as it were, of feeling as much joy as if you had just knocked a century, so proud are you of the small breath made in your defence, and you retire triumphantly to the Pavilion to get dressed, conscious of the fact that you have done a little bit towards keep- ing up the end' of, Britain's team in the field.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>" Spectators" Must Support,</head>
        <p>" Spectators" Must Support,</p>
        <p>"If the spectators (the great 'British' public) give us their best support, the winning hit will soon be made, and peace and glitdness will reign triumphant over tumult and strife throughout the land. The players of the inter- national team are looking forward to this sup- port and feel convinced they will not look in vain. The message they send out to the great. and glorious B.P. is "Be ' vigilant, sober and industrious; pull together and then the world will realise what a beautiful thing is meant by Britain's ,might. "In conclusion, I trust you are, like my pipe, of which I have previously spoken, well-filled and intact, and I trust we may be able in the near future to raise our glasses, charged with a non-alcoholic beverage, and exclaim God Save the King and • Good Health to England, Home and Beauty.'" ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>OBLIGING "TERRIERS."</head>
        <p>OBLIGING "TERRIERS."</p>
        <p>Give The Germans "All They Ask</p>
        <p>For!"</p>
        <p>Private Alec Livingstone--who was employed by Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., before he enlisted in the Warrington Territorial Service Battalion—writing to friends who live in St. Mary-street, Latchford, says: "We all spent Easter Sunday as well as can be expected. At 7.30 a.m. I went to Holy Communion, which was held in a schoolroom not far behind the firing line. About 10.30 a.m. all the battalion attended a short service held in a field. We could not go to a church, because they have all been destroyed by the •Germans. On Easter Monday we walked two miles for a bath, which went down' champion, as it was only the second we have had since we came. When we got to the baths we found that they were in an old brewery, and consisted of tubs filled with .hot water. Still, we had a time of our lives! On Sunday night the Germans tried to shell us out of our billets, but I am very pleased to say their efforts -were without success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Where the Honoured Dead Sleep.</head>
        <p>Where the Honoured Dead Sleep.</p>
        <p>I have just been to look at the graves of Lieutenant Seymour-Jones and Corporal Ball, both of whom we were all very sorry indeed to lose. Their graves look very nice, and are situated in a little churchyard. You would laugh to see us here—billeted in a barn and sleeping on straw on the floor, which is very comfortable and warm. But, unfortunately, the Germans have made a few holes in the roof, and when it rains it makes it very damp. The mouth-organ you sent has livened us up. A chum of mine is playing it as I write, and we are all singing. "The Examiner" has just arrived, and you should just see the crowd wait- ing to have a look at it. The last time we were in the trenches was the liveliest we have had so far, but the Warrington lads gave them all they asked for."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>" A BAD DAY" FOR THE "TERRIERS."</head>
        <p>" A BAD DAY" FOR THE "TERRIERS."</p>
        <p>Heavily Shelled.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. S. Steel, solicitor, Warrington, has received a letter dated Bth April, from one of the officers of the Territorial Battalion. In the course of the letter the officer says "Our boys are simply splendid and • are ‘doing splendid work. Our casualties have been daily increasing, and I suppose will do until the end. The men are most cheerful under very trying conditions. "We had rather a bad day yesterday.Wed- nesday.last). A shell caught one of our billets, killed one and Wounded another. Five other men were hit going into the trenches, but not very badly.. I think only one case is what you might call severe. Another man *is run over and had a rib fractured. The shelling habeen very heavy lately. I think they like to let us know when they get a fresh supply of ammuni- tion, as they occasionally have what you may call a spasmodic burst, and then it is a case of look out. Work done by the aircraft is:simply wonderf</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>NARROW SQUEAK:</head>
        <p>A NARROW SQUEAK:</p>
        <p>Comrade's Rifle Deflected 'A Bullet</p>
        <p>In The Nick of Time.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Willett, of Howard-street, has received an interesting letter from her husband, Private E. Willett, who is one, of the Warrington Territorials serving at the Front. "We have been in the trenches six days," he writes, "and we have had it a bit stiff since I last wrote. We have just now finished with the trenches for a spell, in'order that we may have a rest, which has been well-earned.</p>
        <p>Lucky Himself, But Lost Two "Pals." "I have been lucky, but I have lost two of my 'pals.' One is Fred Jarvis, who got shot through the head. He was an old schoolmate of mine. We buried him near the village where we are stationed. There were several mourners who were also 'pals.' Our colonel read the burial service over him. It was hard to see him laid to rest out here."</p>
        <p>"I am sorry to have to tell you," the writer adds, "that w` Stan ' Daniels got hit, but his wound was not serious.</p>
        <p>Tending a Stricken Comrade. "The bullet hit his rifle,, and that taok nearly all the force out of the shot. I was the man next to him, so I was told off to stay behind to see to his wound, which I found under the shoulder blade. He was very cheer- ful all the time I was with him. He may be home next week. A bullet also hit the rifle of another man next, to me. I do not believe the man would have been hit at all if the bullet had not struck the rifle. It was a good job for me it did so, for I was just behind him. After this, I think I am very lucky indeed, and hope to continue to be so."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>BRITISHERS STICK IT TO THE</head>
        <p>BRITISHERS STICK IT TO THE</p>
        <p>LAST.</p>
        <p>Something The'Germans have Learned.</p>
        <p>"If the enemy came along in millions we should still stick to our positions, firing away until the last man of us was hit. And the Germans know that, so they don't try attack- ing us too often; they have 'had some' before. It is such spirit as this that is carrying us through and will continue to carry us through until the decisive victory is achieved." This passage occurs in a . letter written by Rifleman E. Roberts, of the 2nd Battalion of the Rifle Brigade, to a of. in Warrington, Mrs. Sankey, of 99, Kimberley-street, who had forwarded him Easter greetings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>PROVIDENTIAL PARCELS. "'terrier" Vrites of Good Things That Arrive in the Nick of Time.</head>
        <p>PROVIDENTIAL PARCELS. "'terrier" Vrites of Good Things That Arrive in the Nick of Time.</p>
        <p>Private Duncan Munn, one of Warrington's gallant citizen soldiers, writing to his sister, Miss Doris Munn, who resides with her parents in Fletcher-street, expresses his pleasure at receiving a parcel, which had been forwarded to him, just as he came out of the trenches, and adds "You always seem to send these parcels just at the sight time." Six-day Stretch in the Trenches. "We have been in the trenches six days" the letter (which is dated April 2nd) goes on "and what was most, ,pleasing was the fact that we had very good weather, save for the fact that it was a bit cold and frosty at nights. During the days, however, It has been splendid. I wish you could see me writing this letter. lam sitting just putside our-billet and just in front of me the cooks are busily engaged cooking the dinners and dishing rations out. On my left, I can just catch a glimpse of the church; or, rather, what remains of it, for it is a complete ruin. , ,</p>
        <p>Sacrilegious Germhuns. "Before, the war it had a tower and a clock just like St. James', Latchford, but the Ger- mans have 'had a pop at it,' and they appear to have caught it right in the clock, as it were, #nd as a result all that remains of the time- teller is a few twisted works. . . . With the weather so delightful, the boys are all in good spirits and keep asking such questions as this: 'Are you going for a walk round Hill Cliffe this afternoon?' or 'Will you come and see the boat-race to-morrow?' . . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Boys Who Are Making Ready.</head>
        <p>The Boys Who Are Making Ready.</p>
        <p>"It appears that the boys in our Reserve Battalion who are training at periencing very much the same kind of training as we did—falling in at all sorts of time and marching to all sorts of unknown destinations. Well, they will soon get used to it!" The writes waxes enthusiastic over his most recent parcel from home, and says "I have already had a good feed out of it. I took the bread, butter and eggs into the local cafe and partook of the same with some .good black coffee." AN OBLIGING HEN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Lays An _Egil For Some " Terrier "</head>
        <p>Lays An _Egil For Some " Terrier "</p>
        <p>Nearly Every Day.</p>
        <p>Drummer Jack Raiford, of the Wallington "Terriers,' writing home to his brother and sister, Mr. and. Mrs. R. Raiford, of 3, borne- street, Warrington, says he is glad to say that he had kept well :and .fit, at any rate up to April 4th—the date of his missive. "In fact," he adds, "I never felt better _ We</p>
        <p>have got another trade now, and it is a sor- rowful one, vii., digging graves. We have been amongst it again; indeed„ wie are still amongst it, for Oberman • shells are sweeping right over the place. We are in the trenches as I write, and some others have got to move into the dug-outs But it's all for a</p>
        <p>good cause!-- I have got into this life now and feel like doing as .inuch as anyone. . . . . Nearly ,Battle for the Yolk.</p>
        <p>"I was very pleased with the ,egg which I received on Good Friday. On Easter Monday we were nearly 'scrapping' over an egg which a hen had just laid. She is an obliging hen; she comes alorby, almost every day to lay us one. On Easter Monday I was a bit lucky, for 1 managed to ' bag ' it."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>" Tommies " Put On Their Honour.</head>
        <p>" Tommies " Put On Their Honour.</p>
        <p>The gallant Drummer has also written to his father and mother, Mr. and Mrs. IT. Hal- ford, who live at 9, Walker-street. His letter is enclosed in a specially devised envelope which has lately been issued by the .War De- partment, and which, states, on the face of it, that the correspondence contained therein need not be censored regimentally, though the con- tents are liable to examination at the base. The flap has the following words printed upon it, the signature of the sender being required thereto:—" I certify on my honour that the contents of this envelope refer- to nothing but private and family matters."</p>
        <p>A Shell In A Billet Kills Officer's Servant.</p>
        <p>"I am still kicking strong," writes the cheery Drummer to his father and mother. "We are having another turn in the trenches and . . . I an:k receiving all my letters and parcels all right when once I am able to get out of the trenches. We get plenty of 'grub,' but the biscuits rather spoil it. . . H. Goodwin has just got wounded in the leg, but I don't think the injury is much. Last Wednesday a shell dropped right in our officers' billet and killed one servant and wounded another Gough has also been wounded; in the leg, I think."</p>
        <p>What the " Terriers" Think of the Strikers</p>
        <p>at Home.</p>
        <p>Writing later still to his parents, Drummer Halford says :—" I am sorry to say we have lost four of our Church Lads' Brigade boys— two from St. Paul's and two from Newton. . . . . We have read about the strike and threats pf strike at home and think it a bit rotten I was present at the funeral _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>of Corporal Jarvis—{who-se death was recorded in last w,eek's "Examinerl—and it was a very touching affair. We have made a fine grave for him One day,. as we were digging in a farmyard, we came across a lot of things which tile people had buried just before they fled. • It is a pity to see the towns and villages, out here, all in ruins." "We are only allowed to send one letter apiece ' per day," adds the writer. `111)or censor:" '</p>
        <p>WAR ITEMS.</p>
        <p>MARK OF HONOUR.</p>
        <p>In many places a system prevails by which cards are served out to the families which have supplied men for the Army recording the fact. These records are placed in the windo,ws and indicate to passers-by in ready fashion the homes that are doing their duty in the present crisis. _ _</p>
        <p>Captain Townroe, who is recruiting for the Territorials, is issuing a neat paper badge for this purpose in Warrington. It is shield shaped, A.urmounted by a crown and the Royal coat of 10ms, and contains the words.:—</p>
        <p>ON SERVICE.</p>
        <p>This House</p>
        <p>Has Sent</p>
        <p>A SOLDIER.</p>
        <p>NOT QUITE WHAT HE MEANT.</p>
        <p>Extract from a Warrington " Tommy's " letter home: "You might send some bootlaces —leather ones. I haven't had a proper smoke for a fortnight!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>GONE TO THE FRONT.</head>
        <p>GONE TO THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>We understand that Captain James Bain- bridge,, of the Reserve Battalion of the sth King's Own Lancaster Regiment (son of Mr. William Bainbridge, Walton House, Warring- ton) sailed on Wednesday, March 31st, to take up duty with the parent battalion at the Front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>POLICEMEN DON THE KHAKI.</head>
        <p>POLICEMEN DON THE KHAKI.</p>
        <p>The Watch Committee reported to the Town Council on Tuesday evening that 'they had granted permission for the following additional Members of the Borough Police Force to join H.M. Forces: Detective-Sergeant Holland and P.C.'s Taylor, Grant, Roscoe, Richards and Fiffe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>DA. G W. N. JOSEPH'S HELP.</head>
        <p>DA. G W. N. JOSEPH'S HELP.</p>
        <p>The Medical. Officer of Health for the Borough (Dr. G. W. N. Joseph) has been accorded per- mission to place his services at the disposal of the Government in connection with the in- fegtious work at the Temporary Military Hos- pital, Winwick—as the metamorphosed Lunatic Asylum is now dubbed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>NURSE SULLIVAN FOR THE FRONT.</head>
        <p>NURSE SULLIVAN FOR THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee recommended to the Town Council on Tuesaay evening, that per- mission be granted to Nurse Sullivan to under- take military nursing in France during the period of the War. and that her sister be appointed to take her place on the Borough Nursing Staff during her absence.</p>
        <p>\ MEN OF ARMY AGE NEED NOT APPLY.</p>
        <p>The Town Council have approved a worn- 'mendation by the Tramways Committee to th“ effect that no men eligible for service in the Army be appointed for any work in connection with the tramways.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>NATIONAL RESERVE.</head>
        <p>THE NATIONAL RESERVE.</p>
        <p>Those men who have been waiting so long for an opportunity to join this force will be glad to hear that another local company is now being raised. Men can be taken on pay at once, and the company parades daily at 9 a.m. at the Drill Hall, where names may be handed in of those desiring to join.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>POLICEMEN-SOLDIERS' PAY.</head>
        <p>POLICEMEN-SOLDIERS' PAY.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Watch .Committee have de- cided that married men in the Police Force who have either been called up to the colours or have voluntarily gone on active service shall be paid "such a sum as with their Army pay will make up their wages-and the sum of ss. per week, pro- vided that in no ease shall a greater weekly sum than 15s. be paid." Single police officers who have "joined up" are to be allowed ss. each per week to supplement their Army pay.</p>
        <p>SYMPATHY WITH COUNCILLOR ROBERTS "The Chairman (Mr. S. Brocklehurst) stated that news had been received from France that Captain 11. Gordon Roberts (son of Councillor 11. Roberts) had been wounded. The Clerk was instructed to convey the sympathy of the mem- bers to Councillor Roberts and to express the hope that his son would soon be fully recovered."--This was an extract from the minutes of the Finance Sub-committee of the Warrington Insurance Committee, which was approved at the last monthly meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>CARDS AND DANCES FOR SOLDIERS' COMFORTS.</head>
        <p>CARDS AND DANCES FOR SOLDIERS' COMFORTS.</p>
        <p>A social was held in the C.E. Mission School- room, Woolston, on Tuesday evening, the proceeds of which were given to a fund for providing soldiers' and sailors' comforts. Whist was in- dulged in, and Mrs. Range afterwards presented prizes to the following successful players:— Ladies: 1, Miss Bibby; 2, Miss Dennett; gents: 1, Mr. T. Buckley; 2, Mr. J. Platt.. Mi. Ryder acted as M.C. for the whist, and Mr. G. Platt for the dancing. Mr. W. Wainwright was the pianist.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON SOLDIER-ATHLETE WINS CHESHIRE R.F.A. MARATHON RACE.</p>
        <p>At Cambridge, recently, the race for the beautiful Cheshire Royal Field Artillery Cup, given by Captain Williams-Ellis took place. A gold medal to the winner, and silver ones to the second and third, were also given by Lieutenant Deiry. A fair number turned out over a course of about ten miles. These in- cluded Dodd and Brown, of. the Crewe Harriers, Sergeants of the Stafford Harriers, and Meredith, of Warrington. At the outset, Dodd made the pace warm, and led for nearly two miles. Meredith then took the lead, and running very strongly, finally won by about a quarter of a mile, thus securing "the cup and gold medal. A great ovation was accorded the victor, who was carried shoulder-high by his comrades.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>IN THE HUN'S HANDS.</head>
        <p>IN THE HUN'S HANDS.</p>
        <p>Among the local men who are prisoners in the hands of the Germans is Private James Collins, whose wife resides at 21, Catherine- street, Warrington. After serving a number of years in the Lanca- shire Fusiliers he returned to civilian life, but his name was retained on the reserve list, and he was among the first lot of Reservists to be called, up after the outbreak of war. He was married on Monday, August 3rd, and the following day got orders to join his regiment, being drafted over to France a few days later. He was wounded in the big battle of Mons and fell into the hands of the Germans, and he has now been a prisoner of war for upwards of seven months. Private Collins is only 26 years of age, and before being called up he worked as a labourer on the L. and N.-W. Railway.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>CAPTAIN RONALD WILLIAMS.</head>
        <p>CAPTAIN RONALD WILLIAMS.</p>
        <p>The appointment of Mr. Ronald F. Williams, Divisional Officer for the North-Western Divi- sion (Lancashire and Cheshire) Labour Ex- changes and Unemployment Insurance, as temporary captain and adjutant of the Ist Dock Battalion, Liverpool Regiment, was gazetted on Tuesday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>VICAR'S CONFIDENCE.</head>
        <p>A VICAR'S CONFIDENCE.</p>
        <p>"I have every confidence that it will end sooner than most people expect. There will be a great forward movement shortly, and we hope things will come to an end very briefly." This was the hopeful view of the war ex- pressed by the Rev. W., Bracecamp, chaplain to the Territorials, at the St. Paul's sale of work last week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>STRAINING AT IIIE LEASH.</head>
        <p>STRAINING AT IIIE LEASH.</p>
        <p>"Terriers " ailing _Patiently For A</p>
        <p>Forward Move.</p>
        <p>Private Alex. Livingston, a Warrington Ter- ritorial, writing to his sister, Mrs. John Bibby, of 17, Arpley-street, under date April 3rd, says: "We have come down for a rest, after having been in the trenches for six days, and I can tell you we needed it. You would have laughed to see us come in late last night, to be billeted in an .old barn, and to have a soft bed of straw, for which we were very thankful, after what we had gone through. We had a pretty lively time with the Germans, but the lads from War- riftton gave them more than they asked for. Our casualties were, I think, about a dozen this last time.</p>
        <p>"We were all sorry when we lost Lieutenant Seymour-Jones; he was one. of the best officers we had. But it is only what we. can expect; we never know who is to go next. The weather is keeping .grand."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>. FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>. FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>FOOD VALUE OF POTATOES.</p>
        <p>I remarked some time ago that the potatcr provides more food for our tables per acre than anything grown, and I refer not to gross weight, but to nutritious material. Thus an acre of potatoes is practically more valuable as food than three acres of wheat, and yet one acre of wheat is sufficient to feed six or seven people. The average reader does not, I fear, realise that potatoes have reached twenty-two tons to the acre, and that he can grow at the rate of ten tons in his garden if he tries. A quarter of an acre in the garden of the farm would provide, at ten tons per acre, fifteen pounds a day throughout the year. Suitable soil, a strong and free cropping variety, sprouted in boxes, good cultivation, a moderate dressing of dung, and the employment of lcwt. of sulphate of ammonia and 4cwt. of super-phosphate, or 6cvrt. of slag, will not fail to give a good account of themselves, and nothing should be omitted in either direction. When the potatoes are in full foliage it is a precaution, which a large , grower cannot afford to neglect, to spray the whole crop with sulphate of copper and lime, as if disease appears there may be otherwise serious loss.—Professor Long, in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>DEARTH OF FARM LABOURERS.</head>
        <p>DEARTH OF FARM LABOURERS.</p>
        <p>That there is in many district; a scarcity of skilled labour will be readily admitted. The response of the farm workers to Lord Kitchener's call has been remartable, more par- ticularly in the North of Scotland. I know of one Donside parish where practically every able- bodied man of military age is with the colours, and there are islands in the Western Hebrides where the whole manhood of the district is serving in either the Army or the Navy. An Aberdeenshire farmer of my acquaintance was left just at the beginning of the spring season with only one middle-aged labourer, all the others having answered the call of the drum. It is true that the difficulties of the English farmers are scarcely so serious. (Scotland, as the Prime Minister pointed out some time ago, has, in pro- portion to its population, -the best recruiting record of the three kingdoms.) At the same time, it need not be seriously disputed that in the agricultural districts of England there is a temporary scarcity of workers,• owing to recruit- ing and other causes.—William Diack, in the April "Contemporary Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>ALLEGED CONTAMINATED</head>
        <p>ALLEGED CONTAMINATED</p>
        <p>MILK.</p>
        <p>ii4;l3-fA</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Further Analysis To Be Made.</head>
        <p>Further Analysis To Be Made.</p>
        <p>A case of considerable importance to farmers and milk sellers generally was heard at the Colwyn Bay Petty Sessions on Saturday, when a local firm of leading dairymen were sum- moned for selling milk to the prejudice of the purchaser, in that it contained an amount of sediment, consisting chiefly of cow-dung, equal to 19}, grains per gallon.—Mr. T. Garth Jones, of Ruthin, appeared to prosecute, and the firm was defended by Mr. T. S. Steel, solicitor, of Warrington. The prosecution alleged that a sample of milk was taken from one of the defendants' carts in Colwyn Bay, which, when analysed by' 1 the public analyst, was found to be above the average standard of milk laid down by the regulations of the Board of Agriculture. The 'analyst, however, said the sample contained sediment, 27.8 parts per 100,000, which coil- sisted chiefly of cow-dung, equal to 191 grains per gallon. The analyst for the prosecution was cross' examined at considerable length, with a view' to showing that the proper method had not been adopted in analysing the milk. The wit- ness admitted that he had ascertained the quantity of sediment by volume and not bY weight, and by such method the sediment had not been separated from the moisture. Ile had not washed the sediment with- alcohol to get rid of the water, nor with' ether to get rid of the fat, some of which he admitted would come down with the sediment. He had examined the sediment microscopically, but could not give the proportion of road dust or sand, which would not be detrimental to health.</p>
        <p>The Inspector under the Food and Drugs Act admitted that if the defendants had sieved the milk through a gauze and muslin.eloth_ (whid it was stated they had done) he was of opinict that they ,had done all that could be reason- ably expected of them.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel addressed the Court at length on</p>
        <p>the case, and at the close said that after con-. sultatiog with Professor Lloyd, of London, awl other experts, he proposed to call on behalf of the defendants, and in view of what they con- sidered the unsatisfactory way in which the, analysis had been made, the defendants asked that the third sample should be analysed bY the Chief. Analyst at Somerset House. This course was assented to, and the case was adjourned for a month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>IN THEIARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THEIARDEN.</p>
        <p>POPULAR HARDY FLOWERS.</p>
        <p>Now is the time to see to planting hardy flowers, 'such as carnations, pinks, violas, pansies, pyrethrums, tic., and good strong plants of these can only be got by ordering early and from reliable firms.</p>
        <p>CARNATIONS. There is no 'doubt about the popularity of these flowers for cutting or decorative purposes' They will grow in almost any soil or situation, but they like a fairly rich soil, mixed with gritty substance, such, as old mortar rubble. A few good all-round varieties are: Amy Robsart (white), Mrs. R. Morton (apricot), Daffodil (yellow); Elizabeth Shiffner (orange), Duchess of Wellington (lavender), Mrs. G. Marshall (crinv son), Cardinal (scarlet), Miss Wilmott and Lady Hermoine (pinks). Fancy Carnations: Sam Weller, Linkman, The Nizam, Hidalgo, King Solomon, Rory Buchanan. King Edward, Alice Ayref. The above nre, mostly yellow and white ground, with pink an'-' crimson edges. There are plenty of others, but the above are good deters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>PINKS.</head>
        <p>PINKS.</p>
        <p>These can be treated like carnations, and the following varieties are all good for border oil' ture and double flowers: Her Majesty, Mrs' Sinkins, Alba. Major and Alice Lee (whites)9 Monarch, La France and Clove Pink (pinkp), and Charles Pink (striped with crimson).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>VIOLAS.</head>
        <p>VIOLAS.</p>
        <p>These beautiful coloured flowers are coming t° the fore with great rapidity, and no garden i° complete without a few of _ these charming flowers. These plants will take any amount manure, being very gross feeders, so land wel' manured will suit them to perfection. Tile varieties are legion, but the following varietie° are well worth growing: Moseley Perfection (yellow), Mrs. Chichester (White and purple); Bridal Morn (sky-blue), Agnes Kay (white en,a heliotrope), Cr. C. Murray (black and blue,P Maggie Mott (soft' mauve), Duchess of Fife (811'. phur, orange and violet), Councillor Water° (crimson purple), Mrs. Morton (sulphur yellosc)' Archie Grant (indigo blue).</p>
        <p>PANSIES. These flowers differ from violas, having their flower); marked with various colours, --ali2-B,,re inclined to be straggly or uneven in grovit' whereas the viola is generally of a tufty growtv; These flowers 'do very well in partial shade, cool, moist moist soil, and the flowers should be piciwr very often, and all dead flowers removed da if possible. Then a grand mass of bloom on eac; plant will result. Seeds can be sown now Olio giant type being the best) under glass to gisre good display in autumn. These, as with violas,: look lovely edging rose. beds, the soil for roa.er suiting them very well. There are many vsr,l,f; ties; but a gwd six are: Alice Lister, Jes°,:t Lister, Coronation, Councillor Mack, Rube' Ferguson and Jimmie. PYRETHRUMS. These are of great value for cutting in sPrwl. the flowers are on long stalks, and look Weir lent in the garden or in vases for house dec°!: tion. They will do well in any soil, and large clumps in a year or two, which prodilie large quantities of flowers. There are and double varieties, and both should fin' place in the garden. tiTi .Six doubles are: La Vestale (pink), Carl Nares (crimson), Argentine (white), Queen (salmon pink), J. N. Twerdy (deep raarc)"</p>
        <p>and Le Dante (rose and orange). -cf Six singles are: Carmen Sylva (blush), Kelway (cardinal), White Swan (white) let (rose), General French (crimson), J'Ailif , an'</p>
        <p>Huge (carmine).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>• – 0 , Iv; 6 • .1' • 1-</head>
        <p>• - 0 , Iv; 6 • .1' • 1-</p>
        <p>HERO'S PRAISE.</p>
        <p>(0 , .0/ Sal/S k Zam-Bu Private 4. id 4 Acton, V. grand thing. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Kept Fit by ZAM-BUK In Trying Campaign.</head>
        <p>Kept Fit by ZAM-BUK In Trying Campaign.</p>
        <p>HAVING just won the V.C. for twice rescuing wounded comrades under fire at Rouges'Bancs, France, Pte. 10684, Abraham Acton of "B" Company, 2nd Border Regfment, tells to-day of the share the well-known Zam-Buk had in his proud honour. This brave soldier, like thousands of others in France, always carries a box of Zam-Buk in his haversack-. Acton knows that there is no useful bravery without physical fitness; and it is because Zarn-Buk has so often con- tributed to the physical well-being of himself and his comrades, that he has written a letter of gratitude to the •proprietors of the celebrated "first-aid." "You can't place too much faith in Zam-Buk," says Pte. Acton. "I have used Zam-Buk for my feet, especially to keep Frost-bite out, and to cure Sprains; also for cleanly and quickly healing Cuts from barbed wire and other things. Zam-Buk is indeed a grand thing for every soldier on active service to carry in his haversack."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>AN EXPERT OPINION.</head>
        <p>AN EXPERT OPINION.</p>
        <p>The "War Office Times" after rioting the extensire use and success of .7am-Buk among our troops, wrote as follows on February 1.501: Zam-Buk is indispensable. Zant-Buk is the most effective alleviator of pain, not only for foot troubles. but for many other ail- ments and accidents to which officers and men on land and sea are liable. We should like to see onu or two bodies of this excellent ''first-aid" supplied to every p2an of the Expeditionary force."</p>
        <p>ICESf you hare a relative or friend --at the front or going, take it upon Yourself to see that he is at once provided with one, two, or three 1 rid. bores at Zani-Buk. They will prove invaluable in a thousand emergencies just as Zam-Buk is doing every day at home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>MORAL OF PRZEMYSL.</head>
        <p>THE MORAL OF PRZEMYSL.</p>
        <p>SYmbol of Austro-Ilungarian Inepti-</p>
        <p>nide and Callousness. A Hungarian. corres'pondent, writing to "The Times," says:— M s of file tfaalilll ofeyWashburn's valuable description appeared the Galician fortress, which in histor. "The Times" of April 3rd, is an HungaleraiL:locument. To the student of Austro- affairs it discloses the very roots of the evil that cannot fail to compass the crumb- ling of the foundations of the Dual Monarchy. Imagine the bravery of the defending forces and compare it with the haughty emptiass of their leaders; think of the Cossack shedding tears at the heartrending spectacle of human beings devouring the raw flesh of carcases while their officers worry about their ridiculously exagger- ated smartness of appearance, and you will crowd into one mental picture the tragedy of the Austro-Hungarian people. Przemysl will be much more than a mere episode of the Great War. Its fate has risen to the significance of a sYmbol. Przemysl stands for the ineptitude of Austro-Hungarian officialdom', with all its miserable contempt for the dignity of human beings, which, one may fairly 'predict, will fall With the fortress. The Loss of a Rich Province. I do not propose to go into the military im- portance of Przemysl. It is sufficient to state that the fall of the fortress means the irre- trievable loss of the province which it was destined to defend. It means the loss of a terri- tory of 48,750, square miles, one-quarter of the surface of the Dual Monarchy, with a popula- tion of eight millions. It means the loss of One-third of Austria-Hungary's military power, Galicia having contributed to the army 30 per cent, of its recruits. Economically speaking, Galicia is a province of enormous natural wealth. Geologists estimate its coal reserves at 115,°00 million tons; it has been called the salt treasury of Europe; its production of oil vies with that of Roumania. Auatria-Hungary can- 112t afford to lose a province three times the size Belgium without relinquishing her claim to a Great Power.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
