      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>„ THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APR44 17, 1915.</head>
        <p>„ THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APR44 17, 1915.</p>
        <p>WAR SENDS up PRICE OF GAS.</p>
        <p>Council Approve An Increase Of Fivepence Per Thousand Feet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>SOME OPPOSITION AND CRITICISM.</head>
        <p>SOME OPPOSITION AND CRITICISM.</p>
        <p>The war having encroached upon our purses in most other directions is now to increase our gas bills. At their monthly meeting on Tues- day evening the Warrington Town Council by a large majority gave their approval to a recom- mendation put forward by the. Gas Committee in favour of a rise in the price of the indisgens- able illuminant and very convenient heat pro- ducer. The increase, as was explained in the course of a long discussion, is brought about by the increased cost of production, consequent upon the higher prices of coal now obtaining, and the granting of war bonuses to the workmen engaged in making and distributing the gas, and of allowances to the dependants of those em- ployes at the Gasworks who have gone forth to do man's work on the battlefield. The higher rate decided upon will Come into effect as from April Ist, and the increase is a substantial one —scl. per thousand cubic feet. Erstwhile Hie charge has been 2s. 7d. per thousand; hence- forth, until better times come round again, it will be 3s. The prompt payer, however, will get a satisfactory rebate on his gas bills, for if the cash is forthcoming any time within a month after demand, he is to have a discount of 6d. in the cost of each thousand feet consumed. The proposal to increase the price of -gas met with some opposition, on the ground that the working man and the tradesman would be too hardly 'hit.</p>
        <p>AN ADDITIONAL £ll,OOO TO BE FOUND. The adoption of the minute of the Gas Committee recommending the increase to 3s. per thousand cubic feet was moved by Councillor LOCHER, who said that, as an industrial under- taking, the gas department had to look ahead' in reference to their prospects. Their estimated expenditure was considerably higher than it had been in the past, and to meet it they had to propose an increase in the price of gas. He could assure the Council that the matter had had the serious consideration of the committee. He was bound to admit that there had been a difference of opinion as to whether the increase' should take place at the present moment; but at any rate the great majority of the com- mittee were in favour of the advance suggested. Extra costs that would be entailed in the pro- duction' of gas supplied the reason why the committee proposed the increase. The war bonus which was granted to employes of the department, it was estimated, would during the year ending March 31st, 1916, entail an extra outlay of £1,326. Allowances to be paid during the year to dependants of employes of the department who had joined H.M. Forces were reckoned at £1,222, and other items which helped to bring about the increase• might be tabulated as follows:</p>
        <p>WY, - Additional rates at is. 4d. in the 2 Estimated interest on expenditure for new machinery at Longford Works Estimated increased cost of various goods 830 Estimated increased cost of coal, 56,000 tons at, say, ss. Less extra for coke</p>
        <p>7,000</p>
        <p>equally shared between the gas consumer and the ratepayer.</p>
        <p>WHAT THE SLOT METER CONSUMER PAYS-</p>
        <p>Councillor WILSON seconded the amendment, but said that he wished to make it clear that he dissociated himself from the remarks made by the proposer, and that his reason for the action he was taking was that he wanted the class to which he belonged—the working clasg— to be put on the same footing as the larger con- sumers of gas. The more gas that was con- sumed the better it would be for the large con- sumer, who had a special rate and a large dis- count. He wanted the slot meter consumers to be put on the same footing as the ordinary gas consumers were on..</p>
        <p>Councillor LOCKER replied that the slot con- sumer got the benefit by reason of the fact that he got more gas for his penny; in fact, he got just about as much as would be represented by the discount on a quarterly bill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>COMMITTEE HOPE TO AID RATES OUT OF ENHANCED PRICE.</head>
        <p>COMMITTEE HOPE TO AID RATES OUT OF ENHANCED PRICE.</p>
        <p>That made a grand total of £11,337 extra. The increased cost of acid, which \ was put at £750, the committee expected to realise on the sulphate of ammonia produced. As a result of last year's municipal trading in gas the com- mittee were able to hand over, in relief of rates, £4,000, and they hoped to be able to allocate a similar sum at the end of the current year, if the Council agreed to the proposed additional charge for the gas. He' said "he hoped," be- cause the committee could not estimate as correctly as they would like to do owing to the unsettled state, of the world's markets, and, therefore, the figures he had mentioned had been placed before the Council as a recom- mendation in the hope that at the end of the year they would be in a-position to give a more exact statement of affairs. lie wished to with- draw a, resolution which appeared in the miputes to the effect that application be made to the Local Government Board for sanction to borrow £475 for the purposes of the gas under- taking as the Government had requested that no money, should be borrowed at present. Alderman TINNION formally seconded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>COUNCILLOR GOUGH SUGGESTS FURTHER CONSIDERATION.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR GOUGH SUGGESTS FURTHER CONSIDERATION.</p>
        <p>Councillor GOUGH moved, as an amendment, that the resolution of the committee to increase the price of gas from 2s. 7d. to 3s. be ieferred back for further consideration. He said there was a resolution passed by this committee that they- would not inerease the price of gas until the gas engineer gave his report on what it was costing the Corporation in extra war bonus on wages and extra increase on the price of coal. At the last Council meeting during the discus- sion on the budget Councillor Roberts attributed the increase of the rates to the rednction in the price of gas, which, he said, was a mWake. . . .</p>
        <p>Cthincillor ROBERTS:That was not the state- ment. I said that I warned the Council against a reduction in the price of.gas believing it would be a contributing cause to the condition of things which, now obtains. Continuing, Councillor GOUGH said that that remark was a sufficient signal, because Coun- cillor Roberts was the " daddy " to a lot of the members of the Gas Committee. (Laughter.) Municipal trading had been adopted by most Corporations because the community ought to be better supplied and more cheaply supplied by a public body than by any private limited firm; but they had got members of private limited firms on that Council who were there on purpose to help their firms at the expense of the community. (" Oh, oh !")</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>MAYOR INTERVENES.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR INTERVENES.</p>
        <p>At this remark the Mayor rang a vigorous fan- tasia on his bell, whilst Alderman SMETHURST observed that he "thought it was too bad." Councillor ROBERTS: I think it is stupid. The Moon: Councillor Gough, those members you refer to do not it here. Councillor GOUGH : I say it, Mr. Mayor., I am sorry if you don't like it, but we must have dis- cussion.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: Yes, but you must keep it within proper limits. Councillor GOUGH : I think I shall show reason for what I am saying. The MAYOR: I can't let you go on as you were going. Councillor GouGrt: I'll try not to displease you. What class forms the largest, section of the community? the Councillor proceeded to ask, and answered his own question with an emphatic "Why, the working class of course !" Most property owners, he continued, had just increased the house rent on purpose to recoup themselves against the advance in the rates from Bs. to 9s. 4d. Alderman Tinnion, at the Gas Committee meeting, moved the resolu- tion that the price of gas be raised from 2s. 7d. to 3s. A Profit Limit Suggested.</p>
        <p>The increase was out of all proportion to any requirement for the extra cost on the price of coal; or the extra war bonus on wages.`The com- mittee had a working balance of £1,500, and made a profit last year of £4,000, and also had a reserve fund of £lO,OOO. A penny per thousand on the gas realised about .22,000. The increase of scl: would •realise £lO,OOO. - When the resolu- tion was moved for the inerease, he (Councillor (Thugh) moved that a limit should be put on the profit derived from the sale of gas, to relieve the rates, at £5,000. Some of the members of the committee scouted the idea. Why were the Gas Committee seeking to make such large pro- fits on the sale of gas? To raise large sums of money to relieve the rates, and thereby help private persons and private firms to benefit at the expense of the working classes. (Cries of "No, no:") It had become a very serious ques- tion, whether the Corporation were justified in nviking unlimited profit out of the sale of 'gas on purpose to relieve the rates at the expense of the working classes. He -therefore- submitted that the time • had arrived when the Corpora- tion should take a stand. and put a limit on the profit making on the sale of gas to relieve the k•ates, or that the profits derived should be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>– NOT MORE THAN THE ORDINARY – METER MAN.</head>
        <p>- NOT MORE THAN THE ORDINARY - METER MAN.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILSON: You tell me that the con- sumer of gas through a slot meter gets the same number of feet for his money as the consumer through the ordinary kind of meter? Councillor LOCKER: Yes, I say so; in fact, I said so as emphatically as I could. Alderman TINNION : That is not so.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON thought Councillor Locker had made the case for an advance in the price of gas so clear that an amendment was hardly necessary. If a manufacturer saw that the cost of material for the next twelve months would be so mach per cent. higher than it had been the previous year, he had to provide ways and means of seeing that he did not lose, but got his own 'back. The Gas Committee had endeavouged to cut their coat according to their cloth, and to ask the consumers of gas to pay the deficit. They were business men, and it was their duty to see that they did not waste the' money of the public.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>RISE ALTOGETHER TOO AWFUL, SAYS</head>
        <p>RISE ALTOGETHER TOO AWFUL, SAYS</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR OSBORNE.</p>
        <p>Councillor OSBORNE, having said that he was sorry that any acrimony shoal] have /wen introduced into the discussion, gave it as his opinion that the irlcrease in price proposed wag altogether too much. At any rate—and this was his strong point—it was decided in committee that the matter should be left over until a financial statement had been sutimitted as to the past year's working before any increase was ' suggested to the Council, and he was, therefore, surprised when he opened 'his agenda, to find that it was suggested that there should be an increase of 5d., which to his mind, seemed an awful amount. Whatever they might say or think an increase of such a size was very largely in the nature of..a hardship on the poor con- sumer. The speaker went on to suggest that the Council would have seriously to consider whether, before long, it would not be possible to go in more generally for electric light, and allow'gas, which was becoming so expensive to produce, to go by the board. He suggested that the matter of .the proposed increase might be left over for anothor month, when they would be in the position of having before them a completed statement for the last financial year, and would knaw exactly how they, stood with regard to the gas undertaking. Then, if it were found necessary to put up•the price of gas—well, he was sure they would all accept the rise as uncomplainingly as possible. ks far as he could see at present, he thought the rise proposed was altogether too big a one, and was also uncalled for, and he certainly thought the proposal should be referred back.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR PEMBERT6N ADVISES ‘` GOINp INTO" THE RESERVE FUND. Councillor PEMBERTON, supporting the amend- ment, said he could not understand, as a busi- ness man, why the Gas Committee should be picked out for these rises for the purpose of trying to meet the increased 'rates of the bor- ough. He was afraid there was a spirit of retaliation in the minds of some members in regard to the reduction in the price of gas that had been carried in the Council Chamber in the past. There was an increase in the price of coal, but it was expected to be only tempor- ary, and if the colliery owners banded together and formed a powerful combination, and, would not reduce the price, the Government in office at the time would have to deal with the mat- ter. They had 0t15,000 in reserve, and in these extraordinary times it would be proper "to go into it." He did not believe in these extra charges. They should try to economise in other directions. It would be a grave injustice to the gas users if the price was put up.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>"SLIM AND SLIGHT ARGUMENTS."</head>
        <p>"SLIM AND SLIGHT ARGUMENTS."</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNIOic hoped the Council would support the resolution of the Gas Committee, -sayinc, that he had never heard such slim or slight' arguments advanced against any pro- posal as had been brought forward that even- ing. All sorts of slims had been named as representing the gas department's reserve fund, but as a matter of fact th,e Only reserve they had was a sum of £lO,OOO, and that they could not touch save in the event of some extra- ordinary call upon them, such as a huge gas explosion would involve.- Did anybody expect that the Gas Committee were going to make gas and sell it under cost price? They would certainly have a good chance of doing so if the price of gas was not increased, in face of the present difficulties. Ile defied any member of the Council to make- the gas undertaking pay, if they were to continue to sell it at the price that was being charged that day. The Alder- man went on to refer to the fact that the corn-, mittee were only putting the price of coal at ss. per ton extra, and opined that the gene'ral body Of the members would be perfectly satis- fied if the "committee could renew all their contracts at an advance of ss. only.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Not What May Be; But What Is.</head>
        <p>Not What May Be; But What Is.</p>
        <p>It had .been suggested that the Government might step in and regulate coal prices; but they were not dealing, with what might be, but with what was. The present price of coal. was regulated by the law of supply and demand, and there were any number of people quite prepared to pay, the advanced price, ana he did net hesitate to say that the committee would be called upon to pay a great deal more for their fuel than they anticipated. There had been some talk about the poor working man. Well, the poor working man had had to pay a considerable,increase in rent because they only got £4,000 last year from the gas towards • the relief of rates, instead of £14,000. If the gas 'undertaking had been able' to pay .214,000 to the rates last year, instead of £4,000 the Council would have required £lO,OOO less out, of the other rates. If that otlo-000 had Come from the gas consumers it would not have meant an extra outlay Of above 10. a week so far as the ordinary gas con- sumer was concerned. Councillor GOUGH Question!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>How. "the Poor Working Man" Loses 6d. a</head>
        <p>How. "the Poor Working Man" Loses 6d. a</p>
        <p>Wee.</p>
        <p>Alderman Tix.wiox went on to Say that the poor working man—about whom they heard so much—by having dear rents and chea.per gas, lost at least 6d. a week. It would be 'much cheaper for the working man to pay more for his gas and less for his rent and rates. The Gas Committee would have toapend at least £ll,OOO more—personally he thought that figure was understating it—and they were only asking for an extra .XlO,OOO in the, price of gas to meet that. True, that figure would allow of a profit of .R 3,000 or so, but he did not think that was too large a sum, but, on the con- trary, too small a one, to hand over in relief of rates. Referring to the point raised by Councillor Wilson as to whether the consumer who. takes his gas through '. slot-meter 'gets the same value for his, money as the house- holder who gets it through an ordinary meter, Alderman Tinnion said that there was less profit made out of the slot-meter consumer because of the interest on the cost of installing</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <p>such meters and the extra cost of collection and supervision. In. conclusion the Alderman said that the Gas Committee were not wanting to make an increased profit, but wanted to pay their way, and. did not, at the end of next year, want to find themselves face to face with a loss which would have to be thrown on the rates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS AND "THE DEADLY FACT."</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS AND "THE DEADLY FACT."</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS, who also supported the committee's proposal, said the cry raised that night -of the interest of the working man as applied to that question was about the most fallacious cry that had ever been brought for- ward in the Council. He was deeply surprised that a Man of the admitted financial acumen of Councillor Osborne should not be able to see the utter fallacy of the argument he had adduced. There had been a tendency for years to bring pressure upon the Gas Committee with a view to reducing its profits, and that pressure had eventuated until the profits had practically dis- appeared. When the profits were being made a very substantial sum was handed over every year in relief of rates. The present position was that there was very, little to hand over, and the policy he had mentioned had, therefore, resulted in a position which had brought about, to a very considerable extent, the last increase of the rates of the borough. There was no mis- take about that; they could not get away from it, for it was a solid fact.</p>
        <p>Smaller Rents and Dearer Gas Would be Better. Mention had been made of increased rents, and he contended that the tenants would be better off, even if the as had been at the highest price ever paid in Warrington—if the larger sum had been available in relief for rates. They were receiving lid. a week in the shape of cheaper gas, but they were paying for it, through the higher rates and rents, to the extent of nearly 9d. a week. "I contend," the Councillor went on, "that men like Councillors Gough and Pemberton, who come here posing continually—until we are almost sick of hear- ing the statement—that they are the friends of the working men, are the worst enemies the working men of Warrington can possibly have, because they are utterly ignorant of true finance, and absolutely blind— Councillor GOUGH : You cannot serve two masters.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS : Blind to the facts or the situation, which are sb absolutely clear.</p>
        <p>The Privileged Outsiders.</p>
        <p>I have protested, and shall continue to pro- test, as long as I am a member of this Council, against the inequitable position which makes us, every time there is a reduction in the price of gas, compelled to study the outsiders who have no direct interest in Warrington, except' what comes irom their occupation of business premises here, which is a separate and distinct factor. I do object to any financial risk being run by this borough for the benefit of outsiders, who do not care a " tup'ny cue" so far as Warrington is concerned financially. The outside portion of Warrington. takes one-fourth of the entire amount of gas manufactured in War- rington. If it is going to be impossible at the present cost to produce gas and sell it at the price at which it is now being sold, I cannot find words in which to express my opinion of the thick-headedness of the members of this Council who would stand by and see them- selves mulcted in a responsibility and possibly in a loss, and all for the benefit of outsiders, who have no jot or tittle of interest in War- rington, except when they come in a different capacity as ratepayers. Any deficit there might be would have to be charged on the rates of the Borough and not on the outside districts at all, and that would be a pretty position, wouldn't it?</p>
        <p>"The Biggest Blockheads-If :"</p>
        <p>The members of the Council would be- the biggest blockheads professing to be business men who ever sat round a table if they were content to run the risk of having to saddle the rates 'with a deficiency in order that those liv- ing outside the town might continue to have gas, produced at a' loss, practically given to them with a pound of tea. (Laughter.) That was the position that Councillor Osborne Councillor OSBORNE : That is not the posi- tion. •</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS: That is the position that Councillor Osborne is in effect, by his suggestion to-niglit,' aSking no to run the risk of bringing about. • Councillor PEMBERTON : Ridiculous! Com- plete nonsense!! Councillor OSBORNE : My_ position is this: Leave it over until we get a definite statement of the past year's working, to show us how we really stand._</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS: Under normal conditions there might be something to be Said . for that proposition, but the- present conditions are not normal and they are not going to be normal for some considerable period. We are .faced with the deadly fact that fuel all round, for whatever purpose it may be used, has greatly increased in price.</p>
        <p>What the Workers Are Realising.</p>
        <p>In conclusion Councillor Roberts said: If the gentlemen who sit at the lower end of the table are the real friends of the working men, all I can say is that the working men will realise sooner or later—in fact, they are realising now—what this friendship really amounts to, and many of them are crying out "Save us from a friendship of such a nature !" Councillor LOCKER amplified his previous statement as to the slot-meters and the ordinary meters, and said that, when everything was taken. into account, the slot-meter users got equal justice with the ordinary consumer. In- cidentally he mentioned, as german to the proposed advance in the price of gas, that a large Corporation like Salford, which had better mechanical equipment in its gas depart- ment than had Warrington, was increasing the price of its gas 6d. per 1,000 cubic feet. Councillor PEMBERTON : But their price is not -35., though!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>ONLY FOUR VOTES FOR THE AMEND-</head>
        <p>ONLY FOUR VOTES FOR THE AMEND-</p>
        <p>M ENT.</p>
        <p>The amendment only found folir supporters —Councillors Gough, Pemberton, Wilson and Osborne—and was consequently defeated, the committee's minute being then adopted. Some little discussion followed as to the Council's powers to dip into the special reserve fund set aside for gas works.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>QUESTION –</head>
        <p>A QUESTION -</p>
        <p>In view, said Councillor OSBORNE, of what Alderman Tinnion had said as to the reserve fund being set aside to meet any big contin- gency, such as a wholesale explopion, and that it could not be allocated to other purposes, he would like to call the Alderman's attention to the following item which appeared in the Gas Committee's minutes and to ask how he ac- counted for it :—" The General Manager [of the Gas Department] reported that owing to pressing circumstances and the necessity of having the Telpher machinery and other plant in operation for next winter, and with the authority of the committee, orders for the work had been given in anticipation • of the loan required for the same, and the Town Clerk having advised that the Treasury would not approve of the issue of a loan for such pur- pose, it was resolved that the application to the Local Government Board for sanction to the new loan for gas works purposes be withdrawn and that such sums as may be required to provide for. the new plant be taken out of the reserve fund:"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>--- AND THE ANSWER.</head>
        <p>--- AND THE ANSWER.</p>
        <p>After considerable discussion the Town Clerk replied that the special reserve fund referred to could only be used for extraordinary pur- poses and the fact that it was now practically impossible to get a loan except for the most pressing work might, he thought, be inter- preted to be an extraordinary circumstance. Councillor LOCKER added that the machinery which it was proposed to instal would effect a huge saving and he was quite sure that in a year•or two they would be glad that they had taken steps to bring their plant well up-to-date in this respect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>She Deserved Some Credit.</head>
        <p>She Deserved Some Credit.</p>
        <p>A little granddaughter of Sir Edward Burn 6. Jones quarrelled with her baby brother, and bit, scratched and pinched him. Her horrified nurse said reproachfully : " Th 4 devil must have put it into your heart to do such things." .</p>
        <p>Perhaps the devil did put it into my heart to bite," said the child, "but I thought of the scratching ctnd pinching."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S FIRE FIGHTERS. Brigade's Last Year's Work Reviewed.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S FIRE FIGHTERS. Brigade's Last Year's Work Reviewed.</p>
        <p>The annual report of the Superintendent of the Warrington Borough Fire Brigade (Mr. Prank H. Turner) was laid before the Town Council at their meeting on Tuditay. It stated that the number of " calls " to fires was 26, a decrease of four fires on the previous year. Three surprise alarms were also given at differ- ent places by members of the committee. The brigade's services were not required at six fires (three in the borough and three outside). There were nine fires outside the borough, and one false alarm. The total damage at fires during the year was estimated at £5,428, dade up as follows: Borough, £3,218, an increase of £1,839 as compared with the previous year; outside borough, £2,210, a, decrease of £1,910. NEARLY £30,000 WORTH OF PROPERTY</p>
        <p>PUBLIC WORKS IN WAR- TIME.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>AT STAKE.</head>
        <p>AT STAKE.</p>
        <p>At six fires the damage was under £3; at four, between £lO and .240; at three, between £4O and £100; at two, between £lOO and £200; at five,. between £2OO and .2260; at four, between £4OO and £685; and at one the damage was over £l,OOO. The total value of the property at the risk of the fires was £28,280. In one case the fire extended to adjoining premises, and in three -cases it extended-beyond one floor. The number of fires in lock-up shops was eight, and it was found necessary to send more than one engine to three fires. The brigade was engaged 132 hours, and 24 extra men were called in to assist them during the year. The total dis- tance travelled by the engine f to fires was 106 4/10ths miles; the motor engine beitig driven 31 4/10ths miles, and the steamers 75 miles. The fire bell was rung 24 times, and the average attendance of firemen. was 18. The whole bri- gade turned out on five occasions, and the low- est attendance was 13.1 The staff consists of 20 'officers and men. A member of the permanent staff is now -always on duty at the station.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>FIVE CASES OF INCENDIARISM.</head>
        <p>FIVE CASES OF INCENDIARISM.</p>
        <p>The causes of fire during the year were: In- cendiary, 5; overheating, 4; defective construc- tion, 2; children playing with matches, 3; sparks, 1; electric wires fusing, 1; leaking petrol tank, 1; unknown, 8. False alarm, 1. The fires occurred at: Stacks 6, stables 3; Market stalls 2, dwelling-houses 2, sheds 2, farm buildings 1, motor ear 1, bakehouse 1, auction room 1, stores 1, cotton waggon 1, corn store and stable 1, steam joinery 1, rubber drying store 1, enamel- ling shop 1, tobaceonist shop 1. The water supply of the borough is improving as more hydrants are being laid down. No difficulty has been experienced in obtaining a sufficient supply of water at any of the fires which occurred in the borough. There are now about 620 hydrants and five tanks in the borough, and over GOO hydrants outside the borough. The fire appliances at the various public in- stitutions were inspected by the Inspector of hydrants and found to be in good condition. At the School of Art, the Fever Hospital, and San- key Sanatorium the staffs have been instructed in the use of the appliances. The appliances of the brigade comprise: 1 petrol motor fire engine with first-aid appliance, 2-3 gallon chemical ex- tinguisher, 2 steamers, 1 60ft. escape, 1 401 t. escape and hose carrier, 1 ladder truck, 3 ex- tension ladders, 3 ordinary ladders, 1 Pompier ladder, 3 hand pumps, 4 smoke helmets, 12 standpipes, hydrant keys and tools, 14 hand pipes,. 2 jumping sheets, 4 life lines and belts, 2 sets of harness, and 2,000 yards of hose. FIRE PRECAUTIONS IN PLACES OF AMUSEMENT.</p>
        <p>"Since June,"; -adds the Superintendent, "I have, in conjunction with the Chief Constable and his Inspectors, made an inspection of the various places of entertainment in the ',town, with the view to ascertaining that the fire ap- pliances are satisfactory, and also to ensuring that adecivate means of safety are provided for the public in case of_fire. During the period under review, I have (including attendance at meetings of .the Watch Committee and inter- views with officials, managers and architects) made over 80 visits in connection with this matter."</p>
        <p>The Superintendent concludes with an ex- pression of his thanks for the services rendered by the Chief Constable (Mr. M. Nicholls) and his officers and men, and for the valuable assist- ance rendered by the brigade s of the •Penketh Tanning Ca., Ltd., and the Latchford Steam Laundry Co.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>QUESTION OF HONOUR.</head>
        <p>A QUESTION OF HONOUR.</p>
        <p>Councillor Pemberton's Public Refer- ence to the Recent Action.</p>
        <p>Immediately after the members of the Town Council had taken their seats at Tuesday's meeting, Councillor Pemberton rose in his place and made a brief statement witt reference to the outspoken comments offered by his Honour Judge Gurner concerning the Coun- cillor, during the hearing of a recent County Court action‘ in which he was the defendant, and arising out of which counsel last week made an abortive application for a fresh trial. "DEFENCE AN HONEST AND TRUTHFUL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ONE."</head>
        <p>ONE."</p>
        <p>"Mr. Mayor," said Councillor Pemberton, "I ask your permission to make a personal ex- planation. No doubt you have seen the reports of a certainounty Court action in which I was the defendant. It is with reference to that case and the 'remarks made by the judge that I wish to say a few words. Acting under the advice of eminent counsel I made an applica- tion to re-open the case on Thursday last, and asked the judge to order a prosecution in order that I might have an opportunity of vindicating my honour and refuting the aspersions cast upon me by him. This he refused to do. My object in mentioning the matter to-night is to assure everyone here that my defence to the action was an honest and &amp;truthful one. Were it not so I should be unfitted to represent the ratepayers of Warrington in this chamber. I repudiate with all the power I am capable of the privileged expresgions of the judge of the County Court."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>NEW TRADE-WATER SCHEME.</head>
        <p>NEW TRADE-WATER SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Council Rescind Resolution Authoris- ing Progress.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Council's projected scheme for increasing the supply of water for trade purposes has been indefinitely hung up, the resolution which authorised the Water Com- mittee to take the necessary preliminary steps having been rescinded at the monthly meeting of the municipal authority,on Tuesday night. Councillor Pemberton proposed that the following resolution passed by the Council at a recent meeting be rescinded "That the engineer be instructed to prepare the necessary specifications, &amp;c., for the plant and appliances for the provision of new works, to be situate on land adjoining the electricity station ,and on the bank of the River Mersey, and that the Water Committee be empowered to advertise for such .plant and appliances at such time as they may determine." , Councillor Firth Lindley seconded on the ground that in view of the recent circulars from the Local Government Board re the borrowing of money for public works, he did not think it was worth while going on with the scheme at present. Councillor Archer: This only means a tem- porary postponement. The Mayor: It means rescinding the resolu- tion altogether. Before the scheme could be proceeded with. Councillor Pemberton's proposition was carried.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR GOUGH'S BELATED PROPOSAL.</p>
        <p>Councillor Gough was down to propose the rescinding of the resolution of the Council altering the scale of charges for water for manu- facturing purposes. The Mayer, - however, ruled that the Coun- cillor's notice of motion was out of order, point- ing out that the Council's resolution stated that the scales to which the Councillor apparently objected, should take effect as from March. 25th last, and as that meeting of the Council was being held on April 13th, it was no use putting the proposition to the meeting. The Councillor could do nothing in the matter of getting the resolution altered until the June meeting. Councillor Gough's resolution was left undis- posed of at the last meeting of the Council, there being no quorum when it was reached.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>t*:, 'l4 – *4</head>
        <p>t*:, 'l4 - *4</p>
        <p>t tit 1, It t, 4 %."; „irk, ,44-.,4</p>
      </div>
