      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>I GREAT RELIGIOUS FORCE,</head>
        <p>I GREAT RELIGIOUS FORCE,</p>
        <p>No Local Scheme for Unemployed Ensign Booth Speaks Of Her Necessary. Famous Grandfather.</p>
        <p>To the monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Council, held on Tuesday evening, the Finance and Estate Committee reported that at a recent meeting the Town Clerk read to them a letter, from the Local Government Board with regard to the organisation of labour, and the restriction by the Treasury of capital issues by local authorities, together with correspondence with the Treasury as to the issue of fresh mortgages for which their approval is required, from which it appeared that new issues would only be approved for the following purposes:— (1) The issue of fresh mortgages for the pur- poses of replacing and renewing maturing mortgages; (2) For schemes or works in progress for which the authority or sanction of Parlia- ment or a Government Department had been obtained.</p>
        <p>The approval of the Treasury will not be given to the issue of loans for any other pur- pose, notwithstanding that Parliament or a Government Department had sanctioned the borrowing of the money.</p>
        <p>WORKS IN PROGRESS TO BE OkLAYEO. It was resolved that the attention of the several committees be drawn to the restriction of capital expenditure referred to, and in order to comply therewith they be requested to postpone the consideration of any contemplated capital expenditure and of any tenders not already accepted; to postpone wherever possible the commencement of any works under con- tracts already entered into, and to delay any works in progress.</p>
        <p>A letter Was read from the Town Clerk of Swansea forwarding the following resolutioh passed by his Council, and requesting the Warrington Council to pass a similar resolu- tion :—" That owing to the exceptional financial conditions occasioned by the war, and the need for pushing forward with schemes to find work for the unemployed, the Local Go4ernment Board be requested to assist local authorities by sanctioning the postponement for one year of their obligations relative to the redemption of debt, and also to sanction the extension of the period of repayment by one year"</p>
        <p>COMMITTEE'S PRACTICAL SUGGESTION. The Town Clerk was instructld by the .com- mittee to reply and to state that while stlemes were not required to find work for unemployed in the borough, the committee is-of opinion that the suspension of the sinking f and and the ex- tension of the period of repayment by One year might be found of great assistance, both for imperial and• local purposes, in meeting any unusual demands upon the resour.:.;:s of local authorities during the continuance (1 the war.</p>
        <p>Local Salvationists had "a field day" on Sunday; when Ensign Mary Booth, a grand- daughter of the late "General" William Booth, the founder of the "Army," and a daughter of the present head of the "Army." " General ". Bramwell Booth, paid a visit to Warrington and took part in three largely-attended reli- gious meetings—two in the local corps' head- quarters in Academy-street and one in the Ebenezer Chapel in Buttermarket-street. The meeting at Ebenezer was held in the afternoon and was presided over by Mr. H. Thornton, who, in the course of a few opening remarks, said that during the past few cen- turies many great names stood out prominently in connection with religious movements, and among those names was that of the Grand Old Man of the Salvation Army, the late ' and revered "General" William Booth. It was when the churches seemed to be passing through apathetic days, when they seemed—at any rate, to him—to be somewhat neglecting their duty, "General" Booth stepped into the breach, with 'what splendid results they all knew.</p>
        <p>THE "GENERAL" A WONDERFUL ORGANISER. The Ensign, a young lady of slight stature. who wore her poke bonnet and the severe blue uniform of the " Army " with becoming grace. said it was difficult to realise what a wonderful life that of the " General " was. lie was a wonderful organiser, and in that connectiou she recalled how at the time of the Boer War it was said that what was wanted at the War Office was a man who could organise like "General" Booth. Even people who did not like the " Army's " methods—its uniform and itS drums—were bound to acknowledge that the " Army " was one of the most wonderful organ- isations in the world—perhaps, the most won- derfnl religious organisation considering the rapidity with which it had grown and had spread right round the world. As an Ameri- can millionaire once said to the late "General," "I can't say I like the Salvation Army, but I believe Almighty God does!" No doubt a great many people felt like that.</p>
        <p>Secret Of His Oratorical Success. Again, what a mighty speaker the old "General" was, and one of the chief reasons why he was such a successful speaker was that he so thoroughly believed what he talked about. Again, what a wonderful writer he was, wield- ing a pen that was as trenchant and convincing as his oratory. The one thing concerning ,her sainted grandfather .that made the greatest im- pression upon her was his great love, his great heart, his wonderful and never-failing sym- pathy for the poorest—for the outcast among mankind. To the very end he was never tired of planning some fresh way in which he could help the low-down not only from sinking lower, but also of raising themselves up, and the deeper they had sunk—even though the sinking be their own fault—the more brightly burned the flame of love in his heart.</p>
        <p>Always Tendering the Helping Hand. Always he tried to -stretch out a helping hand; aye, he not only tried: he succeeded, for through his instrumentality not only were hosts raised from the lowest depths, socially, but were also won for Jesus Christ. Once the "General" was asked "What is your favourite flower?" and without hesitation he replied— " You will find my favourite flower blooming where you might least expect to—in, the slums!" He not only thought about the poor, the downtrodden, the qutcast, and the sinfu} in a distant sort of way; that did not content him, but, instead, he devoted his whole strength, his whole genius, to helping them— helping them not only spiritually but also practically. He was not content to merely preach at them or about them; but if they would not come to him he went to them—went to them in the public-houses, in the back slums, in -the worst and foulest resorts where poor sinners congregated. And thus did he reach tens of thousands who had never seen the inside of a place of worship; thus did he take the joyous message to places where it had been but faintly, if ever, heard, for it was the wretched and the outcast that he most cared for, most Cherished; it was for them that he gave himself unsparingly.</p>
        <p>4 The Late " G-eneral's " Indomitable Courage. Another dominant feature in his character was his courage; his indomitable courage in the face of rebuff, abuse and misrepresenta- tion, for, she supposed, no man had been More lied about, more written against, more talked against, and more slandered than William. Booth. And in spite of it all he had unswerv- ingly persevered with his life's work, and to- day the harvest of that work was being reaped in every corner of the earth. During the afternoon the local Salvation Army Band played a number of selections in excellent style, whilst they also assisted at the other meetings. .</p>
        <p>The way to keep a friend is to see very little of him and to want nothing at all from him.</p>
        <p>The world was made for all, and it won't do for a few to try to fence it in.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>COST OF EDUCATION IN CHESH IRE.</head>
        <p>COST OF EDUCATION IN CHESH IRE.</p>
        <p>increased Rate.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Cheshire Education Committee at Crewe.on Monday, Dr. W. Hodg- son presiding, Mr.. R. T. Richardson, chairman of the Fin- ance Committee, submitted the estimates of elementary and higher education for the year. The total estimated expenditure for the year ending March 31st next was .2'262,371. The estimated Income was .2126,327, showing a deficit to be met out of the rates of .2136,044. The teachers' salaries were estimated at £179,000, as against £174,850 last year. A rate of Is. in the .2 would produce .2141,113. This would be e halfpenny in the X more than last year. The estimates were approved and passed to the County Council Finance Committee. The estimated expenditure on higher educa- tion was estimated at .258,148, leaving a defi- ciency to be provided out of the rates of .235,588. A rate of 2d. in. the for higher education was approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>4 Proposed Orphanage School</head>
        <p>4 Proposed Orphanage School</p>
        <p>It was reported that an application had been received from the Governors of the National Children's Home and Orphanage stating that they were contemplating the erection of a new and up-to-date school near Frodsham to ac-- commodate about 200 children of both sexes, and inquiring whether, in the event of the proposal being carried through and the whole of the cost of the building being provided for, the, Cheshire Education Committee would be prepared to recognise the school and consent to an arrangement whereby it might be main- tained by the local Education Committee. ,</p>
        <p>A resolution was adopted informing the Governors that the Cheshire Education Auth- ority cannot undertake to maintain the school proposed, but would willingly support any application made by the Governors to the Board of Education for an annual grant to the school.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>CORPORATION SERVANTS AND</head>
        <p>CORPORATION SERVANTS AND</p>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>No Rises Of Salary For Men Who</p>
        <p>Ought To Go.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Tow‘n Coun- cil held on Tuesday evening, Councillor' Wilson drew attention to a minute recommending in- creases of salary to five members of the Borough Surveyor's staff. Whilst, he said, he did not oppose the advances recommended, he would like it to go forth that the Council expected men, who were physically fit and of military age, to do•their duty to their country at the 'present time. It was an absolute scan- dal that there were young men in the employ- ment of the Corporation, who were fit to serve —and perhaps somepf them were recommended for increases of salary—but who were not tak- ing their part. • It would serve them right if The Mayor cut the speaker short by remark- ing that of the five officials, on whose behalf advances were recOmmended, three were unfit for military service, One had actually joined the forces, and the fifth was married. The General Purposes Committee thought that was quite sati§factory: Advances had not been given to any of those whom the committee thought ought to go. Councillor Wilson: I should think not. I hope it Will go forth that the whole of the members of the Council expect every man in the service of the; Corporation to do. his duty to the country at this time. (Hear, hear.) The recommendation was carried:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>WAR BUDGET."</head>
        <p>THE WAR BUDGET."</p>
        <p>In the new number of the "War Budget" the photographs depict life on board an armed liner, striking features of campaigning in the Egyptian desert, the destruction of forests in Belgium and France by the Germans, the method of protection adopted by the British troops against poisonous gases from shells, and the engineering skill which our soldiers are dis- playing. There are photographs 'which show indu- bitably that the Germans do not spare either the Red Cross or religious buildings. Photographs from East Africa, the Dardanelles, Serbia, the Carpathians, and other points of interest help to 'keep the reader in touch with the latest events in the world struggle. A specjal article entitled "Where the German Flea Hides," describs the Kiel Canal in detail, and is accompanied by a large picture map of the canal. The price of the "War Budget "is 3d. weekly..</p>
      </div>
