      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATIM,DAY, APRI,I. 17, 1915.</p>
        <p>TWENTY-FIVE YEARS OUR TOWN CLERK.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday, at the close of the Council meeting,, the MAYOR called attention .to the fact that. Mr. J. LYON WHITTLE had that day completed a• quarter of a century of. service as the principal official of the Corporation. During that period many important events have happened. The Water Works have been acquired, the .Tramway and Electric Lighting services have been initiated, innumerable street improvements have been effected, the Elementary Schools. have been brought under municiparcontrol, and scores of other matters closely affecting the material and physical in- terests of our townspeople have been carried through. In all these affairs Warrington has had the unstinted and loyal service of a man whose one ideal has been to do the very utmost possible for his native town. The condition of our town to-day in many respects bears elo- quent testimony to the ability which Mr. WHITTLE has brought to bear in the further- ance of his duties and in the habitually unsel- fish devotion with which they have, always been characterised. During the South African War and on every occasion when distress and anxiety (by whatever cause. occasioned) have been in evidence in our midst, Mr. WHITTLi has personally initiated and organised 'measures of help, which have always been tholiough and effective, and in the adminis- tration of which the human element has in- variably been paramount. He has been no perfunctory servant—he has .lived for War- rington. To those whose privilege it has been to count him amongst their personal friends, he has been invariablY loyal and even chival-. rcus—qualities which do not find too frequent expression in these days. We wish him niany years of !usefulness and that in his present anxieties—common to so many of us, but not to all—he may be-sustained by the-knowledge .that his only son is emulating the father's spirit in the noble service he is rendering to our beloved country in the present war.</p>
        <p>WOMEN WORKERS, MEN FIGHTERS. The old song tells us klaintively that men must work and women must weep. We are rapidly approaching the time when zoieh senti- ment as this will be, only temporarily let us hope, out of date, and when it will be true that men must fight and women must work. In very many cases our industrial ranks have been thinned already by the requirements of the: war, and the thinning out process will undoubtedly develop considerably as time goes otr. Some men must remain at their jobs--.—it is essential that they should do so, for war munitions do not come into existence spontaneously; but there are trades of a non- military type which will be drained fairly dry. ;yet it is necessary that trade should go on as usual if we are to produce the wealth that the guns will immediately blow into smoke— the most precious smoke this century will see. Can our women Polk help to keep the flag of industry flying at home, w4hile their husbands and brothers are keeping The Flag flying on the Continent? An effort is being made to collect trustworthy information on this point, and a preliminary meeting is to be held in the John Crosfield Memorial Hall on Monday evening to allow of the Government scheme being explained and discussed. Mrs. A. H. Crosfield will preside. Miss Truda Crosfield, , who came home last week, after having been on service for several months at the military hospital at Cherbourg—which is now closed, the centre of activity having been transferred further north--N‘jll 'be one of the speakers.</p>
        <p>A THEATRICAL SECRET. HOW TO POLISH YOUR TEETH</p>
        <p>Actresses obtain that brilliancy and snowy white- -- --- 'loss of their teeth with</p>
        <p>AURA DENTAL PASTE. Alt discolourations and stains will disappear from the teeth after using. From all Chemists, or send P.O. 9d. for Large Tube, or Two Penny Stamps for Sample Tube, to AURA, 3, DENMAN-ST., LONDON, S.E.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>MRS. CROSFIELD AND THE GERMAN PRISONERS.</head>
        <p>MRS. CROSFIELD AND THE GERMAN PRISONERS.</p>
        <p>Interview In Cell.</p>
        <p>tilE OFFICERS' PLANS TO COM-</p>
        <p>PLETE THEIR ESCAPE.</p>
        <p>We have received the following report, by a Welsh Journalist, of an interview in a prison cell between Mrs. A. H. Crosfield and her sister and the German officers who escaped from the Denbigh internment camp and were re-captured.</p>
        <p>Mrs. A. H. Crosfield and her sister, Miss Elliadi, who are spending ' a short Easter holiday at Harlech, had the unusual and in- teresting experience of interviewing the two German officers who were captured in the neighbourhood of Harlech and disturbed the Sunday quiet of that peaceful and beautiful spot in North Wales. Mrs. Crosfield and her sister, whose lin- guistic accomplishments are well known, escorted by a county magistrate, were con- ducted to the cell in the police station in which the two officers had been temporarily interned, so as to give the latter, of whom only one could speak intelligible English, an opportunity to express \ themselves in their mother tongue.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Feigned Cheerfulness?</head>
        <p>Feigned Cheerfulness?</p>
        <p>They, found the two fugitives, Lieutenant von Sdnders Leben and Lieutenant Hans Adler, the famous aviator, looking pale, un- kempt, weather-stained and weary, but with feigned (or real) cheerfulness, complacently smoking the cigarettes which, thanks to Eng- lish consideration, they had been allowed to extract from their knapsacks. These latter, wheti searched, were found to be well filled with cigarettes, chocolate, fountain pens; and a map with a clearly defined line from Port Madoc to the coast of Spain, showing exactly. the line of escape the officers had planned. "How did you manage to elude the vigi- lance of the police for so many days?" asked Mrs. Crosfield.</p>
        <p>" Oh I we had not the slightest difficulty, for we only saw four or five people in the course of as many days, and had it not been for pech und .schlechtes vetter ' (bad luck and bad weather), we should ere now have given everyone the slip and been well on our way to Spain !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Plenty of Money.</head>
        <p>Plenty of Money.</p>
        <p>" But how about nioney ?" asked Mrs. Oros- field. v •</p>
        <p>"We had ten pounds when we escaped, and still have five left," he answered, with a con- fident wink.</p>
        <p>"Are you very tired after your adven- tures?"</p>
        <p>" Well, five nights in the open fields make one look forward to A bed again, but we are quite Proud at having stirred up the English and led them such a dance; we have enjoyed our escapade immensely, and would not have missed it for the world!"</p>
        <p>"_Why? Does prison life bore you?"</p>
        <p>The answer was 'characteristically unGer- man : "Not when we receive such charming visitors; but otherwise the monotony of our lives is unbearable, as we are given no papers excepting The Times' to read, and are not even allowed any German books. After all, it is not surprising that we should attempt escape. Many English interned in Germany have done, the same, and often with better results, as the conditions there are much easier; here, one is constantly checked by the sea."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>An Irish Girl.</head>
        <p>An Irish Girl.</p>
        <p>"Is it true that you are engaged to be married- to an Irish girl?" "No; two years ago, when I was studying in Cork, I nearly succumbed to the witchery and charm of a beautiful Irish girl, but noth- ing further came of it." At this moment the police officer entered, and the prisoners were presently escorted to the car which conveyed them to Denbigh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>WOMEN FOR MEN. Who Are Willing To Work While The Men Fight?</head>
        <p>WOMEN FOR MEN. Who Are Willing To Work While The Men Fight?</p>
        <p>A notice has been issued by the Warrington Women's Liberal Association, coffin-, a meet- ing to be held at the John Crosfieid calling Hall, next Monday, at 7.30, to consider the questiOn of women making good, as far as pos- sible, the shortage of men in different trades. The Board of Trade has inaugurated a scheme of registration, and has issued a notice stating that if the full fighting power of the nation is to be put forth on the field of battle the full working power of the nation must be made available to, carry on its essential trades at home. Already in certain important occupa- tions there are not enough men and women to do the work. This shortage will certainly spread to other occupations as more and more men join the fighting forces. In order to meet both the present and the future needs of national industry during the war, the ,Government wish to obtain particula:s of the women available, with or without pre- vious training, for paid. employment. Accord- ingly they, invite all women' who are prepdred; if needed, to take paid employment of apy kind, industrial, agricultural, clerical, &amp;c., to enter themselvir upon the register o*women for war service which is being prepared by the Labour Exchanges. Women can register at the Labour Exchanges or on a form to be supplied by the local agency of the ,unemployment fund or women's societies.</p>
        <p>The object of the proposed registration is to discover what reserve force of women's labour can be made available, if required. It does not necessarily follow that women who are regis- tered can, or will, be offered employment. Nor by registering will a woman in any way pledge herself to take employment if offered, and will be perfectly free to refuse any offer if the nature of the work, the pay or other consider- ations are not satisfactory: Any Woman who by working helps to release a man or equip a man for fighting does national war service. Every woman who can work and is free to work should come forward. The Women's Liberal Association have been approached to assist in the work of getting a census of possible- women workers, and at the meeting to which we have referred Miss Truda Crosfield and Miss Knowles, organising officer, Women's Department, Government Labour Ex- changes, will explain the scheme. Mrs. A. H. Crosfield will preside. All auxiliaries are invited to bring two friends to ,the meeting. ADULT SCHOOLS AND THE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>At the Academy-street Women's Adult School on Wednesday evening Miss Ewart and Miss Marquis, Of the Warrington Labour Exchange, attended and explained the war service for women which is being organised by the Govolli- ment. The Government are prepared to assist women to train for such occupations as farm- ing, gardening, &amp;c., and other schemes are also being thought out. Women will be wanted in ammunition factories, clothing factories and to work in agricultural districts to increase the nation's supply of food. They will be required to take any kind of work which will release men, that they may join the Army, such as ,clerical work, shop assistants, &amp;c. Higher Wages. - - - - - - - - -</p>
        <p>Miss Ewart said that she had beard of several cases where women and girls have taken posts previously held by men and boys, and have given such great satisfaction that their wages were increased to a higher figure than was paid before.</p>
        <p>An appeal was made to all women present to tell all their friends about this work and to urge everyone to apply at the Labour Exchange any morning from 10 to 1 p.m. for a .form .con- taining all particulars.-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>SATURDAY, APRIL 17, 1915.</head>
        <p>SATURDAY, APRIL 17, 1915.</p>
        <p>LIVELIHOOD AND POVERTY.</p>
        <p>Warrington And Other Towns Compared In Ratan Tata Inquiry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WHAT THE "POVERTY LINE" MEANS.</head>
        <p>WHAT THE "POVERTY LINE" MEANS.</p>
        <p>Crushing Effect of Poverty on Children. Going deeper into the figures, The authors state: ", In Warrington and Reading the principal cause is the inadequacy of wages to support three children; in Northampton, the inadequacy is for larger families. In York, there was a large proportion of cases where the chief wage-earner was dead. In no town does permanent unemployment or irregularity •of work of a capable man rank as an important f cause."</p>
        <p>It is conclUsively shown that the burden of poverty falls with crushing effect upon the, non-wage-earning members- of the working class—in plain English, it means chiefly the children.</p>
        <p>If all the figures for the four towns were added together it would be found that 879 out of 3,287 working class children, or 27 per cent.' "are living in families which fail to reach the low standard taken as necessary for healthy existence." Taking the individual towns of Northamp- ton, Reading and Warrington, it is seen that in rough figures the following children are below the poverty line Warrington: School children, ; children under 5 years nearly 1. Reading: School children, nearly 1; children under 5 years, 45 per cent. Northampton: School children, nearly I.; children under 5 years, over 4.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>SECOND ARTICLE.</head>
        <p>SECOND ARTICLE.</p>
        <p>In our issue of last week reference was made to the fact that 12 per cent. of the working class families in Warrington were below the poverty line. Many Will be glad to know a little more as to what the poverty line really is: It is a term which was employed by Mr. Itowntree in his well-known survey of York.. The food standard set up was ,equiva- lent to workhouse diet and, practically vege- tarian.' Prof. Bowley's new standard assumes what is 'known to be true in practice, viz., that the ordinary working man does buy a certain amount of meat-21b. is. taken to be the minimum.</p>
        <p>For the new standard the food allowance is estimated thus: Man over 18 years, 4s. 6d.; lad 14 to 18, 3s. 10d.; woman over 14 years, 3s. Bd.; school child 5 to 14 years 2s. 3d.; child under 5 years, is. 6d. The weekly allow- ance for clothing and sundries works out as follows: Man over 16 or woman over 18 years, Bd.; all others, 7d. • Besides these amounts for food and cloth- ing, allowances have to be made for expendi- ture on rent (which is taken to be the lowest obtainable), also fuel and State insurance.</p>
        <p>It must be remembered that these estimates were drawn up before the war, and that the statements regarding the proportions • of families living in primary poverty refer to a period 18 months ago. The allowance for fuel in Northampton is only is. but it is not clear what amount is estimated to be the minimum fuel require- ment in Warrington. • It is recognised that fuel is more extensively used in the North of England than in the South and. Midlands. Probably a fair estimate for Warrington would be 2 cwt. per household per week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>What About Rents?</head>
        <p>What About Rents?</p>
        <p>The towns surveyed differ very widely from each other in ,their leading characteristics. Three..out Of the four are of somewhat similar size. At the time when the inquiries took place the following were the estimated popu- lations: Northampton, 90,064; Reading (as enlarged in November, 1911), 87,693; War- rington, 72,100; while Stanley stood at 23,294: The last-named place is of mushroom growth, and is situated in the heart of the Durham coalfield. Mining is therefore the predomin- ant industry, though,building and agriculture are fairly well represented. Warrington, with the • possible exception of Derby, is the most representative industrial town in the coun- try. Indeed, Warrington may be called a microcosm of industrial England. In North- ampton boots and shoes may be said to govern the situation. The building, clothing, brew- ing and. engineering 'trades are also followed.. Reading, of course, is famous for its biscuit trade, probably one-fotirth or one-fifth of the working classes being dependent upon it. But the town has other trades, such as printing and engineering and seeds, while railway work employs a considerable number of men. Here comparisons are interesting. The pre- dominant rent in both Reading and North- ampton is 6s. to 75., while in 'Warrington it is 4s. to 55., and iff.Stanley (Durham). ss. to 6s. among the rent-payirig houses (though 31 per cent. are let rent free .by colliery com- panies). Random estimates are often made as to the causes of poverty, and one therefore turns with a good deal of epectation to a book like "Livelihood and Poverty."</p>
        <p>The poverty here referred to does not in- clude that which arises from bad and waste- ful expenditure, including intoxicants. It is the poverty of inadequate income. To what extent is this kind of poverty due to unem- ployment, illness, or death of the chief bread winner, or to low wages? Here is a sum- mary of a table given on page 40, Stanley being omitted because it is g`-careely safe to generalist from such small numbers.</p>
        <p>Percentages ot house- holds below the Rowntree standard. Immediate cause of 1913. 1913. 1913 1899.</p>
        <p>poverty. W. N. R. T. Chief wage-earner dead.... 6 21 14 27 Chief wage-earner ill or _</p>
        <p>old Chief wage-earner out of • work or irregularly em-</p>
        <p>plived _ 6 -- 6 6 Wages insufficient to meet needs •B7 65 69 57</p>
        <p>'lOO 100 100 100 W., Warrington; N., Northampton; R., 'Reading; Y., York.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>CLARA BUTT CONCERT</head>
        <p>THE CLARA BUTT CONCERT</p>
        <p>Some 01 The Features 01 The Programme.</p>
        <p>As announced last week, on Monday evening, April 26th, Madame Clara Butt and her concert party will visit the Parr Hall. Warrington, and will give one of her world-famous concerts. Judging by all the signs a grand welcome se-ems assured the great Contralto. - There are few concerts nowadays that can command ,a full audience on their own merits alone. The others must have one branch or another of the countless war philanthropies for an object—while luckless professional per- formers galore must either give their services without fee or reward, or else endure the anxiety of knowing that their talents are get- ting lamentably rusty for lack of exercise. There are notable exceptions, of course, and Madame Clara Butt would be perhaps the greatest of them all but for the fact that she has volun- tarily devoted fame and fortune alike these last four or five months partly to the needs and deserts of those of her brave fellow-countrymen who are doing • their country's service, and partly to relief of brother and sister profes- sionals who have been hard hit by the exigen- cies of the period. Though the programme for the Warrington concert is not quite complete, it is probable that Madame Butt will sing "0 Rest in the Lord" from "Elijah," Elgar's "Land of Hope and Glory," the chorus of which will be sung by the Warrington Musical Society, under the conductorship ,of Mr. Frank H. Crossley, two old Irish airs arranged by Herbert Hughes and a new song -by her accompanist, Mr. Harold Craxton, "The Home Flag." Mr. W. H. Squire will play the 'cello solos ".Traumerei " (Schumann), a Mazurka by Chopin, "Le Cygne" (Saint-Saens), and "Valse Apache" (A. Van Biene). Small Union Jacks and autographs of the vocalist will be sold at the Warrington concert for the benefit of the Clara Butt fund for relieving distress, by the ladies of the choir. A record house at Warring- ton is assured. Mr. R. H. Pearson, 75, Bridge- street, has charge of the plan and tickets. T.M. the King and Queen have signified their intention of attending Madame Clare Butt's London concert.</p>
        <p>The great diva's tour is proving one long triumphal progress, huge audiences being the order wherever she appears. At Burnley this week over 500 disappointed persons had to be turned away from the doors, whilst at Bolton nearly a thousand more tickets could have been sold did accommodation permit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Ellesmere-street Wesleyan Sunda School.</head>
        <p>Ellesmere-street Wesleyan Sunda School.</p>
        <p>The Sunday school anniversary in connection with the Ellesmere-street Wesleyan Sunday School will be held to-morrow, when the special preacher will be the Rev. W. Fiddian Moulton, of Cliff College, who will also give an organ recital and address at the Bold- street Wesleyan Church on Monday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Poverty in Wards.</head>
        <p>Poverty in Wards.</p>
        <p>One other table is worth mentioning as showing the wards in which primary poverty is greatest in Warrington. They shall be placed best first and worst last. Proportion of households below Ward. poverty line. Latchford Bewsey Whitecross Fairfield St. Austin's Town Hall Orford St. John's</p>
        <p>Howley It would be interesting to know how far this order corresponds with actual poverty, including that arising from thriftlessness. Townsmen will not be surprised to see St. John's and Howley occupying the worst places. These two wards are among the three worst as respects general death-rate and death-rate for tuberculosis. Bewsey is also among'the three w,orst wards for the general infantile and tuberculosis death-rates' and it may surprise many people that the Bewsey Ward stands so well as regards primary poverty: It would seem that the economic position of Bewsey Ward ought to reflect a better record of public health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Opinions Wanted.</head>
        <p>Opinions Wanted.</p>
        <p>It would be useful if opinions could be col- lated from local men in Warrington and the other investigated towns as to the significance of the facts now presented. For example, what is the influence oi London and the rural areas upon the wages of Reading? Does not the low-paid agricultural labourer drag down the standard of pay for the Reading towns- / man? - Similarly, has the proximity of Ireland any such tendency in Warrington? Is trade unionism more strongly organiSed in North- ampton than in Warrington? If so, why?</p>
        <p>no the mixed trades of Warrington under- mine the virility of Warrington, as compared, say, with the cotton towns of Lancashire? Would Warrington be the better for incieased facilities for earning among women? What is the cause of the abnormal number of deaths of children under school age? To what extent are bad houses, low wages, large families, insanitary conditions to blame? And when the facts are known, and some of the causes shrewdly, guessed, which is the way ont of the wood? There are enough questions to keep a sociological society busy for a long time to come. But these things must not be left to the expert and experienced alone. The captains of industry and their lieutenants need to tackle these problems and see how far improvement can be effected locally and nationally, by legislation or by private en- deavour. No town after all stands alone. There are competitors who cannot be left out of consideration, both at home and abroad. But at least a step forward is taken with each fresh recognition that things are not as they ought to.be and are capable of being bettered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>WANTED, AMBULANCE</head>
        <p>WANTED, AMBULANCE</p>
        <p>MEN!</p>
        <p>There is urgent need for ambulance mein in Warrington to take the places of those who have gone ,on duty in the various naval and military hospitals. One of the best ways for serving the country by men who, for v:-Acias reasons, can not enlist, is to take up ambulance work. There are many in Warrington. who have the jrst-aid certificate who have not joined the Brigade; they should do 'so at once. They could be of great use in doing temporary duty at the local hospitals and unloading wounded who will arrive in increasing numbers, accommodation having been made for 2,600 at Winwick Asylum alone. ' Sergt. Baldry, 125, Church-street, will be pleased to hear of anyone holding the certifi- cate willing to join the Brigade or anxious to join a new class for beginners: - The latest class, numbering 30, have just sat for their certificates. Supt. Baldry can be seen at the Police Station Wednesday and Friday evenings. To the Editor of " The Examiner." -</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I, through your columns, urgently appeal for recruits for our Ambulance Divi- sions? The Deputy-Commissioner for this district reports that all the men available for service have been drawn from the various units, and that the immediate usefulness of the St. John Association will cease unless their places are rapidly filled. For this purpose new classes are being formed at Stockton Heath, and other centres; and, in the present cir- cumstances, every effort will be made to qualify candidates in the shortest possible period. Men desirous of such instruction and qualification are requested to give in their names at once. It should be noted that while special calls are made on qualified "first-aid" men in time of war, the instruction and experi- ence gained in an Ambulance Division are equally useful in all walks of life in time of peace.—l ,am, sir, yours, &amp;c., FREDERIC ROSE, Superintendent Stockton Heath Division.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>STAR 'CINEMA ENGAGES FALABA , LEADING VIOLINIST.</head>
        <p>STAR 'CINEMA ENGAGES FALABA , LEADING VIOLINIST.</p>
        <p>The proprietors of the always-popular Star Kinema announce that they have secured for the forthcoming season the services of the leading violinist on the " Falaba " which was torpedoed last week by a German submarine. The usual musical .programme will, of codrse, also continue as usual.</p>
        <p>High-class programmed have been the order this week—during the first half of which premier place in an all-star bill of fare was occupied by "The Vendetta," a wonderful and thrilling filmed version of Marie Corelli's re- nowned masterpiece. To-day's powerful attrac- tions include "The Spoilers"—that famous series of graphic adventures by Rex lAach.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>" Thrills" Next Week.</head>
        <p>" Thrills" Next Week.</p>
        <p>Places of honour ir. next week's respective fine film-menus are assigned to "The Child Killers" and "The Fighting Strain of Old England."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>FX A M R 1</head>
        <p>FX A M R 1</p>
        <p>THE</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Parish Council.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the .Stockton Heath Parish Council, last night, Mr. W. Mclntyre was re-elected. chairman, and Messrs. Whitley, Pearson and Warburton overseers. Mr. Bottomley gave notice of his intention to move at the next meeting that only one Parish Meeting be held during the year. It' will be recalled, that at the last Parish Meet4ng it was recommended that at least two be held during the year.</p>
        <p>Warring ton Dock? The General Purposes Committee reported to the Town Council on Tuesday night that they had given their approval to the following minute submitted to them by the Dock r Sub- committee: "The committee considered the pro- vision of a dock at Warrington, and agreed that it was desirable to ascertain first the views of the Man,chester Ship Canal Company.. It was resolved that Alderman Tinnion and Councillor llalliwell be appointed to interview the Engi- neer of the company."</p>
        <p>To Inquire the Reason Why. The General Purposes Committee reported to the Town Council on Tuesday evening that they had resolved that the following members be appointed a Special Committee "to inquire into the cause of the increase in the education rate, and to consider smile system that will prevent such increase in the future": Aldermen James Evans and Tinnion, and Councillors Archer, Chrimes, Dickenson, Henshall,,joseph, Osborne and Pemberton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Councillors On Duty.</head>
        <p>Councillors On Duty.</p>
        <p>The following members of the Warrington Town Council attended the monthly meeting of that body, which was held in the Council Chamber of the Town Hall on Tuesday even- ing: The Mayor (Alderman P. Peacock), Alder- men Smethurst, Bennett, Tinnion and James Evans, and Councillors Locker, Skelland, Osborne, Firth Lindley, Halliwell, Wilkinson, Wilson, Hawthorn, Gough, Dickenson. Jen- kins, Roberts, Pemberton, Cunnington, Chrimes and Archer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Only Twenty Houses Per Acre.</head>
        <p>Only Twenty Houses Per Acre.</p>
        <p>The Street Improvement Cotnmittee reported to the Town Council that they had considered the matter of the nuinber of houses to be erectedron the land included in the Town Plan- ning Scheme, and had resolved thatAhe number of houses on any one acre shall, not exceed twenty. The committee further considered the, areas to be prescribed in the scheme as free from building restrictions, and also the -matter of the provision of open spaces, playing fields and allotments, and decided to defer, the allocation until after a conference with the Local Government Board.</p>
        <p>War Holds Up Market gate Scheme. To the Town Council on Tuesday night, the Street Improvement Committee reported that at a recent meeting a. letter was read from the Local Government Board with reference to the application of the Town Council for theissuo ef a Provisional Order to enable them to acquire by compulsory purchase, certain lands required for the widening and improvement of Market Gate and pankey-street. The Bohrd stated that they were prepared to grant the Order for certain properties, but excluded others, and further made it a condition that the Order (if granted) should not be proceeded with during the war. It was resolved that under the cir- cumstances the application be withdrawn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Concerning Insured:Conqmptives.</head>
        <p>Concerning Insured:Conqmptives.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Health Committee, so it was reported to the Town Council on Tuesday, a letter was read from the Town Clerk of Huddersfield with reference to the amount pro- vided for the treatment of insured persons under the National ...Insurance ..Act suffering from tuberculosis. It was-resolved that, it appearing that the llioney available for the institutional treatment of insured persons is insufficient to cover the cost thereof, the Town Clerk call the attention of the Treasury, the Local Government Board and the National In- surance CommissioNers to the disproportionate division of the presJnt basis on the Sanatorium Fund ,behreen the treatment of domiciliary and institutional cases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Beds for Tuberculous Patients.</head>
        <p>Beds for Tuberculous Patients.</p>
        <p>Sanatorium accommodation cannot always be provided for tuberculous patients. Many must remain in their own homes, but it is often very advisable that patients should sleep in separate beds. Last year the Guild of Help provided three beds on loan. So useful did the experi- ment prove that five additional beds, complete with bedding, have just beer made available for lending to families which are not able to make provision for. themselves. The scheme is worked in close co-operation with the Medical Officer's Department. Arrangements are made for thorough disinfection from time to time. The expenses have already been nearly met, but .if any care to sharp in this useful work they may communicate with the secretary, Mr. Wilfrid Rowland, at Winmarleigh Chambers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Disappointment An Incentive.</head>
        <p>Disappointment An Incentive.</p>
        <p>There was a good attendance at the usual Pleasant Afternoon in connection with the Wycliffe P.S.A. Brotherhood on Sunday after- noon, when Mr. Deakin, of Northwich, was the speaker. "Gather up the fragments," was the title of his discourse, and in the course of it le urged his hearers not to allow the disappoint- ments of life to cause them to fret, but rather to turn them to advantage, to draw inspiration from them, and to utilise them as incentives to greater effort.—The ,Rev. T. T. James read ex- tracts from a fitunber of letters received from members of the Brotherhood who' are now fight- ing at the Front, and added the wish that they might one and all be restored to their friends safe - and • sound -and victorious.—Messrs. A. Healey and Yearsley also took part in the meet- ing, which Was among the most successful yet held.</p>
        <p>Church Institute Members With The Colours.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the Parish Church Institute it was reported that many of the most active members were now on military service. It was further reported that though the Insti- ture is financially in a sound condition, it was in need of more and keener members to make it really a successful parochial institution. "We cannot expece, nor could we very well desire," says a writer on the Institute in the current issue of the Warrington Parish Church Magazine, to see great numbers of young men making use of the Institute at the present time. But, if the Institute is to justify its existence there must be a much greater appreciation and use of the facilities for social intercourse and retcreation than there has been in recent years." At the annual meeting of the Tennis Club in connection with the Institute—which was also held recently—it was reported that all the male officers and members of the committee for the year 1914, except two, had joined the Local Leather Trade.</p>
        <p>• Writing in the current issue of "The Leather Trades Review," the Warrington correspondent says :—" The course of trade during the week showed little change, and opened up briskly on Easter Tuesday. Inquiry was sustained right through the week, and was stronger than usual for leather for forward delivery. The character of the demand shows some altera- tion, for whilst the call for all classes of leather suitable for Army purposes continues as! urgent as 'ever, an inquiry ,is now coming in increasing bulk for leather for the domestic trade. The advent of spring has 'infused new life into this section. A big quantity of heavy leather is being made, but, nevertheless, con- tractors appear to find the deliveries all too small 'and slow, for there is pressure all round, and a systematic hunt for extra spot lots to increase the total, but there is not much doubt that the supply of. leather coming along is much ,belomothe requirements of the occasion. The crux of the question is the enormous amount of wastage under abnormal conditions, for whereas the life of an Army boot under ordinary circumstances would 'certainly average twelve months, under the severe strain of win- ter trench work the average has been very little over Iwo months, or, to express it in another way, the leather is being used up quicker than it can be produced by the vege- table tannage. .Prices show remarkably little variation: they are generally firm, with indi- cations pointing to a maintenance of the posi- tion."</p>
        <p>M!gle:</p>
        <p>Closed for the Present. The evening reading room at Silver-street Schools has been closed for the present.</p>
        <p>Who Did It?</p>
        <p>The Museum Committee reported to the Town Council on Tuesday That two purses be- longing to members of the staff hail been stolen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Police Officer Promoted.</head>
        <p>Police Officer Promoted.</p>
        <p>The Watch Committee reported to the Town Council on Tuesday evening that P.C. Ander- son had been promoted to the rank of a detective officer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>War Again!</head>
        <p>The War Again!</p>
        <p>The Warrington and DistTict Master Smiths' and National Master Farriers' Association an- nounce that owing to the increased cost a materials, wages, &amp;c., they are compelled to increase by 10 per cent. the prices charged for all classes ,of work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Local Musical Success.</head>
        <p>Local Musical Success.</p>
        <p>At the examination held in Liverpool in con- nection with the London College of Music on April 14th, Reginald T. Tonge, Hood-lane, gained a first class pass in the Primary (Pianoforte) Section; 80 per cent. He is a pupil of Miss Clara Cotton, A.L.C.M.</p>
        <p>Municipal Horses? The Paving and Sewerage Committee re- ported to the 'Town Council at their meeting on Tuesday night that at a committee meeting held on March Ist the Borough Surveyor ke- ported upon the substitution of motors for horses in connection with the Work of his department,. and was requested to submit a further report dealing with the policy of the committee purchasing its own horses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>How Germans Can Be Thrashed.</head>
        <p>How Germans Can Be Thrashed.</p>
        <p>Captain Townroe has repeated his visits to the Royal Court Theatre, the Picturedrome, Picture House and other places of entertain- ments this week and made recruiting speeches iji favour of the new Battalion of the Terri- torials. Sir John French, he said, had told them how the Germans could be thrashed—by men and shells; the more men and shells and the smaller would be our total losses.</p>
        <p>Non-Panel Doctors. The position as regards nen-panel doctors remains unchanged by the new medical certifi- cation rules which -came into force on the Ist January, 1915. Non-panel doctors are not to be supplied with the official medical certificate forms by the Insurance Committee, but ap- proved societies are not entitled to refuse to consider sickness certificates issued by non-panel doctors on their own private forms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Sick Soldiers On Furlough.</head>
        <p>Sick Soldiers On Furlough.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Insurance Commigee learns that the War Office would be responsible for payment for medical treatment in respect of soldiers on furlough, living more than two miles from the Military Hospital (Orford Barracks), or in respect of any soldier, irrespective of place of. residence, who was un- able to travel to the hospital. The Clerk has been instructed to communicate the information to the panel doctors, and also to supply them, on application, with the necessary forms on which to submit their accounts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Christian Endeavour Celebrations.</head>
        <p>Christian Endeavour Celebrations.</p>
        <p>At the Elmwood-avenue Congregational Church on Sunday was celebrated the Chris- tian Endeavour Society anniversary. Very good congregations marked the occasion. The Pastor (the Rev. C. P. Pitt) occupied the pul- pit, ably discoursing upon "For Christ and the Church."—At the evening service the choir rendered the anthem "The Radiant Mon," Mr. W. H. Wallington taking the solo part; Miss Doris Travis gave nicely a selection from "The Messiah." Mr. Reg. Wallington mani- pulated the organ.</p>
        <p>11111 Cliffe Baptist Sunday School Anniversary. To-morrow the Hill Cliffe Baptist Church will celebrate their Sunday school anniversary. The special preacher, morning aid evening„ will be the Rev. A. Harvey, of Widnes. At the evening service the choir will render an anthem, while Mrs. Kenworthy will sing a solo. In the afternoon a young peciPle's service will be held, over which Mr. B. Few will preside. Mr. T. Dale will be the speaker, and Mr. J. Turton will sing a solo. The Boys' Brigade and Girls' Guild will take part in this service. At all the services there will be Special singing by the children.</p>
        <p>Prescription Checking hi War-time. The Pharmaceutical Executive Committee (London) have informed the Warrington Insur- ance Committee that they were so busy with work which they had undertaken for the War Office, that they, were unable to deal with the checking of the prescriptions expeditiously. They therefore suggested that the committee should make other arrangements for the check- ing.—The Clerk submitted letters from various persons and firms who undertake the checking of prescriptions, and eventually it was decided to leave the matter in the hands of the Clerk and the Secretary of the Local Pharmaceutical Committee, to make the necessary arrangements with a suitable checker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Shorthand Writers and "</head>
        <p>Shorthand Writers and "</p>
        <p>At a recent meeting of this association Miss L. Fleming ,gave an address on "Jerome K. Jerome.: The Marl and His Works." The speaker said that it would, no doubt, be 'a welcome change in these troublous times to turn for a few moments to the humorous side of life, and the works of this great writer would appeal to everyone as affording an oppor- tunity to get a glimpse of the really humorous. Several amusing incidents to be met with in the works of Jerome K. Jerome were dealt with in a very, .effeCtive Manner. The address was highly. appreciated by all present. A vote' of thanks to the speaker, proposed by the Presi- dent (Mr. T. W. Durant) and seconded by Miss E. M. Higgins, was carried unanimously. The reading practice for the evening was taken from the writings of Grahame Hood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Bands In The Park "As Usual."</head>
        <p>Bands In The Park "As Usual."</p>
        <p>The minutest of the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee, confirmed by the Town Coun- cil on Tuesday evening, contained the intima- tion that it has been resolved that "the sum of £7O be paid to the Warrington Bands' Associa- tion, is in previous years." It will be remem- bered that when the estimates of the various committees were before the Council a meeting or two back it was pointed out that no money had been provided for bands in the Park, it being suggested that the usual concerts should not be held during the war. Some of the Coun- cil felt, however, to use the words of the Mayor, that if ever there was a time when the people required music to keep up their spirits in their spare time and to distract their minds for an hour or two from the horrors of war it was now, and he accordingly suggested that the matter, be reconsidered, and, in the event of it being found impossible to budget for the bands, his Worship generously offered to defray the cost of them himself. The resolution passed on Tuesday night shows that the committee have now given way to the opinion of the majority of the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Friends and the After-the-War Attitude.</head>
        <p>Friends and the After-the-War Attitude.</p>
        <p>"I say unto you: 'Love your enemies.'" A brief dissertation on this well-known Divine command was delivered at the Friends' Meeting House, Buttermarket-street, on Sunday evening. by Mr. Chas. Rothwell, of MancheSter. ,If, said Mr. Rothwell, the religious life of to-day was. to have any reality the problem which was wrapped up in this command must be faced in a time like the present, when the command seemed so specially hard of observance. As he conceived it, the teaching of Jesus, as laid down in this oft-quoted text, meant that they must make peace possible at the earliest opportunity, for they could not apply Christian principles until peace was possible. Then, when the last shot had been fired and men gathered round the conference table to settle terms, was the time for the application of the grand Christian prin- ciples. When that conference came to sit, it should be borne in mind that all punishment should be remedial and redemptive and not vengeful, and this war contained within it a bigger task than merely punishing the Germans, and that task was the bringing about of a newer and a better Empire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>MILITARY AND COURT CLIQUES. Nlr. Croatleld And The Coming Debacle.</head>
        <p>MILITARY AND COURT CLIQUES. Nlr. Croatleld And The Coming Debacle.</p>
        <p>ANXIETY FOR GREECE.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—M. Venizelo's dignified and statesman- like letter to King Constantine is written in a spirit of lofty patriotism and in language worthy of Greece's noblest traditions.</p>
        <p>To filter but slightly the famous lines written of the greatest of mathematicians and astronomers—to alter these lines but slightly, may we not say:—</p>
        <p>"Greece and her destiny had hid in shades of night, Lo, Venizelos spake, and led to glory and the light."</p>
        <p>It has been said that "a man of ideas may be worth a parliament, an army, nay, a? whole nation without them "; but in Greece, happily, no such antithetic comparison need be drawn. For Greece knows her greatest of statesmen, and pays honour and homage where they are due. The people of Greece owe, and kayo given whole-hearted, loyalty and deep 'devotion to the man whose splendid abilities, courage and genius have been the saving and the making of his country.</p>
        <p>The nation is with him, and knowing that, well may we ask: Will the nation allow his priceless services and incomparable ability to be, thrown away? Has not Europe Suffered enough already through the terrible evil and danger of unchecked autocratic power? All the world knows what it has cost Bulgaria, and what it will cost Germany and Austria- Hungary. There, before many months are past, the Kaiser; acting in concert with the extreme militarists of Prussia, will have brought about a debacle so stupendous as to be without a paeallel in history. Can such -a system, then, be tolerated in Greece, or will the nation rally to the support of her greatest citizen, statesman and leader, and make. it clear to a military and court clique that what is a limited monarchy and constitutional government in name shall be made so in fact?</p>
        <p>All those who love Greece, who glory in her immortal past and hope for her great future. are awaiting with painful anxiety her decision. —Yours, &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>A. H. CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>Music Success.</p>
        <p>Miss Ethel M. Burgess, of Weaverham, a pupil of Mr. W. H. Payton, was successful in obtaining honours in the Senior Division Piano- forte Examination of the Trinity College of Music, London, recently held in Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Esperanto Conference.</head>
        <p>Esperanto Conference.</p>
        <p>The twenty:ninth quarterly conference of the Lancashire and Cheshire Esperantists' Federa- tion, will be held on Saturday, May Ist, at Warrington. The delegates will be officially welcomed at the Town Hall by the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) at 3.15 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Tradesmen and the Markets.</head>
        <p>Tradesmen and the Markets.</p>
        <p>• The Markets Committee reported to the Town Council on Tuesday evening that at a meeting of the committee held on March Ist a letter was read from the Warrington Trades- men's Association with reference to the mak- ing of bylaws for the earlier closing of the markets, but that it was resolved that no action he *aken in the matter at the present time.</p>
        <p>Entertaining Fighters' Another of an enjoyable series of concerts was given to the local soldiers' end sailors' wives in the Friends' ,Meeting House, Buttermarket- street, on Thursday evening, when about fifty attended. Musical selections, which included violin solos, were given by Miss Mary 'Gornall, Miss Reynolds and Mr. Folli. Refreshments were served at a nominal charge. To Engineers and General Craftsmen.</p>
        <p>A Warrington Association of Engineers and General Craftsmen has been formed to bring together those who are interested in engineering, model making and other handicrafts. A work- shop is to be provided, where models may be made or tested, &amp;c. Lectures, discussions, and . exhibitions will form a ,Prominent feature of the association's work. The desire for an association of this description in the town has long been felt, and members .are assured of • ' / highly instructive and practical programme. An exhibition of models is shortly to be held. Interested persons desiring further information , should write to the Secretary, Mr. H. B. Bell, ~ 38, Bewsey-street, Warrington. , • . Quarter of a Century as Town Clerk. . -, At the close- of the monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday evening. the Mayor (Alderman Peacock) said he believed . he would be voicing the feelings of the Council • - in offering hearty congratulations to Mr. J. _. Lyon Whittle on the completion of a quarter of ' • a, century's service as Town Clerk of the borough. Mr. Whittle was appointed on March ,T 25th, 1890, and therefore' the present was the first opportunity he (the Mayor) had had of calling the Council's attention to the interest- -: ing anniversary.—The Town Clerk thanked the ' . Mayor and the Town Council for their good ,f ' wishes. Temperance Queen To Continue Her . "Reign." ' The war having caused the abandonment of the usual May Festival promoted by the local . Temperance bodies it has been decided that' • . the present Temperance Queen (Miss Eva Tanner) shall' continue her "reign" until a - successor is appointed. During her year -of : offiCe Miss Tanner has accomplished much good work for the cause of Temperance and has ' personally obtained the signature's of over a hundred friends and acquaintances to total - abstinence pledge cards. She has accomplished , useful work for the cause in other directions, being gifted with a delightful voice, which she - has used unsparingly on Temperance plat- , 'i forms throughout the town. , ~ . %Armors College Successes.. ~ - The following students .of Wood's College have been successful in securing their short- • hand speed certificates as follows :—Miss Emily Williams, speed 120; Miss Gertrude Hough (120), Miss Constance Ward (100), Miss Eva Long (100), Miss Annie Darlington (100), Miss ; Dorothy Howard (100), Miks Annie Haslem. (90), ' Miss Olive Billington (90), Miss Mabel Isaac (90), Miss Florence Walsh - (90), Miss M. . Meadowcroft (90), Miss Jean Bunten (70), Fred Shelly. (70), Miss Marion Twiss (60), Miss Jean Bunten (0), Miss Marion Twiss (50), Miss Elizabeth F. Billson (50), Miss Fanny Martin ~ (50), Miss Helen Wallington (50), Miss Rose ' Byrne (50), -In addition to certificates the • following students were awarded prizes :----The Misses Eva Long, Annie Haslem, Marion Twiss.- ' E. F. Billson, Rose Byrne. Details of recent suceesses of .pupils in obtaining situations will be found in Wood's College advertisement cn our front page. . ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>LATE MR. LONG'S ULL.</head>
        <p>LATE MR. LONG'S ULL.</p>
        <p>Munificent Bequests For AVorkers</p>
        <p>And The Poor.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Long, J.P., of Thelwall Heys. Thelwall, and Cleabarrow, Windermere, whose death occurred on the 13th Februaly, left estate of the gross value of £99,136 18s. 7d., of which the net personalty is sworn at £75,711 ss. id. The will is dated 9th February, 1906, whereill testator leaves: Sump of .2500, £lO,OOO, and an annuity of £1,500 to his wife, and the use of Cleabar- row, and also .210,000, hoping she will aI)PIY the same for charitable purposes and persons as may be indicated in any memoradne; left by him; also £l,OOO, hoping she wil; use it for the benefit of clerks, workmen and others employed in the business. • To his daughter Marian Shipman, lii4 freehold residence, Thelwall, desiring her if she shall cease to reside there to make it over to the Corporation of Warrington, the open space part to be used as a site for a convalescent home. £250 each to the executors. ZOO each to his sisters. k5O each to nephews and nieces. The residue of his property he leaves to Iti'k daughter for life, and then as she may appointa to her children. On failure of these trusts, an r subject to the life interest of his wife, one JiL is to go as his wife may absolutely appoint, an subject thereto the whole to the Corporation el Warrington for a convalescent hospital for the poor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CAMP.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CAMP.</p>
        <p>Good Work TO Go On in Spite Of</p>
        <p>The War.</p>
        <p>'ENNIES SOUGHT FROM FORMER</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>CAMPERS.</head>
        <p>CAMPERS.</p>
        <p>A tale Of splendid work accomplished ,on behalf of those who are not in a position to help themselves .wa3 told last night at the annual meeting of the subscribers to the Warrington Children's Summer Camp, which was held, under the chairmanship of Mr. Edward Wal- lington, at the Old Academy, Bridge Foot.</p>
        <p>RECORD SPOILED BY WAR. Mr- E. E. Bradshaw, acting hon. secretary, in the annual report, which was presented to the meeting by Councillor Jenkins, the hon. "cretarY, stated that last season was a fairly successi .1 one, the weal-Aer being fine during the•utime the children were in camp. Unfortunately, owing to the war, and the dis- located railway services, the last detachment ()F boYs was prevented from going during the 'fourth week of the school holidays; but later, tbase boys (133 in number) were taken to :Prod- "[lamp and all had a delightful' Satufday after- lloon on the-hills. The number of children who spent an enjoy- able week at the camp were 268 girls and 131 b°3''s, making a total of 402. This is the highest number for three weeks ever taken. In addition - to this, several sick, weakly children were sent t° Prestatyn, and were much benefited by the °Pen-air life and healthy conditions of the eamp. The visitors increased in number, and the im- Provements that had been made since the pre- . 'ions season greatly added to their comfort. Yhe committee are anxious that the people of • .7arrington should realise the many advantages 4; be derived from a holiday at camp. The ---argesf are kept .as low as possible, as the ..-tiect is not to make a, profit out of them, but . cover, working expenses only. The Committee 'record their .appreciation ft!'t the services rendered by Councillor. McKee, • examining and passing the childten;' the -L'irector of Education and his staff tor their -great assistance in selecting the children, .111arshalling and seeing them safely off on their furneY to Prestatyn; Lieutenant-Colonel B. x'airclough, for the use of the Drill Hall. .The whole of the camp buildings have been Painted and are in good repair. T• he following special efforts were made on l.half of the funds, with the results stated !Farrington Bdwling Committee (White -'leather Day), £l5; Old English Fair. £10; •blond 's Employes Trip Committee; £5; Arpley- ttreet School (collecting books), £l2 10s.; Fair- Girls' School (collecting books), £4 is. Id.; • James' School (collecting books), £2 3s. SKL; raining College Chapel (collection), £1 16s. 6d.; 'leathside Boys' School (collecting books), '2l lid.; Smith-street School (collecting books), 6d.; Mr. J. S. Hill, chemist. replenined • n!edicine chest. 1:-Iwia_rig to the increased cost of provisions and the extra expense of maintaining the camp good order, there is a considerable -overdraft the bank, and the committee appealed for lielr subscribers to enable them still further to "tend its usefulness. The report was adopted. , SEASIDE HOLIDAY FOR NEARLY 6,000</p>
        <p>CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>teviewing the work accomplished in connec- t- with the eamp, Mr. Wallington said that "" committee and the general public had good Leason to be satisfied with the 'report. It was a -rtunate thing for the children that the war ri not break out a month earlier, else it would 'ave been impossible for them to have On a/IY of the youngsters to the Camp. n the L. other hand, they must not lose sight of the that if the war .had broken out a month titer than it did, the debit balance would have oeeti considerably; larger than it was-, at , the Present time. He had been looking over past • annual reports, and he found from them that - .tile camp had been in ii tiseemlee between 5,000 eleven or twelve sears, and that during Inlld that 6,000 children had been taken to it for calth- holidays.giving, It must be very gratify- to the workers and the subscribers to know 'aat they had been the means or bringing a ray sunshine into the often rather drab lives of many children—children whose home sur- roundings were, generally speaking, anything but bright and cheerful, and whose prospect was very dismal. Obvious Benefits to the Hapless Youngsters. aTh_e difference in appearances between the !), iches of children who were just going out to s eazuP and th " marked ose who were just coming back a in the extreme; indeed • one could not help but notice the ruddy and healthy glow -on the faces of those who had just returned and mentally contrast it with the pallid counten- ances which 4-1' their holida-ys7ey wore before they set out for Besides providing for the chil- dren, the committee also found accommodation fer convalescents. The, camp was available for theta a from Whitsuntide to the middle of July, theYid frwn the middle of August to the close of season. Whilst they could not provide con- valescents with all the comforts and Con- of the more permanent convalescent holnes, such as were to be found at seaside resorts up and down the country, they could, at any rate, allot them comfortable huts to live in, ._and with plenty of plain, wholesome food and 'in abundance of • health-giving and health - restoring pure air. No special fixed charge was , • Inade to the convalescents, but each case was (I • Nit with on its merits b'y the committee. Children, Convalescents, and the Poorer Folk. The camp also catered for another class of Leople. In a large manufacturing town like vv_arrington there Must be a considerable num- i . 'er of families of very moderate means who L-"vere anxious to obtain rest and change and ' llefit, but who could not afford to go to the • seaside, as could their richer 'neighbours, if it 7ntailed incurring' the cost of the ordinary '(-?,dging-house or boarding-house. The. i Stimmer • P met their needs. By means of t, they arid be provided with huts in which to live at with plain but good and nourishing 'food, • .Iterms that were within the reach of their ,ekets. In addition to the living accommoda- ,pn, there was a recreation room for the chil- • 1., to romp in should the weather be wet, 41° all' that the parents, who elected to take lei? holiday in this way, were required to do l'as to Pay their expenses. No Profits Out Of Holiday-making Workers' Th Families. niace committee were not at all anxious ,to heZe a •lirofit out of such families. It was he'"„ nse they felt that they were engaged in a th,efieent and useful work that they renewed to•;:r anneal to the ladies and gentlemen' of the otli",) to the manufacturing firms and to the agencies that had.-in the past, so gener- fl contributed to the camp funds, .asking tole' to again give their usual subscriptions, noilL if Possible, to even increase them. n quence of the war the expenses, this year, ittZl be considerably increased, for, as they flu:T. the railway companies had declared 'lll-„Ir intention of not issuing cheap tickets. r rotinnittee eve must, therefore, have ir larger the -Ile. or theyffould be compelled to reduce I tile nutnber of -Or ldren that could be sent to tho„ear°, P. In this connection, he (the speaker) tot that an appeal might also be made • "e five, thousand young persons who had • ed the privilege of a week's holiday in of -4.131,a5t• Many of them, in fact the majority wct.'eni, had now left school and were at • ,it„ k, and if each one of them could be induced tY.:contribute only a humble penny per year dtet!t. treasurer would be able to wipe out the ,r.t balance at the bank. Chariire Iron. Treasurer heartily supported the ionsan's suggestion that those who had pre- Inl • asic3ea :enjoyed holidays at the camp should be ,elto," snbscribe the small sum of one Penns'. ,a Sing and ventured to think that there was not • .31.1 e One who would refuse. He added that 'A? credit of the camp was perfectly good in tecestatyn, and that they were able to get the "sarS goods at the lowest possible Prices. CARETAKER WITH THE FORCES. It -takerwas mentioned that Mr. Rogers, the care- after the camp, had joined the colours, and, -won serg at the Front, had returned home Ned, but had now gone back again. Laer cir OFFICERS RE-ELECTED. •'Nvhilsf reenall )resident, was again chosen i• "ero viee-presidents and the cerann'' • _kjounc also re-elected. 7etaty istant W. H. J. .Tenkins was re-elected 3r., illor with Mr. Ernest E. Bradshaw as his ; whilst Mr. J. A. Ransome. was again niiten tri3asurer. „e eMeeting concluded with a vote of thanks 9a Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>TERRITORIAL ROLL OF HONOUR.</head>
        <p>TERRITORIAL ROLL OF HONOUR.</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL W. LESLIE</p>
        <p>desire to make a solicitor of him. Young Leslie, however, preferred a life which per- mitted of more outdoor work, and after, serving for five years in Mr. Steel's office, he joined his father in the furniture business, which the latter carries on.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>LIEUTENANT'S LETTER,</head>
        <p>LIEUTENANT'S LETTER,</p>
        <p>The following letter was received by Mrs. Leslie on Thursday morning Dear Mrs. Leslie,—lt is with great regret that I have to inform you that your son, Wallace Leslie, was killed in action on April 12th. I will forward his personal effects in a few days. Arrangements have been made to bury him in the soldiers' cemetery amongst others who have given their lives in this great struggle. I have no doubt the company officer will write you as soon as he returns from duty. Please convey my deepest sym- pathy to all your family in the loss you have sustained.—Yours faithfully, G. H. Ingram,</p>
        <p>Lieutenant and Q.M. It is interesting to note that Mr. Steel has two of his staff on active service, one a lieutenant in the 563 Cheshires, and the other a clerk in the A.S.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>PRIVATE JAMES SI XS\M ITH He Died " Doing His Bit."</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JAMES SI XS\M ITH He Died " Doing His Bit."</p>
        <p>The relatives of Private James • Sixsmith, of 48, John-street, Warrington, received on Mon- day .afternoon notification of the fact that he was killed in action on April Bth. He belonged to the 4th (Territorial) Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment. A single- man,. 28 years of age, 1 he was the son of the late Mr. James Sixsraith and of Mrs. Sixsmith. prior to join- ing the Territorials in September, he was in the employ of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., working in the wheel de- partment. His fellows there, amongst whom he attained considerable popularity, heard of his death with profound regret. As 4 , Private Sixsmith attended the Heathside School. Tie- was a ,member of the congregation of St. Ann's; Chureh and belonged to the Bible Class connected with the church. For several years Private. Sixsmitli worked at Messrs. Guest Bros.' tannery, , He was associated with the Ancient Orddr of Druids, and. a , keenly</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>PRIVATE J. SIiSMITH.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. SIiSMITH.</p>
        <p>deceased's moither, The later speaks for itself. Lieutenant Dickson, wrote as follows : "I am very sorry to have to tell you that your son, Private J. Sixsmith, was shot at 12.15 p.m. yesterday, and has since, died of wounds. The bullet, which evidently came from a sniper, who was over to our left, hit your son a -few inches above the heart and passed out at the centre of the back. We dressed' his Wounds and made him as com- fortable as we could under the circumstances, but the poor chap never regained conscious- ness and died on the way back to the dress- ing station. I am writing from the trenches in which he was hit. I need hardly say it was a great shock to us. We are all deeply grieved at his loss. He was a splendid fellow in every way. Ever since he joined 'D' Company in Dunfermline he had always played the game.thoroughly, and had never been known to grumble or shirk his wotk. You will no doubt hear from several of his 'pals' out here, who knew him more inti- mately than 1 did, but may I, on behalf of the Company, 'both officers and men, offer you and all his relatives our most sincere sym- pathy?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>CHUM'S LETTER TO MOTHER.</head>
        <p>CHUM'S LETTER TO MOTHER.</p>
        <p>A chum of the late Private Sixsmith wrote the following letter, which was received by Mrs. Sixsmith on Wednesday :</p>
        <p>"Dear Friend,-1 am very sorry to let you know your son, James Sixsmith,, was killed in action on the morning of April Bth. James and I were together in the trenches and had just had dinner when he left to go to the rear of our trench. He had not been out above a minute or so when 1 heard him shout, and he came back to me, holding out his hands for me to catch him. At that moment he went unconscious, the bullet hav- ing penetrated his left side above the heart. Every care was given him, hut it was a hope- less case, and he passed away the same even- ing. He was well-liked by all the company, and all of us have felt his loss just as though he were a brother. I have got his razor and would like it as a keepsake, being his pal.'— Yours sincerely, Private G. WOOD, 16th Platoon, '0 'Company, 4th South Lance- ._</p>
        <p>shires." Private Lunt, of Folly-lane, another of the deceased's trench-mates, has also written home, speaking in the highest terms of the late private.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>THE EXAVIINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 17, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>RUN OVER BY MOTOR-WAGGON. Private Thomas Shovelton In Hospital.</head>
        <p>RUN OVER BY MOTOR-WAGGON. Private Thomas Shovelton In Hospital.</p>
        <p>- Run over by a motor-waggon a week or so ago, Private Thomps Shovelton, of the 4th Battalion, South LAcashire Regiment, is, we are glad to say, making satisfactory progress towards recovery. Private Shovelton has a wife and family of three young children tesiding in Neston-street. He is 36 years of age, and has been in the Army eighteen years. Originally in the 2nd Battalion of the regiment, where he held the rank of Sergeant, he trans- ferred some while ago to the sth Battalion. He was educated at the Hamilton-road school. A younger brother is in the East Lancashire Reginient:</p>
        <p>• Horse Took Fright. Writing to his wife. Private Shovelton describes his accident. lie was engaged on transport duty, when his horse took fright, causing him to be thrown to the ground. One of the wheels of a motor-waggon passed over his prostrate form, and several of his ribs were fractured. Private Shovelton is now in hospital in France.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>"GOT NEAR THE GERMAN LINES."</head>
        <p>"GOT NEAR THE GERMAN LINES."</p>
        <p>. A Hero's Death, Private Martin Rooney, King's Liverpool Regiment, 13, Lower Ship Yard, Warrington, was killed near La Basso on March 10th. Pte. Rooney, who was 35 *ears of age, was employed by Messrs. R. W. Collin, builders. He was formerly in the .Militia, and joined the King's on, the nutbreak of the war. On Saturday his relatives received the follow- ing: lam sorry to have to confirm the rumour that Private Martin Rooney has been killed. He was one of an advance party who took part in an unsuccessftd attack opposite La Hasse on the 10th March. He got so near the German lines that it was quite impossible to recover his body. I am sorry to have to tell you such bad 'news,- hut I can tray say that Private Rooney died like, a hero, doing his duty in very trying circumstances."</p>
        <p>I--T.ETI7-3 HIS SPIRITS ITP—SEND HT X[ A PARCEL. (See Milling's Adrt., Page 1.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>PRIVATE ARTHUR BARKER. Keen Cueist Shot In The Thumb.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE ARTHUR BARKER. Keen Cueist Shot In The Thumb.</p>
        <p>In a recent letter to his parents, Mr. and Mrs. Barker, of 11, Hardy-street, Private Arthur Barker, a Warrington Territorial, says: "I had a bit of an accident on Saturday (March 27th) while in the trenches. I was slightly wounded in the left thumb by a bullet, but I am glad to say the hurt is better now. It was only a bit of a cut !" Private Barker, who is 27 years of age, is a single man, and is attached to A Company. Prior to volunteering for active service he was employed at the Whitecross Works. He is a member of the Junior Conservative Club, Orford-lane, and, a keen eneist, he was captain of the club's second billiard team. He has won several prizes on the green cloth. Ile has figured prominently on the greensward, having played for the Whitecross Football Club. Ile was among the medal winners in one of the competi tions in which the Whitecross Club took part.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>PRIVATE JOHN HENRY RILEY.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN HENRY RILEY.</p>
        <p>Former Footballer Shot In The Bach</p>
        <p>And Chest.</p>
        <p>The name of Private John Henry Riley, of 77. Haydock-street, also figures in the list of War- rington Territorials wounded. The first news that the gallant " Terrier " had been bit was conveyed in a letter to relatives, written by Private John William Riley, who, is the cousin of Private J. H. Riley. According to the official notice, Private Riley was wounded in the back and chest on March 30th. Private Riley, who is 21 years of age, is the son of Mr. James Riley, of 57; Howard-street. He is a member of the D Company of the Bat- talion, and has served over three years in the Territorials. He was formerly employed at Messrs. Rylands Brae.' works, and at one time played for the Rugby football team in connection with the works. He was educfited at the Silver-street school. Private Riley has several relatives in the Army and Navy, including two cousins, who are also serving with him in the Territorials.</p>
        <p>SPECIALITIES ARE ALWAYS WELCOME AT THE FRONT. (See Milling's Advt., Page I.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Chums Hit By Same Bullet.</head>
        <p>Chums Hit By Same Bullet.</p>
        <p>There is a remarkable feature about the wounding of two local Territorials, Private Win. Robinson, of 127, Bostock-street, Warrington, and Private John Broadhurst, of 71, Lovely- lane. Chums at school and ever since, the two</p>
        <p>PRIVATE W. ROBINSON. Wounded in Action.</p>
        <p>young fellows enlisted together, fought side by side, and were both wounded in the knee by the same bullet.</p>
        <p>Private Robinson is the son of Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Robinson, of V:t Bostock-street, who, by the way, have another son in the Army, Gunner Arthur Robinson, R.F.A. Educated at Wycliffe School, Private Robinson entered the employ of Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co., Ltd., remaining there till the time of his enlistment. He joined the Territorials about a year ago. He was a member of the St. Barnabas", Bible Class and of the Young Men's Club, in which connec- tion it is worthy of note 'that he won several' handsome prizes at billiards. The news of the' double casualty came on hand on Monday, when a letter from the two chums, who had written one on each side of the same piece of paper, was received by the parents. Private Robinson's letter read:— We were going into the trenches last night, but just before we got in I was shot right through the leg, blow the knee. " Jerry " (Private Broadhurst) was shot just above the knee, and now we will soon be in hospital. We are going through some pain, but you and Mrs. Broadhurst have no need to worry, as we are getting well looked after and going on all right</p>
        <p>Writing from No. 3 General Hospital, Le Treport, under date April 9th, Private Robin- son says:—</p>
        <p>I was brought into this hospital this morning suffering from a bullet wound right through the leg below the knee. The same bullet hit Jerry above the knee, but they got it out of his leg, and I think we are doing well. I am not with Jerry now, but he came in the same train as I, and I expect he is in the same hospital, as it is a very big one. I expect Jerry will get to England. I don't know whether It shall, but I hope so. It made it worse for Jerry, the bullet stick- ing in his leg, but we have got over the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>PRIVATE LEONARD GOUGH. One Of Three Soldier Brothers.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE LEONARD GOUGH. One Of Three Soldier Brothers.</p>
        <p>Shot in the left ankle, Private Leonard Gough writes home to his parents at 15; Percy- street, Warrington, in a cheerful vein, speaking lightly of his injury, and observing that it is .the fortune of war. He describes No. 3 General Hospital at Le Treport, in which he is a patient, as a very nice place on the top of cliffs, and illustrates the truth of this designation with a picture postcard.</p>
        <p>A Bullet from a Wood.</p>
        <p>Writing under date April Bth, he says:—" I am sorry to have to write to you under the present circumstances, as I am in hospital, wounded. Last night we left our camp for the</p>
        <p>PRIVATE L. GOUGH. Wounded in Action.</p>
        <p>trenches, and three more stretchor bearers' and I went with one of the companies to the trenches. When we got within five hundred yards of our trench, I was hit in the left ankle, just as we were passing a wood. My wound is not serious as the bullet has gone right through; just grazing the bone. I shall soon be all right again. Except for my leg, I feel champion. I am writing you from the emergency ambulance, but I am being sent down to the base, and then will send you my address."</p>
        <p>••• " 4 looto. , . , . , . Ot. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Billet Shelled.</head>
        <p>A Billet Shelled.</p>
        <p>A subsequent letter dated April 9th, reads as follows As you will see by the above address —(No: 3 General Hospital, Le Treport)—l am settled down in hospital. . . . The nurses and all the staff here are very kind indeed, and do all they can for the wounded. In the ward that I am in is Private Robinson, who was wounded the same day as myself. When I was hit, I was taken to The dressing station, and then sent on to the field ambulance. Next morning I was sent down the line, and finally arrived here. Ov Wednesday, when we went into thg trenches, luck seemed against our Battalion, as one of our billets was shelled by the Huns about an hour before we left the trenches. The first shell they sent found its Mark, as one of our boys was killed and another wounded." Private Gough is nineteen years of age. He joined the 4th South Lancashires as a bands- man, but on the outbreak of war went out as a stretcher-bearer. He was employed, prior to the war, at Messrs. Crosfield and Sons' soap works, where he had been since leaving the Heathside School. He was a member of the Yo.ing Men's Bible Class of the Working Men's Mission, and occasionally conducted a class himself at the Sunday school. _</p>
        <p>_ Three Brothers in Arms.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Gough have given two cther sons to the Army. One—Private Albert Gcugh a Reservist in the 2nd South Lancashires—has been missing since an engagement in which he took part on October 21st. The other—Corporal Arthur Gough, of the 4th South Lancashires—is "somewhere in France."</p>
        <p>COMFORTS FOR 'OUR BRAVE LADS. (See Milling's Advt., Page J.)</p>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN BROADHURST.</p>
        <p>Wounded in Action.</p>
        <p>then. We are in a lovely hospital by the sea, and we are getting looked after well. It was on Jerry's birthday that we were hit, so that a postal order and a bullet apiece, if they were added up, meant a good birth- day. Private Robinson, in now in hospital at White- chapel, London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>PRIVATE:. ' urtoADUURST. Bullet Fiw Birtlixlhy Present.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE:. ' urtoADUURST. Bullet Fiw Birtlixlhy Present.</p>
        <p>• Private John, Broadhurst, the son of Mr. and MTS. Jonathan' Broadhurst, of 71, Lovely-lane, Warrington, who was injured in the knee by the same bullet which incapacitated his chum, Private Robinson, sent the following message home on Monday, writing on the other side of the sheet used by Private Robinson, for his intimation to his parents:— Everything is "ring o' roses." I was hit on the night of my birthday, and I have got the bullet—a very nice birthday present, you will say—that wished me many happy returns of the day. What does she mean? The pain seems to be going easier every' minute now, so On't worry. The Ambulance waggon will soon be here to carry us far away from the firing line. Like his comrade and old schoolfellow, Private Broadhurst is a keen billiard player and possesses several trophies won at the club in connection with St. Barnabas' Church, of which he is a member. - He was employed as a painter and decorator prior to going on active service, and, in furtherance of his prospects with the object of attaining to a high; standard in his work, he attended the School of Art; to which he won a scholarship _</p>
        <p>—..-...-- Private Broadhurst's brother, Harry, has joined the King's Royal Rifles, and an uncle of his is in the South Lancashire Regiment. Two cousins, Gunner Thomas Broadhurst and C.S.M. Gasby have lost their lives in the war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>PRIVATE J. P. MITCHELL. "Keen Worker" In The Machine Gun Section.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. P. MITCHELL. "Keen Worker" In The Machine Gun Section.</p>
        <p>Private James Patrick. Mitchell,' of the machine gun section, was wounded on Monday, as stated in the following letters received yes- terday by his mother, 10, Union=street, War- rington:— " April 12th, 1915. •Dear Madam,—l am sorry to inform you that your son, 'Paddy,' was wounded in the shoulder this morning while in the- trenches. He received immediate attention and has made as, comfortable as pos- sible. I am writing this in the trenches, close to your son, and he asks me to tell you the wound is not serious. He is very plucky and</p>
        <p>I hope will soon, recover. He may possibly be sent to England. He was a keen worker in the machine gun section, and wf shall be glad to have him back again.—Yours truly, T. IlroowAY, Machine Gun Section." Another letter, from the. Rev. J. H. Bayn- ham, chaplain, says the wound was through the right shoulder and lung. "He seemed wonderfully bright and cheerful considering. His wound, I believe, is not dangerous, and he tells me he is not in much pain. He sends his love."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>FATHER IN THE ARTILLERY.</head>
        <p>FATHER IN THE ARTILLERY.</p>
        <p>. _ . Private Mitchell, who is 19 years of age, worked in the 'forge for Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co. He joined the Territorials ,on the Outbreak of the war. His father, Martin Mitchell, is now training with the Royal Field Artillery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>PRIVATE ARTIR R DENAI.III). Enlisted In September; Killed In</head>
        <p>PRIVATE ARTIR R DENAI.III). Enlisted In September; Killed In</p>
        <p>PRIVATE EDWARD B. DOONEY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>March.</head>
        <p>March.</p>
        <p>Private Arthur Denmaid, whose homo was in Aiken-street, Warrington, and who enlisted in the King's Royal Rifles in September, was mortally wounded in action on Mar3h 12th, and on Monday afternoon the intimation of his death was received ,by his young widpw, with whom much sympathy is felt.</p>
        <p>Married a Year Ago. Last Christmas. Private Arthur Denmaid was born in 1891, and received his education at Silver-street School, leaving there to take up duties at the Cockhedge Mills, where he was employed till the time of his enlistment in the 2nd Battalion of the King's Royal Rifles in September. A year ago last Christmas he was married at Friars' Green Chapel. At one time he played in the ranks of a local Rugby football club. He left for France seven or eight weeks ago. He received his wound' in the course of a British advance, and died in hospital. He leaves one little child.</p>
        <p>KEEP HIS SPIRITS UP—SEND HIM A PARCEL. (See Milling's Advt., Page 1.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>Hit In The Head, But Going On Well.</head>
        <p>Hit In The Head, But Going On Well.</p>
        <p>-Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Dooney, of 54, Forster- street, have received a letter, dated April sth, r from their eon, Private Edward B. Dooney, of the Warrington Territorials, in which he says that he has been wounded. The letter tells how it happened, as follows "It was on April Ist, about two o'clock in the morning. Our boys had given the Germans plenty of lead, so they thought they would give 1113 a bit bark, and I got hit in the head. I am now lying in Not 12 General Hospital, Rouen: I have been operated on, and the doctor told me that I was going on well." Private Dooney, who is 24 years of age, is a single man, and a member of "C" Com- pany. He joined the Territorials after the , outbreak of war. He was formerly employed as a range-fitter at Messrs. Whyman's Foundry, where he was very popular With his fellow- workmen. He attended St. Benedict's Roman Catholic Church.</p>
        <p>- Private Dooney belongs. to a rare military family. His father Is a National Reservist, and has been drafted to the South of England for sentry duty. Unhappily he met with a, somewhat serious accident on the railway, and is at present in hospital. He served nine years in India and afterwards as a Reservist in the South African War. Mrs. Dooney has two other sons—Philip and Ernest Richardson—in Kitchener's Army, while her son-in-law, Wil- liam Savory, is with the Territorials.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>PRIVATE BENJAMIN HOMER.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE BENJAMIN HOMER.</p>
        <p>Shot In Nose When Entering Trenches At Night.</p>
        <p>Private Benjamin Homer is another of War- rington's South Lancashires who have not been destined to come unscathed through the con- flict, for on Wednesday," April, 7th, he was shot on top of the nose.</p>
        <p>The news first arrived on Monday evening— when the Private's wife (at New Cottages, Orford) received a letter sent by him from the Front.</p>
        <p>He said that he had been' going into the trenches on Wednesday night, when the Ger- mans shelled their party; and he was hit on top of the. nose. He "thought his time had come," and now only, wished he could come home..</p>
        <p>On Wednesday morning another letter reached Orford, from the "16th General Hospital," say-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>PRIVATE B. HOMER.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE B. HOMER.</p>
        <p>jug the nurses were very good, and that his bullet., wound was progressing. He had not been able to write sooner because his eyes had been bandaged. He got plenty to eat and drink, and was well looked after. Two days ago a field card (despatched before the above letters) arrived to say he had been wounded.</p>
        <p>~.,...,,,,i,,,,:4n;R•4i11it:41:' . ~.,47.,,,,-47,:im.fAll!T?:i:Ak:Iiiill ::tl,'-',ii:,34&lt;t!..,..,:i5,,,A7,..„.1.,,,:!,:a'..:,;111,..5-,.,A140‘,,t0 -::::,-..,!:f„,),,,,.yo4p.,.\.,..4,,,,,,,,,..,-.,,..;,L..,:tziiiif,:-„is.::...::t..,,,.Q.J,:-..•,:,:4,k. ..'...•.-.:,--,x -4.4:::*.;,,..,.9...4:*;-:,..:.:.;.:.Z•,,kw,.:—'••.,,H '1;,:;. 11;,...:,„...:*:4.0.''' - :,..:,..:,..-:.:::,::',.::: f-.• • .'1:Hi,,,?:,..:„-iiiiiitiiift',,lft ..,:•.!., k.kVit:::::•,-. •.!ii:::.::‘:..:.'1::'''''''T.,,,,,,,,:,,r . •:.:4V,,,,,liiiilkiii w,..R,...:-.4-A,t-pl.:: •,:-.,..-.---:_•• ~,4-iltp-i!''"'-,•-•-:-.14.0.4.g,04,1,R.it: -'''V .;" i,!..'..,•,!.,---,-.:-._ ..,iii:',:1i:44t0:'.A.':,1':',,,,..-: • '-,.&lt; ..:',•-..,:.,,.:.-..,..::::..;:,..,,-,-,,,:!:.1.•:iti1kip.5,A1:41 ........-ftlii;.s.:lti.,..ti H'•-'''-'':::-'&lt;'';'-::;:;.JN,.''-'V.?.;V.4;tl.ttAitt ~,„....,o;v:,,:*o:,:vg-,y, •• .- 4 '.:.--.--,,,...i,.::::-,:::....,..,-:.:%.ik,...Axp.-:•,-:4A:;", n..:,,,,-t,,;;.gt..,„ ~:....:::....,-.-:,,...::.,..„:„.:,..1„.::.,,..„--::::-..-::::::i,,,,, .•-';'-:%'.;-.f:0...:-.:41'4...,-,• .. .. .. . _..,....,..,..5.. .i.ttla o ~?..• ..„,„.„.. wwo ''''' .. ...: .......„..„.„.,...,..,.. ::,.. ...,...::,, ... ......- , ..., . - „„......„,... ,g-„,... .... ....„.„...... -,:„...- . ,4:t:'t:::-,•::-'.-'-':- - • - *-,-.-'.--,-:-,-:-•••,:'; .„ . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>'HIS STORY.</head>
        <p>'HIS STORY.</p>
        <p>Private Homer is the elder son of Mrs. H. Malkin, 164, Manchester-road, Warrington, and his age is 26. He married in November, 1910, and is in peace times employed at Orford Tan- yard. He belongs to the Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church, and is also an enthusiastic member of the Orford Bowling Club. Educated at Fairfield Day School, he first went to work for a few years at Fletcher, Russell's. • His brother Albert (19), also one of the 4th South Lancashires, is now training in the South.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>LANCE-CORPORAL 11. HEWITT. Narrow Escape In The Trenches.</head>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL 11. HEWITT. Narrow Escape In The Trenches.</p>
        <p>(" Examiner" Interview.)</p>
        <p>One of Warrington's Territorials, Lance-Cpl. Harold Hewitt, of 114, Bewsey-road, War- ‘rington, has come home wounded. Interviewed by our representative yesterday, he told of a narrow escape from death that he,had in the trenches prior to his being wounded. He was looking through a periscope which he had fixed in a chink between two of the sack forming the protecting barrier, when a German bullet struck the instrument and glanced off, just grazing the head of the observer. He was present when• Lieutenant Seymour-Jones was killed. The gallant lieutenant, says Lance-Cpl. Hewitt; was fearless to a fault, and was always running risks. He was taking observation over the top of the trench when he received the fatal bullet in his head. Lance-Cpl. Hewitt speaks in the highest and warmest terms of the deceased lieutenant, who, he says, stated, when the battalion was in training at Tun- bridge Wells that he (Lieutenant Seymour- Jones) would be the first to be shot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>Shot Whilst Going To The Trenches</head>
        <p>Shot Whilst Going To The Trenches</p>
        <p>Lance-Cpl. Hewitt, who has been in the-Terri- torials four years, was shot in the right shoulder whilst going to the trenches. He says that the bullet—which he exhibited tip the "Examiner" man—must have been fired by a sniper, as he and his party were a couple of miles from their own trenches at the time. He is twenty-one years of age, and, prior to the outbreak of war, was engaged as a fitter by Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons, at their soap works. Going out with his regiment—the 4th South Lancashires—he was in the trenches within a very few days of leaving England. As a lad he attended the Heathside School. He is not unknown as a concert singer. His brother, Driver E. Hewitt, ia,in the R.F.A., and a brother-in-law is a motor-scout.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>Kindly Nurses.</head>
        <p>The Kindly Nurses.</p>
        <p>Alluding to his journey home from the scene of battle, Lance-Cpl. Hewitt sags he was struck by the kindness of the nurses on the hospital- boat in which he crossed the Channel. "If you coughed in the night," he observed, "a nurse would bring you a drink of milk or something. They went about all night, nearly 'done up' with fatigue, some of them, attend- ing to the slightest sound from a patient." He tells a rather touching story of a comrade who was at first rejected for service on the ground of physical unfitness. The young fellow cried because of his rejection, and was allowed, on account of his keenness, to remain in the regi- ment. He was one of the first to be shot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>PRIVATE WILLIAM MILSOM. One of Four Gallant Soldier Brothers.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE WILLIAM MILSOM. One of Four Gallant Soldier Brothers.</p>
        <p>Private William Milsom is another of War- rington's Territorials who have bled for their country, having been wounded in action on March 10th, a bullet piercing one of his lungs. He is a son of Mr. and Mrs. C. Milsorn, of 101, Longford-street, and writing to his parents from Adelaide Hospital, Dublin, he says I am doing fine here—going out in a motor car nearly every day!" Private Milsom has been a member of the Territorial Battalion for seven years, and took a keen interest in his civilian-soldiering work.</p>
        <p>SPECIALITIES ARE ALWAYS WELCOME AT THE FRONT. (See Milling's Advt., Page 1.)</p>
        <p>He was formerly employed at Messrs. Eylands' Bros. w'orks, and was at one time a keen tuem- ber of the works harrier? club.</p>
        <p>He has three brothers in the Army. Pritate Charles Henry Milsom is serving with the Territorials, Lance-Corporal James Milsam is with the 3rd South Lancashire Regiment at Great Crosby, and Private George 3iilsom is a member of the Wiltshire Regiment. A brother- in-law is serving with the A.S.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>PRIVATE ALFRED B. SHAW. Officially Notified As Dangerously 111.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE ALFRED B. SHAW. Officially Notified As Dangerously 111.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Stokes, of 13, Lilford-street, received official notification on Tuesday morning that her brother, Private Alfred Bertram Shaw, of .the 4th (Territorial) Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment, was "dangerously ill," he having received gunshot wounds in both knees. He was then lying in hospital at Havre. On Thursday of last week he sent a postcard to his father, Mr. Arthur Shaw, saying that he was wounded and that he was under the care of a good doctor, and was doing all right. Private Shaw, who is a single man, and is only 21 years of age, has been in the Territorials for four years, and is attached to B Company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>Good Marksman.</head>
        <p>A Good Marksman.</p>
        <p>.He is a very good shot, and for a period of twelve ,months he held the recruits' cup for marksmanship. Before the outbreak of war he was in the employ of the Whitecross Company. Private Shaw was educated at the village school at Sankey, where his family then resided. He has two cousins serving in the Territorials. From the official list of casualties suffered by- the 4th South Lancashire Regiment (published in to-day's "Examiner ") it will be seen that Private Shaw was wounded on March 26th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>TOTAL TO DATE.</head>
        <p>TOTAL TO DATE.</p>
        <p>The first official list of Warrington Terri- torial casualties, which appears on Page 10 of this issue, has added several names to those that had already become public and makes the total agree, roughly, with the total we gave last week. The casualties, including the Earlestown Company, and some to hand since the official list was published, stand as follows: Billed Wounded</p>
        <p>'Sick</p>
        <p>Total 96</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>MARRIAGE OF MISS ELSIE LUNT.</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF MISS ELSIE LUNT.</p>
        <p>A great deal of interest was aroused by the wedding, of Mr. Frederick John Mynekyme, son of Mr. and Mrs. A. 3lynekyme, of Aberfeldy, Grappenhall, and Miss Elsie Lunt, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. E. Lunt, of Spring Villa, Pad- gate, which happy event took place on Thurs- day afternoon at Bold-street Wesleyan Church, in the presence of a large congregation. An hour before the time fixed for the commence- ment of the ceremony, several clusters of people had assembled outside the church, and when the bride drove up there was quite a large crowd waiting pa get a glimpse of her as she passed from the carriage to the portals of the church. Both bride and bridegroom are very well known, especially the bride, who is a valued and highly respected member of the. Warrington Musical Society. Although Miss Elsie Lunt never appeared as a principal, she has had some minor parts in the operas the society have pre- sented from time to time, and was a useful member of the chorus.</p>
        <p>Pretty Dresses. The church was well filled. The bride, who was much admired as she passed down the church on the arm of her father, was charm- ingly attired in soft white satin and ninon. She wore an old family veil and orange blossom, a gold wristlet and watch and a pearl brooch. In attendance on her were Miss Lena Duck- worth (her cousin) and Miss Hilda M. Lunt (her sister). They were tastefully dressed in white-embroidered voile, with shell-pink sashes and hats, and wore gold expanding bracelets, the gifts of the bridegroom. Their bouqueta were of pink carnations. Mrs. Lunt, the bridles mother, wore a handsome gown of lined green poplin de sole with ninon to match, the vest and sleeves being of the ,same dainty fabric, outlined with Brussels applique. The bodice was embossed with fine tissue with touches of black. Her hat was of black tagel straw with large Lancer plume. The bridegroom's brother, Mr. G. Mynekyme, officiated as best *man, and Corporal Lunt, R.F.A., the bride's brother, home on special leave for the wedding, was groomsman.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. J. Morton conducted the service, which commenced with the hymn "The Voice that breathed o'er Eden," and concluded with the singing of "God bless our native land." to the tune of the National Anthem. The organist was Mr. P. L.' Richardson, and he played appropriate music.</p>
        <p>BREAKFAST AT MASONIC HALL. At the conclusion of the ceremony, the newly- wedded couple ran the gauntlet of a numerous band of confetti-flingers, and were driven to the Masonic Hall, where the wedding breakfast was served by the Winmarleigh Cafe to 110 guests. The wedding presents, numbering about a hundred, Were on view, and Were much admired.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. J. Morton proposed the health of the bride and bridegroom, and Mr. Fred Short proposed the' health of the bridesmaids.</p>
        <p>Subsequently the newly-married pair deuarted. for Llandudno, where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride's travelling costume was of navy blue cloth, cut in the nowadays popular military style, while she also wore a navy blue chip hat trimmed with pansies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>SILVER WEDDING CELEBRATION.</head>
        <p>SILVER WEDDING CELEBRATION.</p>
        <p>A whist drive and dance took place in the evening to celebrate the great event of the daY and also the silver wedding day of Mr. and Mrs. E. Lunt, which was April 7th. The first part of the evening was devoted to whist, and the four prizes offered by the host and hostess were won by the following: Mr. W. Carter (Horwich) cousin of the bride; Sergeant Hughes (First Life Guards) cousin of the bride- groom; Miss Alice Walker, Padgate; and Mrs. Peake, Causeway-avenue. Mrs. F. Short and Mr. Levi Richardson sang. Dancing then took place till nearly midnight. Mr. F. Short and Mr. Turner Cooper acted as M.C.'s far the whist, and Mr. George Mynekyme was M.C. for the dancing. During the evening Mr. P. L. Richardson pro- posed the toast of the bride's father and mother, and congratulated them upon their silver wedding; and Mr. Peake proposed the health of the bridegroom's father and mother.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>Bread Prices To Go Up Again:</head>
        <p>Bread Prices To Go Up Again:</p>
        <p>The Warrington and District i!aster Bakers' Association and the E.I. Co-operative Society, Ltd., announce that on and after Monday next the prices of bread will be:—Best, 9d. and 41d.; seconds, Bd. and 4d.</p>
        <p>TRULY BRITISH. Hurry, boys, and slip into your khaki, Take your place with others in the fight! For your country do this noble duty; Every shirker sterner makes the plight. Passed is now the time for hesitation; Every man the breach must yet step in, Head and hand and heart for dear Old Eng- land, That the good old Union Jack may win.</p>
        <p>Gold there is in plenty herb in England, Work for all the womenfolk—and pay, If you only will protect their interests in the mighty, hero-making fray. Then he quick, and get into your khaki! Snatch up arms and hurry for their sake! Not a man who cannot bear his burden In the task: it is not yet too late.</p>
        <p>England has not won her fame and glory, When at war, by workers out on strike. Every man must prove himself a hero, Answering the call with all his might. Hurry on, and show up in your khaki! 'Tis your chance at last; stay not a night. Rise! To arms, to arms! For God, King,</p>
        <p>Country, Strike the blow—for Liberty and Right! ELEANOR ADELAIDE BRIDGWATER.. Sylfaen, Stockton Heath:</p>
        <p>COMPORTS FOR OUR BRAVE LADS. (See Milling's Advt., rage 1.)</p>
      </div>
