        <p>.i I „ • ' I? ' '. ?:, .1, , k :' '..•,; "1 ' \ ~i.t ' ' iff'' ,:, l'i l'. 1 i - -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>~_ 411 VISNOmr...o., ' la, 1 )t ref uJr ir w E . S.`": ';01. DISTRICT PIEW -i-. __ _,..,...,_.</head>
        <p>~_ 411 VISNOmr...o., ' la, 1 )t ref uJr ir w E . S.`": ';01. DISTRICT PIEW -i-. __ _,..,...,_.</p>
        <p>LAY MM</p>
        <p>Gymnastics and Fire-LtOtting.--The Lads' Club intend to give a gymmustic entertainment on Wednesday emning in the Drill Hall. The Scouts will coatribute a -fire-lighting parade."</p>
        <p>Hairdresser Enlists.—Hr. Sam Savage, hair- dresser, has this week joined, at Manchester, the South Lancashires. During his absence Mr. Fred A. Morris is conducting his business.</p>
        <p>Education Electiont.—Mr. T. J. Ridgway has been re-elected chairman of the Lymm Admin- istrative Sub-committee for Education, under the Cheshire County Council. Mr. S. W. Gould acts ttS vice-chairman.</p>
        <p>Trio of " Tommies " on Leave.—Privates T. A. Richardson (of the village) and Hewitt- (of Cherry-lani), both of the 20th Service Royal Fusiliers, zna Private R. G. Smith (of Top-lane) have during the week all visited their respec- tive homes on a few days' leave. Privates ,Riehardsok and Hewitt have now returned to their training in Woodcote Park.</p>
        <p>" Tommsi's " Hard Luck.--11r. Harry Daniels, of Boothe Hill—by trade a fustian-cutter— enlisted a few weeks ago in the Royal Field Artillery; and has since been training in Lan- cashire. He has had the misfortune, however, to displace some cartilages in his 'right knee whilst doing Swedish drill, and has thus had to apply for his discharge.</p>
        <p>The Late Lieutenant Gladstone and Lymm.— Lieutenant W. G. C. Gladstone, the Squire of Hawarden, and grandson of "the Grand Old Man," whose death has just taken place at the Front, two years ago, addressed a mass meeting in the Lymm Drill 'Hall in support of Mr. Kay Shuttlewoitli's candidature for the Altrincham Division.</p>
        <p>What of May Queen?—The Lymm May Queen annual general meeting is to be held at Mr. Daniel's Pavilion, Booth's Hill, on Monday evening. The present balance is £3 7s. 2d. In many quarters it is hoped that the festival will be held, as that at Knutsford has been aban- doned, and a large influx of visitors might be expected.</p>
        <p>"Welsh " Lymm Soldier Meets Friends.—Pte. Percy Clare's latc,t word to his mother (Mrs. G. Davies, of Booth's Hill) says that he met Private James Booth (" Smack ") who is with a Welsh Regiment. They found him in a billet in Belgium, and were very glad; he looked very well.—Private Booth writes to his home at Statham saying how pleased he was to meet the Lymm " Terriers " out there.</p>
        <p>School Children's Celebrations.—Great times are looked forward to by the day school children of Lymm and Statham, for on Tuesday after- noon they are to celebrate the dedication of the Parish Church new font and the baptism of the Rector's little son by holding tea parties in their respective schools. At the preceding ser- vice, Archdeacon Maitland Wood (of Bowdon) will dedicate the font, and the children will return to their schools for the parties after the christening.</p>
        <p>"God Hath Spoken."—On this . subject (Hebrews i. 42) Mr. J. W. Wright (a prominent worker in the International Bible Students' Association) delivered to a fair attendance in the Congregational Schoolroom on Monday evening a Bible lecture. This was illustrated by a large chart "showing the harmony of the Scriptures." The instructive discourse, which lasted two hcrttrs, had been arranged for by the Congregational Church Christian Service Band. —Mr. Barrett presided, and the speaker was heartily thanked by Mr. J. 11. Holt.—A collec- tion was taken on behalf of the local Red Cross Hospital. Public Roll of Honour.—The public roll of honour to be prepared under the auspices of the Urban District Council will, of course, be paid for by public subscriptions. Lists on which to enter contributions—however small or otherwise—are now hanging in the Council Offices, the Banks, the Drill Hall and other suitable buildings. A few subscriptions have already been received, and in a very short time a larger number should come to hand. Lymmites have been most generous to the more general war-funds, and their own particular fund can therefore be expected to receive even more splendid support.</p>
        <p>Latest News From The Front.—Private Joseph Clare writes to his mother at Booth's Hill under date April 10th:—" Just enjoying a few days' rest. It's awful to see a place like this that we are in ,now—all in ruins! There is not a building that has not been hit, and some of them the finest buildings I ever saw! Well, we shall be in the trenches again for four days by the time you get this. I hope we have a bit better luck and all come out safe again. It makes us say our prayers when shrapnel is bursting over our heads. No one can realise what it is unless they see it. We don't see any of the men who get wounded, for they are taken away at night in ambulance cars, and they do not know themselves where they are going until they get there. .. . We have dropped on a few more Lymm lads in this place —F. Kay's brother, Tom, and also Tom Wibberley. Remember me to all." I n Large, but Ruined, Town.—Private Stanley Gibbs, acting at the Front as a range-finder, writes to his wife at Henry-street (word received on Tuesday) : "What a big town we are in! It is a shame to see the ruins of such nice build- ings. We have not been in the trenches for a week, but I think we go in on Sunday for four days. The weather seems to be 'taking up' a little now. . . . There are lots of Scotchmen in this part. . . . We met Tom Kay out here. He is in the Red Cross in the 2nd Lon- dons. I told you that Alex. Price was a pris- oner in Germany. The boys are well." Writ- ing just before, the Private remarked: "We are now in a very large toivn, almost as large as Manchester. It is a shame to see all the beauti- ful buildings destroyed, and bullet holes all over the doors and walls. -The postcards you sent are very nice, but do not sent any more, as they make me think of home—then I get a little downhearted. . . . We are having some good times at present. . . . I am feeling Al, and hope to be with you before long. . . ."</p>
        <p>•Farewell to the Cup !—The Lymm Wednes- day Football Team (one of the three teams remaining in the Manchester Wednesday League Cup Competition) on Wednesday after- noon paid the Stockport Club a visit. After a keen contest they were defeated. Lymm won the toss, and started with the wind and sun in their favour. In a short time H. Holt scored a splendid goal for them, the ball being " carried " down right from the half-way line in great style. Ten minutes afterwards Stock- port equalised; and again, in a few minutes, were awarded a penalty. This was cleverly Stopped, however, by the Lymm goalkeeper (H. Reed). No further scoring occurred before half-time. In the second half Stockport's improved form was at tnce evident, and—despite the Lymm men's stubborn defence and peri- odical fine forward rushes—they succeeded in adding four more goals to their score. Lymm, however, were handicapped by only having ten men to combat exceptionally sound opponents. —Next week the Lymm team hope to play Manchester Post Office, and may yet prove the " runners-up " in the Cup Competition. Even if they do, however, the usual mectitls are not to be given—owing to the war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>MOTOR SMASH. Landau!cite Crashes Into A Shop</head>
        <p>A MOTOR SMASH. Landau!cite Crashes Into A Shop</p>
        <p>Window'.</p>
        <p>Yesterday morning a disastrous motor acci- dent took place at Broomedge. It appears that a landaulette, belonging to Messrs. Pearson and Knowles, of Warrington, was proceeding along the road, when Councillor G. H. Parker ap- proached On his motor cycle. A collision was avoided, but the landaulette crashed into the shop window of Messrs. Holt Bros.' garage, which stands opposite "The Thresher." Hap- pily nobody was hurt, hut the window was smashed into fragments, and the damage to the car, shop, &amp;c., is estimated at .t,50. At the tinle ,—ten o'clock—the shop was unoccupied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>LYMM TERRITORIALS.</head>
        <p>LYMM TERRITORIALS.</p>
        <p>No Further Casualties.</p>
        <p>No further casualties appear to have been experienced amongst the Lymm "Terriers" at the Front this week—the total thus remain- ing at six (one killed, three wounded, .and two accidentally hurt). Further particulars have, however, come to hand about those whoie names have already appeared on the Roll .of Honour.</p>
        <p>On Thursday night the " Terriers " came out of the trenches after another four days' service in them. They go in again on Monday night. Certain unpatriotic and misaieyous indi- viduals—at present unknown—continue to circulate utterly false rumours concerning the fate of the men at the Front. The remarks we passed last week on this subject must now be repeated no less strongly, -and an appeal made to everyone to refrain from originating or spreading reports of the truth of which they have no proof—before their 'identities are discovered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>WORST DAYS EVER EXPERIENCED</head>
        <p>WORST DAYS EVER EXPERIENCED</p>
        <p>Some idea of what it was like in the trenches when the last three casualties took place is ob- tained from a letter from Private S. Gibbs (de- layed in transit). Writing on April 2nd he says:—</p>
        <p>"We get back to our rest on Sunday night. The four days this time are the worst we ever experienced; the Germans shelled the trenches almost the whole day. Peter Leigh got killed, and five men wounded—including Mr. Linley, who was slightly wounded across the back. One poor chap got a piece blown out of his leg; 'l'm afraid he will have to lose his leg. He is a married man, about 25, with one child. A sight like it I never want to see again.</p>
        <p>"To-day is beautiful—just -like midsummer. LAst Sunday morning I went to church for Holy Communion; I am only sorry that I cannot attend to-day (Good Friday). We shall also be in the trenches or supports for Easter Sunday. Most of our men offered a word of prayer to Qod this morning, and some of them are now read- ing the New Testament—which I am very pleased to see. Times like this make a man realise what interest our Saviour is to him. . . .</p>
        <p>The day we left our chaplain gave .us an address, and I never heard so comforting a One before; it fetched tears into the eyes of most men.</p>
        <p>Shelling Resumed.</p>
        <p>"We expected a quiet day to-day, but the brutes have started shelling again this morning. Yesterday our gunners fetched down a tree on the Germans in the trench; I bet they got a surprise. The aeroplanes are flying about now; we use them for observation. It is very inter- esting, you know. We shall be able to see the dawn of Easter morn. . . The fellows who like may receive our Easter sacrament on Mon- day morning at seven o'clock at the R.A.M.C. Hospital. I know most of us will go."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>Teuton Trickery?</head>
        <p>Teuton Trickery?</p>
        <p>Two days later Private Gibbs continued : "Easter morn. We have had two very good days, considering. Richard Shaw got wounded in the leg, but it is not very bad, and another chap out of our Battalion got shotright through the leg, poor chap. To-day theiGermans are on top of the trenches having all sorts of sport, but our C.O. has ordered us not to get out, as they play all sorts of tricks. Perhaps to-night there will be murder done! . . . To-night we go to our little village for four days' rest; then I think we are moving to another placcir . . . We have been paid. Each man has drawn 25 francs, and we can get a few things when we have our rest. I saw some of the Scotchmen last night; they were going up to the trenches. . . We think we are fighting some of the men who were in the Midland Hotel, because some of us heard them say so; we can shout to each other. . . . We are only to write twice a week."</p>
        <p>Captain G. W. N. Hartley passed this letter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>PRIVATE PETER LEIGH</head>
        <p>PRIVATE PETER LEIGH</p>
        <p>Lies In Hero's Grave In Beautiful</p>
        <p>Belgian Village.</p>
        <p>A Lymm Territorial writes: "Sunday, 4th April, 1915. Dear Mr. and Mrs. Leigh,—l am writing to express my' great sympathy with you in the death of your son, Private P. Leigh. . . . A shell burst over our trench and your son was almost instantly killed. He was buried last night in a hero's grave 'within a mile of.where he met his death, and a simple wooden cross marks his last resting-place in the beautiful Belgian village where we now PP are. . . .</p>
        <p>Private R. Shaw (himself wounded) writes to his mother: "I suppose you have heard about P. Leigh being killed. Wasn't it sad! There were five more fellows wounded in the same trench, but I suppose you would not know them. . . ."</p>
        <p>Private J. Guest rites:"R! "I ex-pea you know about P. Leigh being killed. It has made us all a bit downhearted."</p>
        <p>Private Percy Clare sends word that the " Terriers " were all sitting round in their trench, when a shell burst, wounding five and killing one—Peter Leigh, "who was Shot in the head, poor chap !" Of the wounded, only one belonged to, ,Lymm—Syd. Linley. "I shall never forget it," adds the writer. "I was glad when. it stopped, for it was like hell on earth with the shapnel."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>LANCE-CORPOII U. S. LINLEV.</head>
        <p>LANCE-CORPOII U. S. LINLEV.</p>
        <p>Experiences In Hospital After Gunsho</p>
        <p>Wound.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal S. Linley (full particulars of whose wounding we published last week) writes to his wife at Peppeftstreet from Hinton St. George, near Crewkerne, Somerset. "You will see," he says" that I have had another move, so it shows I am mending. This is a libuse—or rather, part of a house—fitted up as a hospital like the one at Lymm ; and the ladies from round about are acting as nurses. We are in a part of the house that has not been used for over fifty years—nail just lately. It is far larger than Beechwood.' • "-Whea we arrived at the station cars met us and took us to a gentleman's house for some tea. . . . Then another twenty minutes' ride in the cars brought us to our destination. Nine 'of us went from Bristol, and we found four more there, so there is a family of kirteen— all nice fellows! . . . A good many seem to think that by July or August we shall have peace. Let }is hope so! . . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>Plenty of Good! Things.</head>
        <p>Plenty of Good! Things.</p>
        <p>"We have Plenty of good things—cakes, eggs, and other good food, as well as 'fags' and tobacco. So you See we are all right. "low thankful we ought to be to our Father</p>
        <p>for sparing us all! . : . I believe I have a greater 'faith now than ever I had. . . . Keep your heart up, and a smile on your fa,ee; that is the way to help others. . . . Tell Mrs. Hadden that Ernest has a 'bobby's job.' He never goes to the trenches, as he is the officer's servant. . . . I think the big ad- vance will have begun before the other Lyram 'Terriers' get out there. . •'' Before receiving the above letter, Mrs. Linley on Wednesday morning got an official intima- tion that her husband was in the 2nd Southern General Hospital Bristol, suffering from a slight gunshot wound. Private S. Gibbs writes: "We are all very disappointed that Mr. Linley is not with us, as we feel like offering a few. prayers. . .. But I have asked a very dear friend of mine to take his place, and he is going to oblige us all."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>EXAMINER, SA!iUItDAY,T APRIL 17, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SA!iUItDAY,T APRIL 17, 1915.</p>
        <p>services on Sundays, and to Mr. W. Cooke for , splendid work as organist. Messrs. F. E. Evans and W. AL Beckett were re-elected churchwardens, and the sidesmeit, Messrs. T. F. W. Dixon, J. G. Lister-Farrar, Ward Eartley, R:-Ileathcote; W. G,,Lecomber, J. T. Milnes, R. Oliver and Pearson. Mr. Robert Oliver cordially thanked the Rector for his able chairmanship; and con- gratulated him, on behalf of the vestry, on the birth of a son.—ln replying, Mr. Thurston ' referred to the present time—an anxious one for everybody. With others, however, he hoped that a satisfactory conclusion would in time be arrived at. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>RED CROSS ACCOMMODATION</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>PRIVATE RICIIARD SHAW.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE RICIIARD SHAW.</p>
        <p>Why Wounded Hero Pities Those Not Wounded_</p>
        <p>Mrs. 3. Shaw., of Booth's Hill, received on Saturday ilight a letter from her son, Private Richard Shaw., in which he told how her how he was wounded.</p>
        <p>On Saturday night [a !fortnight ago] Harry [Brooks] and I were carrying a wounded pal fram the trenches, and as we got about one mile away we put him down for a rest. As we put him down a stray bullet came and hit me in the leg. The bullet caught me just above the ankle, and it must have gone in the calf of my leg. I was put under X-rays yesterday, so I suppose they will be getting it out in a day 0r50... .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>Lovely Place.</head>
        <p>A Lovely Place.</p>
        <p>"Well, I would like to tell you about the nice hospital we are in No. 4 General Hospital, British Expeditionary Force, France), and I should like to tell you where we are, but we are not allowed to. The people round here are very kind. A lady came round yesterday and gave •us views of the place, and asked me where•l was wounded, &amp;c. _ _ _</p>
        <p>• "It is a luvely place round here, and my bed is just against the window; I can see for miles round. It is beautiful in bed, hearing the birds sing, and I often think about the poor chaps in thelrenches. . . . Don't upset yourself about me, 'because I shall soon be all right again. Remember me to all at home." Private Henry Brookes wrote on the 4th : "Dear. Mrs. Shaw,—Just a line at Richard's reeluest to say that he has been very slightly, wounded in the leg. You need not worry, be- cause he is doing splendid. I was with him at the time, and I am glad I was. He may be in England by the time you receive this card, and will be at home in the course of a week or two. We were carrying a wounded man on a stretcher at the time it occurred. . . . I must say I feel lost and lonely without Richard, as we have bden together since we left home. But I wish him the best of luck, and hope we will soon be together agakn. . . ."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>"TERRIERS" TWO MOVES</head>
        <p>"TERRIERS" TWO MOVES</p>
        <p>Do Not Take Them Beyond Sound 0</p>
        <p>The Guns.</p>
        <p>Private Jack Guest writes to his home, off Bridgewater-street: "We have had another move—they say to a quieter spot. I hope it is; it was pretty warm where we have come from, but I pulled through all right. Easter Sunday was not altogether forgotten; I had the usual egg. and we had a service. We have one ,every week, and all look forward to it.</p>
        <p>All He Wants! "We are in wooden huts now. Ours is all right, save that it is a bit packed. . . ." Writing on Thursday of last week, the Private said: "We have had another move since I sent the last letter. . . . I am going on champion, in the very best of health. Oh! we have got a bedstead to lie on now—but no bed. However, I hope to put that all right with a few bags underneath, and then the blanket and the over- coat on top. We are quite warm enough. I only get up at nine o'clock. All that lam wanting now is the letter you've sent and the war to finish. We can hear the rumble of the guns from here. . . ."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>Rumour-Dispelling Letter.</head>
        <p>Rumour-Dispelling Letter.</p>
        <p>On Sunday he wrote: "We are having fine weather here at present. I only hope it will last, and !then the war will be over that much sooner. We had the usual church service this morning; I am glad to say there are a lot go." This letter reached his home only two days ago, and will serve effectively to dispel th 3 rumour that Private Guest had been killed some week or more ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>SUNSHINE AFTER RAIN.</head>
        <p>SUNSHINE AFTER RAIN.</p>
        <p>Writing to a friend during his last rest from the firing-line, Private Guest says :—" I don't think it will be very long now before we go in the trenches again. I hope we come out safe. We have had enough casualties just lately. We can hear the rumble of the guns from where we are n6O, and hear the shells burst close at hand. One has just gone over now. We have had another move. I only hope it's one for the better, for the. last place was pretty hot, . . ." In a later letter the private says :—" I am writing. this in the trenches. . . . It is a glorious day.; the sun is very warm. It is very quiet here to-day—up. to now (9.30); but it's early yet. Yesterday they were awful with their shells, just dropping them in front and just behind. But, thank God, they didn't hit us. I am not in the same trench as all the Lymm lads. Perhaps it is a good job for me, because in their trench Six were hit with shrapnel. Two were Lymm chaps. . . . I only hope those that are wounded will get well and strong again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>" Fish For Dinner."</head>
        <p>" Fish For Dinner."</p>
        <p>"It being Good Friday, we are hoping to all have fish for dinner (all cod !). I have just had a trench-luxury—a good wash; there hap- pens to be a spring just at the back of the trench. We very often.havei to go without a wash or a shave for the wholb four days. You should see us then; we don't half look rum beggars. . . ." On Tuesday, April 6th, Private Guest wrote: "We have got into huts now. It's all right, save for us being a bit packed. We are having a very fine day to-day, but yesterday it was raining all day—so there is a lot of mud abut. Aeroplanes are going over all morning, but we are well away from the firing-line. I don't know when we go in again. Some say not for a few days yet; but there is no telling. . . ."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>AI0111) HUAI THE SOUTH. More Lymm Lads Training—ln Fine</head>
        <p>AI0111) HUAI THE SOUTH. More Lymm Lads Training—ln Fine</p>
        <p>Weather.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Stanley Hinde, who is at, present training with the Cheshires in the South, sends a letter to Mrs. Hadden, of Pepper- street, saying: "We have plenty to occupy our minds. .. . We got some bad news yesterday. A shell burst and killed ten and wounded forty out of our Battalion—mostly out of " B " Com- pany, from Chester. Ido not know if there are any Lymm lads among them. . . . We are only here for a day or two to do some firing, and then we go back to . We are having grand weather at present. .. . Don't worry about us. We will come back again after we have done our little bit."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>PARISH EASTER VESTRY.</head>
        <p>PARISH EASTER VESTRY.</p>
        <p>New Financial Scheme Highly Satisfactory.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening at the Lymm Parish Church, the Rector (Rev. G. B. Thurston) pre- sided over the Easter vestry. No contentious matter marked the evening. Mr. A. Watkin wrote regretting his inability to attend.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. E. Evans (Rector's warden) presented the accounts. The year as started with a balance of .257 13s. Bd., and general offertories totalled .2192 18s. id., special offertories realis- ing .2308 Bs. 3d. (which included £6O 19s. 7d. for the National Relief Fund, and .221 for the British Red Cross Society). new scheme of finance had been adopted last year, parishioners being asked to subscribe to a central church fund. This had proved a step in the right direction; the accounts of the additional clergy, choir, New- field Mission, Sunday school, and sewing meet- ings being automatically balanced from that fund. A balance of .257 13s. Bd. was now car- ried forward. (Applause.) •</p>
        <p>s Mr. Evans called attention to the fact that the last vestry had decided that the wardens should have the church painted inside and out. Tenders were invited, but unexpected and im- mediate expense with the heating Apparatus reached £lB. Then came the war, and so the painting of the inside fabric had been left over for the 'present. (Hear, hear.) The accounts were passed, on the motion of Messrs. John Coxon and A. W. Callender. Officers Re-elected.</p>
        <p>Hearty votes of thanks were passed to Mrs. Callender and other ladies for the year's deco- rations, to the Rev. H. Gray for his valuable</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>INCREASED'.</head>
        <p>INCREASED'.</p>
        <p>Much-Discussed Move Takes Place</p>
        <p>At Last.</p>
        <p>The much-discussed question of increasing the accommodation at "Brookfield," the Lymm lEd Cross Hospital, has at last been decided by the committee. It is to have two wards added, each ward containing three bed, and th be respectively superintended by Drs. Burrowes and De Jong. These wards are now being fitted up and will be opened as soon as possible. The matron (Sister Colburn) will then be responsible for no less than twenty-five recuperating " Tommies." Yesterday Sergeant Prentice and Privates Bass and Gilley left on furlough for their respective homes, and there are now six patients in the hospital. Another batch may arrive any day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>BELGIAN'S GRATITUDE To "Beechwood" Committee For Kim Hospitality.</head>
        <p>BELGIAN'S GRATITUDE To "Beechwood" Committee For Kim Hospitality.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Watkin, chairman of the Lymm Belgian Refugees' Committee, has teceived the following gratifying letter : " Beechwood,' Lymm, Cheshire, 9/4/1915,— Dear Sir,—Leaving Beechwood ' to-morrow morning for London, where I expect to stay a short time before starting for Calais. I owe a duty and that is to thank you as Chairman of the committee for the kind and sympathetic hospitality which I received here, and for the training which enables me to join the motor- transport of the Belgian Army. I trust that Belgian people will never forget English good- ness in this awful time of war in our home country. _ .</p>
        <p>"I ;Irish your business will be still always all right, good health, and to keep the best of spirits. I shall be very pleased to remember you and let you know news of the battlefield. I beg you .to be my interpreter to the whole committee.—With thankfulness and kind re- gards, believe me, very sincerely yours, 'Louis Vandewalle."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>PERSISTENT THIEF SHOT</head>
        <p>PERSISTENT THIEF SHOT</p>
        <p>In Lymm Meadows In Early Morning</p>
        <p>Mr. Levi Thomason, of Rush Green, has laid the inhabitants of all that district under a great debt of gratitude by shooting a fox--which during the whole winter has been paying havoc with the fowl. Whilst taking his usual early morning walk on Tuesday, Mr. Thomason noticed Reynard in the. fields. Having with him his gun, he shot the 'evil-doer without hesita- tion.</p>
        <p>The fox's last theft had taken place only a few mornings before—when he broke into Mr. W. Winstanley's dairy-farm, and took two fowls. This sort of thing had been going on amongst the local fowl-roosts for several months, one of the greatest sufferers being Mr. C. E. Ardern--who is now having the animal stuffed and mounted. •</p>
        <p>- Curiously enough, the sly animal was only seen on three occasions—just bkore Christmas, by a man going to work past Dane Bank in the dark early morning; three days ago by Mr. Winstanley's teamsman, when it walked across the field he was working in; and, as stated, on Tuesday morning, when it was shot. It is thought that another fox—companion of the first—is in the neighbourhood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>OUGHTRINGTON.</head>
        <p>OUGHTRINGTON.</p>
        <p>CHURCH AND THE WAR. Generosity To War Funds Does No Affect Prosperity.</p>
        <p>Generous contributions to all branches of the war, and a highly satisfactory year's work, were reported at the St. Peter's Church, Oughtring- ton, vestry meeting—held in the upper vestry on Wednesday evening' under the presidency of the Vicar (Rev. G. S. Tudor-Evans).</p>
        <p>The churchwardens submitted the accounts. The year was commenced with £43 6s. 11d, in hand, and the collections for church expenses realised £l5O ss. Income totalled £374 13s. 7d., and a balance remained of £ll 16s. 9d. The total collections had been c224o—an increase of £35 over those of the previous year. To special objects the following contributions had been made: National Relief Fund, .216 4s. 10d.; I3rit- ish Red Cross and St. John Ambulances, £l2 10s, 7d.; cassock fund, £5 12s, 4d.; sick and needy fund, £lO 15s. 5d.; foreign missions (S.P.G), 14s. id.; foreign missions (1.T.M.C.A.), .22 Bs. 7d.; Diocesan House of Mercy, 18s. Bd. • diocesan ordination fund, £2 13s. 9d. The church de- sired to make an Easter offering to their Rector of £24 Bs. 9d.</p>
        <p>In acknowledging gratefully the generous gift, the Rector said he accepted it not only as a token of the good-will of his congregation, but also in recognition of the fact that the Rector no longer had a glebe house to reside in. Services Appreciated.</p>
        <p>The Vestry's best thanks were passed to Mr. and Mrs. Ridgway, Mr. and Mrs. 0. G. Smith, and others, for much kindness shown between the change of rectors. The wardens' and. sidesmen's services were also heartily • ap- preciated-. The Rebtor re-appointed Mr. George Telfer as his warden, and Mr. William G. Limb again acts as people's warden. The Rector's sides,. men are Lieut. T. Ridgway (now at the Front), Messrs. S. Davies, J. Mullineaux and E. Craw- ley ; and the people's, Messrs. J. T. Clarke, C. Gleave, R. Priestner and J. Travis. Messrs. W. G. Limb and 0. G. Smith were appointed lay representatives. Cordial votes of thanks were passed to the choir, district visitors, Sunday school teachers, church decorators, and, all other workers; also to Mrs. Walter Jones for generous support of the church and Sunday schools at Higher-lane and Rush Green.</p>
        <p>Important Resolutions.</p>
        <p>Expert advice is to he obtained for the im provement of the church heating apparatus and a rota of sidesmen on duty in the aura arranged.</p>
        <p>117 Oughtrington Soldiers.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Ridgway and Priestlier having thanked the Chairman for presiding, The Rev. Tudor-Evans returned his best thanks, and referred to the parish's noble part in the war: The names of 117 Oughtrington soldiers were to be found on the church door, and to the war funds the congregation had contributed £64—.04 of the sum being raised by two social gatherings. He wished to acknow- ledge several Easter gifts to the church, includ- ing a beautiful oak bookshelf provided by the self-denying efforts of the children. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>STOP THAT RHEUMATISM.</head>
        <p>STOP THAT RHEUMATISM.</p>
        <p>"Stop it now, at once! There is no need for you to suffer the pangs and agonies of Rheuma- tism any longer. Every day, every hour almost, that you continue to suffer is self-inflicted tor- ture." This emphatic statement is made by Dr. Geo. E. Flood, whose successful labours to find a !liftable and effective remedy for Rheu- matism, and .not 'merely a means of temporary relief, have made his name famous all the world over.</p>
        <p>For the benefit of our readers, we append the prescription which he recommends. It is : Syrup of Ginger 5 oz. Liquid Barkola Compound Syrup of Rhubarb Tiactureof Gentian</p>
        <p>Dose: A teaspoonful 'after meals and at bed- time. We have the assurance of the Doctor that this prescription (which can be Adispensed by any chemist) can be taken with (perfect safety by the-most delicate man or woman. TIK first dose usually brings relief, and if persisted in for a very short time will result in a perfect env of the ailment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>During Forbidden Hours.—On Tuesday, March 9th, Charles Mounfield (of Rose Lea, Lower Walton) allowed hi sdog to be at large in London-road during forbidden hours. Result: Fined five shillings at Tuesday's Petty Sessions. The Missing Muzzle.—Peter Knowles, of Hat- ton House, Appleton, was fined half a crown at the Petty Sessions on Tuesday morning because his dog wandered collarless and, un- muzzled at Stockton Heath on Tuesday, March 30th. •</p>
        <p>At the Petty Sessions.—At the Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday morning, there were on the bench: Mr. F. A. Frost (in the chair), Sir Gilbert Greenall, and Messrs. Thomas Baxter, George Dakin, William Fletcher, Linn. Greening, James Hepherd, Roger C. Parr, J. A. Ransom° and J. J. Whitley. *</p>
        <p>Collarless Doggie.—At Tuesday's Petty_Seq-</p>
        <p>ions, Henry Massey, of Little Legh (who did not appear), was fined half a crown because his dog was found wearing no collar at Stretthn on the highway on Friday, March 26th.</p>
        <p>Building Bylaws.—The Parochial Committee were informed on Tuesday that the Building Committee were going through the bylaws, but as it was "rather a long job" they had nothing to report at present. Sewer-Manholes to be Cleaned.—The Public Health Surveyor recommended the Parochial Committee on Tuesday evening to have the parish sewer-manholes cleaned out. It was resolved to follow this advice, the arrangements being left to Mr. Wright. The Chairman re- marked that the manholes should be flushed every so often. Explanations Failed.—Frank Waywell and Harold Harten, respectively of 88 and 86, Folly- lane, Warrington, were summoned to Tuesday's Petty Sessions for having—in Chester-road, Walton Inferior, at half-past ten on the night of Monday, April sth—ridden their bicycles without lighted lamps.—Both pleaded guilty, but gave explanations.—ln each case the fine was 2s. 6d.</p>
        <p>Unrealised Expectations.—Joseph Percival, of Tanyard Farm, Appleton, should have appeared at the Petty Sessions on Tuesday—for having at Stockton Heath on Thursday, March 4th, caused a lurry to be in Grappenhall-road at 7.10 p.m. without a lighted lamp or reflector.— A constable told how he pointed out the danger to Percival, who replied, "I expected to be home before nine."—A fine of half a crown was im- posed. • Quick Work !—At Tuesday's Petty Sessions, Thomas Moffat, of Mosswood Hall Farm, Stret- ton, pleaded not guilty to having had no con- trol over a. horse at 9.35 a.m. on Friday, March 19th.—A constable said that Moffat stopped his horse on the tram-lines in Victoria-square, and left it there. When asked why, he at first denied having done so, and then said it was only whilst he went into a shop.—Defendant said he had only reached the shop door when called back again.—The case was dismissed. Did Their Duty—At the Parochial Committee</p>
        <p>meeting, held on Tuesday evening in the Coun- cil Schools, there were present: Messrs. J. H. Clarke (who occupied the chair in the absence —owing to the illness of his wife—of Mr. G. Pilling), A. Bottomley, J. H. Clarke, J. McDermid, C. W. Hallows, J. Hallows, F. Pear- son, F. Warburton, A. S. Wright (surveyor) and J. A Moorefield (acting clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>WAGGONS OR IMPLEMENTS? An Unusual Case At The Sessions.</head>
        <p>WAGGONS OR IMPLEMENTS? An Unusual Case At The Sessions.</p>
        <p>Walter Fitter, of 36, Hood-lane, Warrington, was summoned at Tuesday's Petty Sessions for having—on the 4th March, at Walton Inferior— failed to have its loaded weight conspicuously and legibly affixed on a waggon drawn by a heavy locomotive., He was further charged with drawing at the same time and place, more than three loaded waggons, without the consent of the Cheshire County Council. Inspector Breeze said that in Chester-road at 11.30 a.m. on March 4th, he stopped Fitter, and failed to find on the ,waggons any wdight stated. He also asked the man if he had permission from the County Council to draw four waggons. The reply was: "I don't know." Defendant declared that they had been dragging these 'same things about the same road for over thirty years, but had never been com- plained to before. He contended that the vehicles in question were not waggons; they included a threshing-machine and so on, and were farmers' implementh. And was there such un implement in the County of Cheshire with the weight on it? In dismissing the ease,</p>
        <p>Mr. F. A. Frost (chairman) told defendant he ought to b,ave applied to the County Coun- cil for permission to use four vehicles. Defendant: But suppose it is not granted? It is necessary in my work for the machines to go along the road. The Chairman told Fitter that if be could not obtain the 'necessary permission, .he would have to conform to the regulations. The Bench could not make the law on the subject.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>HOARDINGS TO BE MOVED By the Manchester Ship Canal Co.—</head>
        <p>HOARDINGS TO BE MOVED By the Manchester Ship Canal Co.—</p>
        <p>Result.</p>
        <p>The result of the Ship Canal Company's decision to remove the hoardings nw standing twenty steps from Stockton Heath Bridge, engaged the attention of the Parochial Com- mittee on Tuesday .evening. The Acting-Clerk (Mr. J. A. Moorefield) read a letter from Messrs. Dunlop, Wallis and Co., the company's agents : Referring' to your letter.' of the 3rd February last, we hope to be able to arrange for the removal of the hoardings at an early date—probably by June next; and would point out, as stated in our letter' of Decem- ber 19th, that after the removal of the hoardings the position will no longer be a suitable one for a public convenience. And we are therefore unable to grant permission for the erection of the convenience referred to in our correspondence with Mr. Ashton. The Chairman (Mr. J. H. Clarke): I think they are quite right. Mr. A. S. Wright (surveyor) : Shall we have to find a new site for the convenience?</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I am not going to enter into that point. The letter has been acknowledged, but I think they deserve our thanks for the action they have taken. It was accordingly resolved that a letter setting forth the committee's high appreciation of the action be forwarded to the company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>SEWERAGE CAPACITY.</head>
        <p>SEWERAGE CAPACITY.</p>
        <p>War Causes Sudden Alteration 0 Committee's Recommendation.</p>
        <p>A reporf upon the capacity of the parish sewerage system was presented to the Parochial. Committee,on Tuesday evening by Mr. F. Pear- son_ (chairman of the Sanitary Committee).</p>
        <p>• Mr. Pearson said his committee had con- sidered in detail the report of Messrs. Diggle and Son upon the question of the sewers receiv- ing the additional S*ewage brought about by the conversion of the remainder of the present Pail-system closets into w.e.14, and had resolved that the following recommendation be made : That this committee, having considered the report of the,engineers and noted that the dimensions are sufficient,, recommend that the Parochial Committee proceed with the conversion of all the existing pail- closets into w.c.'s at as early a date as is convenient.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>No Money-Borrowing.</head>
        <p>No Money-Borrowing.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pearson added that he did- not think his committee would have adopted the latter part if they had known what they knew now. - The Government were not now encouraging the bor- rowing of mon,ey, and he. had been wondering whether his committee would sanction the omission of the latter part of the resolution. The question would inevitably have to be dealt with; but he did not wish the Parochial Com- mittee to take an proceedings until ,the war was over.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Hallows: I think some 'recommenda- tion ought to be passea, but not one of such a character as to tie any future councils. After some discussion, the second portion of the resolution was altered to</p>
        <p>. . . ,but do not ask for any immediate action to be taken, in consequence of the unsettled state of the country. As amended, the resolution as then adopted unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Pretty Wesleyan Wedding.—On Thursday afternoon, .at the Penketh Wesleyan Chapel, a quiet but pretty wedding was solemnised between Mr. George Walker Weaver; elder son of Mr. and Mrs. George Weaver, of Springfield- street, Warrington, and Miss Jennie Johnson, only daughter of Mr. and Mrs. F. Johnson, of Elm Dene, Sankey. The officiating clergymen were the Rev. Benjamin Weaver (of Bradford). uncle of the bridegroom, and the Rev. John Gunnell. The bridesmaids were the Misses Alice Lawton, Marion Wilde and Amy Johnson (cousins of the bride). The best man was Mr. T. B. Weaver (brother of the bridegroom). Mr. Hugh 'Mellor, son of the Rev. W. Mellor, for- merly of Warrington, officiated at the organ. The churqi was beautifully decorated by Mrs. James, Dodds. N'umerous valuable presents have been received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>PARISH COUNCIL'S ANNUAL</head>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL'S ANNUAL</p>
        <p>MEETING. Dr. Murray Elected to the Chair.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Penketh Parish Council was held on Monday evening, when the members present were :—Messrs. J. G. Withinshaw, W. Davies, R. Routledge, and J. Ditchfield, and Dr. Murray; with Mr. E. Foster (clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>Elections and Re-elections.</head>
        <p>Elections and Re-elections.</p>
        <p>It was proposed by Mr. J. G. Withinshaw and seconded by Mr. W. Davies and carried, that Dr: Murray be .chairman for the ensuing year. Mr. W. Davies proposed and Mr. R. Rout- ledge seconded that Mr. J. G. Withinshaw be vice-chairman, and this, too, was carried. Dr. Murray and Mr. J. Ditchfield were re- elected Overseers. It was proposed by Mr. R. Routledge and seconded by Mr. J. Ditchfield and carried that Dr. Murray be re-elected the representative of the Council on the Earles- town Education Committee. Concerning the Gas Pressure. A letter was read from the Warrington Cor- poration Gas Department in reply to a com- plaint that had been forwarded as to the low pressure of gas in the district. The letter stated that the quality and pressure of the gas had been kept well up to the standard requirements. However, the department pro- mised further investigation. '</p>
        <p>Tar-Macadam For Bad Roads Suggested.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. G. Withinshaw suggested that a letter should be sent to the County Council in regard to the terrible state of the main roads in and around the village, and asking if they could be tar-maeadamed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>Footpath Matters.</head>
        <p>Footpath Matters.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Faulkner asked for information about the Ferry footpath, and, Mr. Woodward, reply- ing', stated that it was,still open and pedes- trians used it regularly. Mr. Midwood inquired if footpaths were being paced all along the main roads; and the Chairman answered that he supposed so, but pointed: Out that that was a County Council affair. ' •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>SANKEY.</head>
        <p>SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Whist Drive for Comforts for Fighters.—ln the Sunday Schoolroom on Friday of last week, a highly successful whist drive and dance were held in aid of the St. Mary's Mission Clothing Fund for Soldiers and Sailors, about 130 ladies and gentlemen being present. The M.C.'s for the card games were Messrs. E. Dumbill, W. Lightfoot and H. Davies, and for the dancing Messrs. R. Plumpton, W. Dwerryhouse and C. Herrimann. The whist prizes were won by. the following: Mrs. W. Purness and the Misses F. Fairclough and M. Darlington, and Messrs. E. Houghton, G. Carman and William Furness.</p>
        <p>A Quiet Wedding.—On Saturday, at St. Mary's Church, Sankey, a quiet wedding was solemnised between Mr. Barker Hatton, of West View, Penketh, and Miss Florence Littlewood. The Rev. J. t. Jones officiated. The bride wore a dress of silver grey corded silk, trimmed with Oriehtal silk and lace, and a black hat with feather ruffle. Miss Charlotte Littlewood (sister of the bride) who acted as bridesmaid, also wore grey and a blue hat with a feather ruffle. Mr. Griffiths Hatton, brother of the bridegroom, was the best man, and Mr. Cook- son gave the bride away. A reception was held at West View, and during the afternoon the young couple left for a short holiday, the bride's travelling dress being of Wedgwood blue fine cloth. Many presents were received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>FOR THE WAR FUNDS.</head>
        <p>FOR THE WAR FUNDS.</p>
        <p>Garden Fete Suggested As A Money</p>
        <p>Raiser.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Furness, the chairman of the Parish Council, presided on Tuesday evening over a meeting held in the Schoolroom for the pur- pose of transacting business in connection with the local War Fund.</p>
        <p>The principal question for consideration was how to swell the coffers of the fund and various suggestions were offered by members present. Mr. J. Newbould proposod an open-air what drive and concert. An amendment favoured a cricket match— Ladies versus Gentlemen—the latter being handicapped with broomsticks. Both suggestions were defeated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>Deputation Appointed.</head>
        <p>A Deputation Appointed.</p>
        <p>Mr. Appleton proposed as a further amend- ment, and Mr. Newbould seconded, that a garden fete be held, and. this proposal re- ceived full support and was declared carried. A discussion as to a suitable venue for the fete followed, and the feeling of the meeting was that Mr. A. S. Aitken, of Hall Nook, should be approached and asked to lend his patronage to this movement and help the committee out of their difficulty. A deputation consisting of the Rev. J. R. Jones and Messrs. Appleton and Lowe was ap- pointed to approach Mr. Aitken in the matter.</p>
        <p>Penny Per Head_ Asked__ For In Aid Of Red</p>
        <p>Cross Work</p>
        <p>An appeal , from the St. John Red Cross Society, through Lady Gerrard, was read, ask- ing for assistance.in the matter of the society's house-to-house collection, by means of which</p>
