      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>NEW BOOKS.</head>
        <p>NEW BOOKS.</p>
        <p>The Philosophy of Spirit. By John Snaith. London : Hodder and Stoughton.. 12s. net. The author of this work sets himself a great task, and we do not think he has .discharged it in a manner calculated to -satisfy the class of readers to whom such a book is likely to appeal. The philosophy of spirit, according to Mr. Snaith, is the logical exposition of thought. In his exposition he follows, Hegel, and gives us a statement of the Hegelian doctrine of the Ego. In our view; Mn Snaith seems •imperfectly to appreciate the development of philosophic thought since Hegel's time. His view appears to be that Hegel made a wonderful philosophical discovery, and that further discovery in this line is not to be expected. • A century has passed since Hegel wrote, and an enormous amount of work has been done in this field, both on the Continent and in England, but, apparently, to judge from Mr. Snaffles book, all this work is of no account.- It is disap- pointing, to say, the least, to find in Mr. Snaith so little appreciation of recent philosophical thought. And when Mr. Snaith comes to deal with scientific thought, he displays, in our view, still less appreciation of the •movement of opinion in scientific circles. Let us give an ex- ample -of the attitude which he adopts towards the evolutionary theory : " The most superficial view of .Nature forces home. the conviction of creation and generation in time of new germs of life in the vegetable, animal and human worlds—germs which, as such, were at one time non-existent, just as you and I, in our empirical being, Once did not exist. It is of no use to cry- evolution and to allege that all new germs are evolved from pre- existent germs and cells (by natural Selection); for Ito theory of evolution can logically set aside the necessity,- and reality of creation, since it cannot be doubted that new germs do come into being. Indeed, this idea of a coming to be of what once did not exist in sensible form, in- volves the idea of absolute creation, and this is what the theory of evolution was thought, and even intended; to have Set aside as incredible in the light of advancing science. Properly looked at, evolution is only another name for creation, and thereby, as a theory of existence, it anni- hilates itself and does not avoid the necessity of an intelligent Creator. To suppose that any theory of evOlution can set aside the fact of direct creation is a mere freak of a vain imagination. Evolution creates nothing, yet creation is a self-evident fact of existence and reason, and cannot be successfully ignored by any mode of mere plausible reasoning. The prevalent idea of evolution does ,not mean growth and improvement. The idka of growth has always been familiar, but not the growth of man out of an animal." Mr. Snaffles point of view does not seem to, us to 'differ. materially from that of those who opposed the evolutionary theory when it was put forward by Spencer and Darwin in the middle of last century. The disparaging view which Mr. Snaith holds Of science and its methods very naturally 'leads him into violent collision with the exponents of the Higher Criticism, for his criticism of the evolutionary theory does not incline one to the belief that he will take very kindly to the methods of historic science.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>YOUR HEAD AND STOMACH</head>
        <p>YOUR HEAD AND STOMACH</p>
        <p>, Headache caused by a disturbed digestion is nearly always accompanied by pains in the stomach, flatulence, vomiting and often by con- stipation. This sort of headache is generally located in the forehead, and is not constant but comes and goes. It does not come on immediately after eating but 'after the -food has had tune to ferment, which it does because the digestive fluids that should take' care of it are insufficient, because the glands that secrete these fluids are weak, because the blood is failing to nourish these glands properly. Rich, red blood is the first essential to proper digestion, and the digestive process cannot go on without it. When lack of nourishing blood causes fermentation, and poisons are absorbed from the ,digestive tract,- pains in your head advise you of the fact. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills give the blood just the elements it needs to correct this condition, and, with a laxative when required, they form a perfect treatment for the headaches of indigestion. Your dealer can supply you promptly with Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People; but substitutes and common pink pills will not do; so always ask for Dr. 'Williams'. FREE.—" What to Eat" is the title ~of a useful little book offered free to readers who send a postcard applying for a copy to Offer Dept., 46, Holborn-viaduct, London.</p>
        <p>BLOOD-GUILT. [To the employers of the men who sank the liner Fuluba, and laughed at the cries and struggles of drowning men and women.'</p>
        <p>Not yet you; tale of hideous deeds is told; Against the hour of reckoning still they mount, When He, the Judge, his Great Assize shall hold, And call you to account.</p>
        <p>On these that mocked the drowning lips' appeal, Slaves of their masters' bidding, hand and</p>
        <p>eye, Swift fall the stroke of vengeance, strong to deal The rats' death they shall die!</p>
        <p>But you, who sent them out to do this shame, Ftom whom they take their orders and their hay, For you—avenging wrath defers its claim,: And justice bides her day.</p>
        <p>What talk is this of " honourable peace," . While in your persons no amends be made? There is no way by which this War shall cease Till that account be paid.</p>
        <p>Then, in that (lay of doom, put not your trust In human pity to excuse your debt; 'High Heaven, that saw you pitiless, is just; And God will not forget. . 0.5., in "Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST-</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST-</p>
        <p>continued.</p>
        <p>I have never seen and do not know, to settling a dispute between a man who has a temper and his wife who has another; or smoothing out the tangled ends Of some young man's love affair, because he is too shy to do it himself." He went on to narrate how be was regu- larly called upon to prescribe for and treat the ailments of natives within a Wide radius of his station; but I may leave all that in order to include a little story by a medical mission- ary in another part of Africa. He related in my hearing several years ago that on one occasion, when going to church to hold a ser- vice, he was astonished to find a wonderful array of bottles. In that array brandy and Yorkshire relish were represented, and, said the missionary, the sight of a Yorkshire relish label in that part of the world would be suffi- cient to turn a Yorkshireman homesick, He inquired the meaning of the bottles in the church, and he was informed that the people switi ing from fever looked to the white Mall missionary to help them, Unfortunately he had only a small quantity of (juininr, but this he mixed—about one g-ra in to .eltult pint of water—and the people went home rejoicing. When be asked how they were, the native pastor said, !` Oh, they all got better," Talk about faith healing, eh?</p>
        <p>A Swedenborgian Claim. I was somewhat surprised to hear from an eminent authority that followers of Sweden- borg vere among the earliest adVocates for the abolition of the slave trade and the settle- pent of Christian and civilised communities in Africa. I was unprepared for This, but have had the proof placed at my disposal, and, although I have not room for more matter at present, interested readers who may have doubts about it will be . welcome to the evi- dence on avowing Oen. ocepticion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 17, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 17, 1915.</p>
        <p>BELGIAN SOLDIER VISITS FAMILY.</p>
        <p>A Flying Visit To Warrington And Happy Re-union.</p>
        <p>RELATIVES REUNITED.</p>
        <p>Warrington has just had a visitor from the Belgian Army, in the person of Sergeant-Major Jean ltucquoy, of 'the Belgian Artillery. He made a flying visit from .the Front to see his wife • and relatives now receiving the hospitality of the .Warrington people. His wife, sisters and mother are in the Belgian Home in Go!- borne-street, • maintained by - - the Working Men's Mission, and he has fourteen other relatives staying at Hall Nook, Penketh. The way in which these relatives were col- lected together within a radius of a few miles is worth noting. Mr. Charles E. Parker, who is in constant touch with the Hall Nook Colony, learnt that seven of their relatives were in this country and he at once sought the good offices of the Working Men's Mission, Who were opening the home in Golborne-street, and accommodation for" the seven was at once offered and accepted. Perhaps a greater happiness was provided when these guests were placed in postal com- munication with their relatives serving in the Belgian Army. The family. at Golborne-street were aston- ished, and overjoyed, in answering a knock last Monday, to find standing at the door one who to the residents was at once husband, son and brother. lie had written telling of his coming, but had overrun his letter. . .</p>
        <p>Jr. Jean littequoy, his wife explained to our representative, is in the Belgian Army a Chef d'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>SELECTING GUESTS.</head>
        <p>SELECTING GUESTS.</p>
        <p>Interviewing Belgians At The Refugee Centres.</p>
        <p>OUR DOUBLE DUTY.</p>
        <p>BY CHARLES AND HELEN PARKER</p>
        <p>The allocation of Belgian refugees by the London. committee, especially in the 'early months of the war, was in many cases so un- suitable that great inconvenience and dis- appointment was felt by many local com- mittees. When asked to find guests for some Warrington homes, knowledge of this fact led us to inform the London committee that we desired personally to select these guests. We were pleased to receive a reply to :the effect that the committee would be glad to give us all facilities and help its in making our choice; hence our visit to the Belgian 'camps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>EARL'S COURT.</head>
        <p>EARL'S COURT.</p>
        <p>Many in Warrington have visited the annual exhibitions which for several years have been held here, and will recall the general character of the grounds and buildings. All the beauty of the place has passed away like "the baseless fabric of a dream." A substantial edifice may preserve or attain a beauty of its own, but these make-believe gorgeous palaces, manu- factured Alps, Indian Bazaars, Venetian Canals, Japanese Gardens, and all the rest, look pictures of ugliness in their dilapidation Only the buildings that could be utilised for their present purpose •have been preserved in fair condition. Where thousands gathered to witness the entertain- ments in the Stadium or refreshed themselves in the restaurants with tea or champagne according to taste or length of purse, or where the elite made merry in the select " Welcome Club," in these places now tired women • trail their babies and listlessly sit and wait weary hours for some kind English hearts to provide them with some place they might look upon as a home. The Dien, many half dazed by the happenings which have come like an earth- quake into tkeir surroundings, sit disconsolate, .and maybe, pour over the , "Independence Beige" to see if they can learn anything of the little bit of Belgium which until now has been to them all their known world, or strain their thought in the attempt to grasp the meaning of the terrible tragedy that is, being enacted on the European stage.</p>
        <p>The Bright Side. Perhaps so far the picture is painted in too sombre a shade. Earl's Court is not altogether a place of sad countenances and benumbed minds. Happily here is 'a resilience in the human mind which enables it to rebound from the effect of its sorrows. Amongst all peoples there are the Mark Tapleys, who are happiest when they ought to be most miserable. Amongst the nation's guests at Earl's Court there are many making the best. of things, or at any rate contented by living entirely in the present. All that is practicable is being done to help the guests to forget their individual and national misfortunes. To help .the mothers a creche is provided, in which the little ones can be put. to sleep in quietness, and voluntary nurses and helpers watch over them while the mothers attend to laundry and other duties. Imagine a large number with twenty or thirty basinettes mostly occupied, and each with sufficient space all,round for the mother, to reach her, child without 'disturbing another. One little- chap we interviewed cackled and crowed and appeared to think, the devastation of Belgium a huge joke, and the Kaiser a brick for enabling this cherub to visit England so early in life. Speak- ing of Belgian babies generally, while the parents on the whole appear under the average height of. English people, the babies are fat and well favoured like the first lot of kine that waddled out of the Nile, as told in the dream of the Egyptian King.. We have_some fine speci- mens of these babes amongst ourßelgian guests in Warrington, and local mothers will have to look to their laurels at the next baby show. Round one , largo stove 'young children play cosily—by the other two newly-made mothers bath their babes. One section is railed off and notices affixed to the effect that fresh milk may be obtained at stated hours for children under three years. Further on we enter a room where noisy, happy children march and sing under the direction of two ladies—very. arduous work it seemed too. Still further, we come to the play- room—here rocking horses, dol&amp; houses, toys of all descriptions from happy English homes have been given to amuse little Belgians, • In this room we were arrested by the Words in .French and Flemish "Father forgive them, for they know not what they do "—a prayer which those homeless and bereft hearts must find difficult to re-echo when applied,as intended, to Germany.</p>
        <p>A Gigantic Bedroom. • One of *the buildings is converted into a laundry, where batches of women work in turns; another is a sewing moth, where competent help and advice is given in the repairing and• re- making of clothes, and the knitting of comforts for the Belgian soldiers.</p>
        <p>The famous Welcome Club is now the hospital, not only for refugees, but also for convalescent soldiers. The area of the Stadium is the great dining hall, and we witnessed the plentiful meal of hot-pot. An astonishing sight is the thousand beds ranged up the side of the amphitheatre—here the men sleep, provision for women and children being made elsewhere. Was there ever such a huge bedroom?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>ALOWYCH CENTRE.</head>
        <p>THE ALOWYCH CENTRE.</p>
        <p>This differs in many respects from Earl's Court. There .are the central offices of the relief movement. Adjoining these a disused skating rink forms part of the crescent where Kingsway joins the Strand. This building may be looked on as a Belgian Club, mostly frequented by the better classes. Here they congregate day by day, to meet friends and relatives, seek letters or information, change' money, dx., and then return to the boarding- houses and vheap hotels, where accommodation has been provided by the committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>" Likely.'" People.</head>
        <p>" Likely.'" People.</p>
        <p>The majority of these are not seekink hospi- tality. Many still have money, rescued from their wrecked country: Many seek advice about returning—or about a new country—but a large number await the hospitality of English homes. It is a curious experience to miwe amongst this motly crowd looking for "likely people." One wants if possible to get hoLl r f the good, simple, bourgeois class, who are usually clean, respectable and willing to work. After making a little tour we approach i% worthy-looking pair, and in our politest man- ner and best French begit;; "Pardon Maclame, • 4.11_4i5. e4-ei3 tilte vous cherchez Phospitalitti 44? We get varying replies</p>
        <p>"Thank yoi Afaclaine. We are, very grateful, but my husband is a glass-cutter and we are getting Hants fur Aluerio, where, he can follow his trade "; ur -</p>
        <p>remereiments,•Madain—hut I am ox- peeting to meet my husband hero.," From one quite respeetabk-livAing pair we got the astounding reply:— " We are most profoundly gr te:'u I. bet me are seehing 14Q11SP or hotel 'tear Ettin, where we have just played our boy in yc.ur w on&amp;rful school,—Collapse of the Warrington deputa- tion.</p>
        <p>We pull ourselves together and address a pleasant-looking man, who speaks fail- 'Eng- lish. After Some conversation we find he has already had a geed home provided, but threw it up because he considered the Anowsy vided insufficient for his nerd,i! This seems risky, so we address a honllS young woman with a little girl of six, and, to our joy, find she has a mother and grandmother and lineleB, and, in fact, just the kind of three-generation fainily we want—and we fix up with them. ;ionietimes when suitable guests are found, some other difficulty may arise, such as "- Not to be allocated" printed across a man's regis- trittion card. This means that. the Govertunent can employ him and be must not be sent away. Then unless 3'ou can how that he is going to a town where suitable work' is obtainahle, you party may fall to pieces,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>Question of Worth.</head>
        <p>The Question of Worth.</p>
        <p>Perhaps your readers would like to ha\ve our opinion of the Belgian refugees as a whole. Are they worthy of assistance, and, if sp, what further help is likely to be required? Some have•approaChed this problem entertaining the delusion that all the Belgian people•are heroes. they have :the modern ThermoPiThe Liege) in their Minds, and, judge this people by the standard of their . hero king. Closer association reveals to them that the Bel- • qlitlig arga ulized lot'good, bad aucl</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <p>ent; very like onrselves; we perchance discover HI them signs of original sin and possibly a lack Of sufficient, evidences .of gratitude for kindness shown. Then these worthy folk are apt to. rush to the other extreinei'and, maybe, without definite intention, do their best, or their worst, to discourage the helpful effort!, of others.</p>
        <p>surely. this tendency to judge the question by the merits or demerits of individual, or of a people, is a serious fault. What we have to ask ourselves is: What is' our National and Christian duty to the Belgian people? National duty.—Fidelity to the treaty obliga- tion of England to preserve the neutrality of Belgium. In this we 'failed. Belgium had herself to try to guard her land and repel the invasion of the cruel and unscrupulous treaty- breakers. The task was beyond Belgium's strength, but the time saved by the attempt at a critital period saved France, and, perchance, saved England. Christian duty.—We are all brethren, chil- dren of the one great Father, linked to one another by an Elder Brother. uho has won the right to our love and .Obedience and who has said "Inasmuch as ye have ione it onto cue of thqse ye have done itsunto Me."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>Loyal and Grateful Hearts.</head>
        <p>Loyal and Grateful Hearts.</p>
        <p>In the light of this obligation springing from such a two-fold source, the question of the worthi- ness or unworthiness does not • arise. Were all the Belgian refugees .unsatisfactory and un- grateful—which they are not—our duty would still be the same, although the discharge of that duty would not be so pleasant and interesting as so many have proved it to be. Like many others, we have found amongst- our Belgian guests many with loyal and grateful hearts as -well as refined and cultured minds. Such can be found in all people. We have met the. like even in Germany, and when we think of friends there our sympathies are with the boy of whom we have just heard who, out of his own little heart, voiced the prayer " God bless the Ger- mans because they have no friends." But our immediate duty is to the Belgians, and to the question, " What further assistance is likely to be required?" we reply that the need is still great, and likely to continue. Although the number coming from Holland (now about one hundred daily) is likely to diminish, there are many Belgian twines, particularly those at seaside resorts, where hospitality was only arranged for the winter iind spring. Further, there are a number of Belgians who came over with limited funds and who have so far been able to maintain themselves, but now, with funds exhausted, are obliged to seek mainten- ance from the English people. On the other hand, the steps taken to provide work for all able-bodied Belgians is reducing the amount required to provide hospitality as the guests. are able, are required, and are glad to provide a substantial part of the cost out of their earn- ings. This especially applies to homes in towns like Warrington where the industries are Sc) varied and where skilled as well as unskilled labour can be employed. When the people of Warrington throw their energy into any effort they always do it well. Is this not a work worthy their best? Much More might be accomplished. If stimulus, leadership and encouragement 'is forthcoming from the town's committee So much the better, but if not the work should be done without this. aid. . _ _</p>
        <p>At the least this committee, we understand, is willing ,to register the names of all for 'Whom separate Warrington homes or accommo- dation in existing homes is provided.</p>
        <p>A SONG OF SPRING. (In the train to Southampton.) The almond tree puts out its bloom Amid the land's abysmal gloom; The crocus lifts its shining spears ln spite of poor old England's tears; The snowdrop spreads its stainless Snow Above the red world's waste and woe; And, though war's havoc comes and kills, Behold the dancing daffodils! And every trunk and every bough 'With new life pulses even now. Already winter yields to spring; Already birds begin to sing; And all the buds on all the trees Are big with cheerful prophecies. And, though war's winter wraps the world, Within its heart sweet buds arc curled Of peace that in the darkness stir; And from the seeming sepulchre, Beneath the slowly brightening skies, I see all lovely things Winter by spring shall soon be slain; The yellow sun resumes his reign; Death is not lord, though millions die; War shall not last eternally. Though, to our poor eyes, tired and blind, The hidden God is hard to find, Each springing flower and leaf I see ,Brings hope and comfort back to me.. And so into the eyes of hate , I calmly smile, and watch and wait, And on the world of death and sin Look boldly forth, for life shall win. Death is not lord, so God decrees: Behold the flowering almond trees! ARTHUR BENNETT.</p>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</p>
        <p>The Survival of the Side. Car.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>CINEMA HELPS CATHOLICS</head>
        <p>CINEMA HELPS CATHOLICS</p>
        <p>Sunday E‘ening Entertainment For St. Mary's Parish Hall Fund.</p>
        <p>A special performance was given at the Star Kinema House, Church-street, on Sunday even- ing, when the proceeds were given towards the building fund of the new Parish Hail, in con- nection with St. Mary's Roman Catholic Church, Buttermarket-street. Several excellent items, including the over- tures " Stars and Stripes," " Cavalleria Rusti- cana," and selections from " The Marriage Market," were rendered by Mrs. IV. B. Roberts's orchestra, while Miss Marie Houghton, who was in excellent voice, sang " Angels tluard Thee " and " The Rosary." Mr. J. Rabbitt gave "Dear Heart " and " I Did Not Know," while Mrs. ilaviour and Mr. Herbert “eary rendered violin solos, and Mr. J. Blackburn selections on the cornet. .</p>
        <p>His Majesty's .recent visit to his troops in Prance, Lord Kitchener's visit to Liverpool, a - fine patriotic picture "England Expects" were among the fine cinema pictures flashed on the screen.</p>
        <p>• The Rev. Father Wilson voiced hearty thanks to the artistes and members of the orchestra for giving their services, and also expressed the thanks of the -committee to the proprietor of the Star Kinema for so kindly providing the bioseope portion of the entertainment, thus greatly, helping the Parish Hall Building Fund. Mr. J. Rabbitt brought the performance to a close by singing the National Anthem, " in which the audiencie heartily joined.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>GOOD NEWS. Many Readers Have Heard It.</head>
        <p>GOOD NEWS. Many Readers Have Heard It.</p>
        <p>"Good news travels fast," and thousands of backache sufferers are glad to learn that prompt relief is within their reach.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Smith, of 33, Melville-street, War- rington, says :—'• For fully three months I was so crippled with lumbago that I had to walk About with my hack bent like an old man. The pain used to catch me right in the bottom of nty back and strike down my loins. It was quite like a knife going through- me. and I was so bad at times that -I hardly dared to move. How I managed to keep about, I don't know. I was in pain all the time, day and night, and the continual suffering almost wore me out. " I tried plasters and embroeations, and I also paid five guineas for a belt, but I am sorry to say that I got no relief. I 'began to think I should never get well again. It was through reading a testimonial that I tried Doait's Backache Kidney Pills. I had not taken many (loses of these pills before I found that they were relieving me. Ne4HOem to say. I persevered with. them, and it was not long before I was quite (1(1.0, Doan's- Pills have, been worth their weight in gold to me, and I am sure they would be to anyone who suffers with a bad back. • (Signed) T. Smith." Severe pain and tenderness in the back and loins, aggravated by motion, and often coming on suddenly, indicate lumbago—a rheumatic condition of • the muscles or nerves of the loins. A thorough course of Doan's Backache Kidney Pills will eliminate the excess uric acid which is almost invariably the cause of lumbago and backache.</p>
        <p>Price 2s. 9d. a box, six boxes 13s. 9d.; of all dealers, or from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Don't ask for backache or. kidney pills,—ask DIS- TINCTLY for DON'S 13.0Clittelle Kiduey rills, the sttuie as Mr. Smith had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>JEAN. 1117CQUOY.</head>
        <p>JEAN. 1117CQUOY.</p>
        <p>Artillerie, which is the equivalent to sergeant- major in a British Infantry regiment, and, for the sake of convenience, we will call him by the familiar title, of sergeant-major. • A CHARMED LIFE.</p>
        <p>Sergeant-Major Rucquoy has been in the Army' since he .was eighteen. lie is now thirty- one, and represents a fine, -clean type of Belgian manhood, lie strikes one as capable, .but is modest and -unassuming. No doubt he could tell a great tale; but, soldier-like,' thinks little of .his own exploits.- lie has been all through the war, was at the battles round Louvain, was engaged in the siege and subsequent retreat at Antwerp, and for months has been fighting with the Belgian Army round .Dixtunde and by the coast. lie thinks he must possess a charmed life—he has laid six horses killed under him, and while shells have again and -.again rained round him, killing and wounding comrades on right and loft, so far he has escaped without a scratch. lie has often been hit by portions of shell, but these have been nearly Spent and have failed to penetrate. SIGNS OF GERMAN DETERIORATION.</p>
        <p>Sergeant-Major liticquoy entertains no delu- sions about the difficulty of the task of driving the Germans out of Belgium, but he is confi- dent it can and will be done. lie does not think, however, that his country will be clear of the invaders until August. At the same time he recognises that the Germans are show- ing signs of wear and tear; they have lost their initiative. Moreover, he brought with him proof of the scarcity of metal from which the Germans are now suffering. This prof .is furnished by the caps of the fuses of three spent shells picked up at various Periods of the war. The earliest specimen is of copper, weighty and businesslike in every look. In the middle period shell the copper has shrunk to very small and flimsy proportions. In the last specimen the copper has vanished altogether, its place being taken by steel and the surrounding metal being aluminium and the interior metal a very light and porous looking composition. In corroboration it may lA added that the " Petit Parisien " has recently pointed out that at. the beginning of the war the fuse of the shell of the German field gun was made entirely of copper, and weighed close on 21b. Gradually the copper failed, and was re- placed in part by aluminium, until towards the end of the heavy fighting in Champagne in Feb- ruary the Fretiili began to find German shells the fuses of which were made of aluminium With an iron cap without any copper, while the shell eases were made of cast iron instead Of steel. These iron-aluminium fuses weigh just over lloz.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>NEXT AUGUST,</head>
        <p>NEXT AUGUST,</p>
        <p>The sergeant-major tells us that the little Belgian Army has never lost faith or courage, although they have had their times of deep depression. It was expected that tho French would have come to their aid much sooner, and their apparent unpreparedness was a great disappointment, and again it seemed -long be- fore the British came. lie tells us how the spirit of hope was kindled when the British Marines appeared at Antwerp, and of .the feeling of confidence engendered when the little Belgian Army took their place to the left of the long line of Allies from the sea to the Vosges.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>KINDNESS ACKNOWLEDGED.</head>
        <p>KINDNESS ACKNOWLEDGED.</p>
        <p>He has been charmed with his visit to War- ringtoti, and desires: publicly to thank the Warrington people for their .kindness to his wife and family, and to the English people generally for their hospitality to his com- patriots. He speaks in glowing terms of the British; between these .and: the 'Belgians there is a marked feeling of comradeship, more marked probably than between the Belgians and the French, '"rhey are' brave -ifien," he says, "good comrades, and they never let go," He returned to his horses 414 d his gons Saturday morning. Au revoir brave son of a brave people! May your good fortune continue, and ,rest content we will care for your dear ones until you come again to, claim them, and when Germany has paid to the uttermost ,farthin,r" for the wrong she has dmte your people, and you have again a home ready in your martyred and 4eroiti country l•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>,f ppy TO SWIM."</head>
        <p>A ,f ppy TO SWIM."</p>
        <p>. . The death tweurreil ott Tuesday, in his sixty- seventh year, of Mr. J. B. Johnson, who had 4 great reputation in his day as a swininwr and diver. Johnson was a Manchester man apd mcmher of it famous family of sw hunters. _For many years he was employed as instructor and attendant at the tiorton Baths, and he con- tinued to be the attendant there until a few weeks ago. la las early days johason had a great name as a trapey,e performer, and his skill in this form of athletics probably prepared him for the special work which he (lid in the water-- as a diver into shallow water from , great heights. In PM he made a remarkable dive into the Thames from London Bridge. In the pet•formance of this, dive he had the co-Opera- tion of his brother Peter, another famous swimmer, who got into the water from a boat at a given time and affected to be in danger of drowning. Another of Johnson's remarkable performances was a dive of 45ft. into 21t.. OW.. of water at Clough thin, Staffordshire,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>RESPONSIBILITY FOR ACCIDENTS.</head>
        <p>RESPONSIBILITY FOR ACCIDENTS.</p>
        <p>(Queries and correspondence should be addressed to "The nub," do the. Editor. Replies cannot be sent by post, and all queries will be answered in our columns.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>ENAMELLING AT HOME.</head>
        <p>ENAMELLING AT HOME.</p>
        <p>Practically every cyclist has at one time or another attempted to improve the look of his mount by either touching up the enamel or essaying to re-enamel the frame. entirely. I expect the great majority ot them have been sorry afterwards that they ever took on the job. As a general rule it is not a success. A moment's thought will convince anyone that it is futile to try to make a rough surface smooth by touching up with enamel. It is impossible to fill up the holes and bring them' level again with the rest of the frame. It is expecting the - much altogether that such a result can be obtained. I always think that the result Is to aggravate the trouble. The Machine looks worse, to my mind, after the work is finished, if a flush finish is desired,' then a flush surface must he had in the first place. 'This flush, surface may either be the metal itself or the old coating rubbed down. smooth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>HOW TO SUCCEED.</head>
        <p>HOW TO SUCCEED.</p>
        <p>1 du not believe that as good a finish can be produced at home as by the professional man, since the important part of the process, namely, "stoving," must be omitted. All the same, an amateur can add greatly to the ap- pearance of his machine if he goes to work in. the right way. Perhaps failure is due, not to the painter or to the material, but to lack of knowledge as to how to proceed. The machine should be taken .to pieces in the first place. Then the frame must be stripped of the old paint. This can be done easily by scraping off the old enamel by the aid of a sharp pen.- knife, or similar instrument. As a general rule the frame must then be rubbed down with. emery cloth. This operation, however, May be omitted if the old paint has been scraped off evenly. - In any case, to polish with emery cloth will give a better finish. The enamel. should then be laid on evenly with a suitable brush. A full twenty-four hours should be allowed before the ..second -coat is applied-, There is no necessity to "take down" the' enamel with emery cloth between the coats un- less dust has settled on it. A thin coat makes the job complete.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>ENERGY FROM SUGAR.</head>
        <p>ENERGY FROM SUGAR.</p>
        <p>A number of people have bee-I-I-WI:Y. surprised at the fact that our soldiers at the Front ap-- pear to have such a craving for sweet things. Those, however, who know the feeding value of sugar have long since realised that this con- stituent in all feeding stuffs is particularly useful in those cases where a great amount of energy has to be 'expended: Sonic years ago a long series of tests were carried out by the German authorities with a view to finding out -whether it was •a .suitable Material for soldiers undergoing • severe hardships and laborious work. The results )were so excellent that sugar became a recognised diet in the Army. I know cyclists are always eager to hear of the little things that Matter. Sugar is a simple and convenient form of sustenance; especially in those instances when the rider is called upon to exert himself to a greater extent than usual. Sugar is an energy provider, owing to the changes that: take place in its composition when assimilated into the body. , Hence its value.</p>
        <p>THIRD PASSENGER MOTOR CYCLING. It was prophesied when .the cycle car mid light car made their appearance that the side.; car would rapidly become extinct. This; has certainly not proved to be the case. No, doubt some motor cyclists have given up their 'foe- mer mounts in favour of cars, but all tblb same the number of side-car outfits has in- creased enormously. There seems to be a great demand on the part of married people for a combination outfit; in fact, nearly all the motor cycles se fitted are used by a .couple of people of different sex. If there is a youngster a place is usually found for him or her on the carrier or in front of the side-car. Them is a number of side-cars on the market, ever, which are specially constructed to lkwom- modate the people. These tars may 1,40 either of the "sociable" type, or the arralagenient be such that one person sits in fr 0,4- of the other. It is often stated that side-cars; are overloadeid. This may he true in sop* (,•ases, but present- day construction is .suclk as• to render.it possible to carry :an extpa passenger in comfort and without puffing any undue strain on the machine,</p>
        <p>RECENT IMPROVEMENTS. If the engine be of sufficient power, if ii change gear be fitted, and if the whole °thin/- be strongly constructed, as in .the case et the modern Machine, there is no reason -,03, there should not be three up. It has ,lteen done fre- quently in the past, and, is tar as one can see, without any haruh ke,utting. There is, thercfore,'no argpmen* ag4inst continuing the Practice with the }llOlO efficient and more reli- able machines that are now on the market. coming of the detachable wheel has given au impetus in the direction of the use of the&lt; sidecar.. The immunity from sideslip enjoyed by the conthination outfit has been a strong point in its favour. The additional cost iltr, curred by the currying Of a third passenger t's very little. It will not make the tyro bill Or the petrol account much heavier. Ipt the case of a lower-powered machine it may take 500 miles off the life of the tyre of the driving wheel, but an average of sixty miles to thy. gallon should be obtained. • A heavier eugined outfit should average 3,000 miles if the tyres are changed over from time to time. As re- gards petrol, an average of 50 m.p.g. should 1* attained. Speed, however, will influence this Point. considerably.</p>
        <p>STREET ACCIDENTS. The official figures relative to street accidents are always of interest to road-users and vedestriaus uhike. The ututisties fur. the year</p>
        <p>ending 31st Dec oilier, 1914, have recently been published. The roads are in reality becoming safer, .though at first sight it might appear otherwise. The 'number of deaths and non- fatal accidents due to motor-propelled vehicles has increased, but ivilen it is remembered that the number of vehicles is greater to-day than ever before and that the total mileage is greater, the figures will appear in their true proportion. During 1914, 2,330 people were killed and 61,670 persons were injured. Eng- land and Wales. accounted for 1,991. Scotland 229 and Ireland 110. Horse-drawn vehicles are credited with • causing 633 fatal accident's. Motor omnibuses were responsible for 22 deaths, mechanically-propelled tramcars for 153, and for all other types of motors for '1,151. With reference, to non-fatal accidents. horse-drawn vehicles accomited for 13,256, motor 'buses 3,667. trams 7,110. All other motor-driven vehicles totalled 22,295 :nun-fatal accidents. The increase in accidents is not great when everything is considered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>PETROL CONSUMPTION.</head>
        <p>PETROL CONSUMPTION.</p>
        <p>I came across a case We other day of the 'owner of ,a 10-12h.p. light car who could never get more than 18 miles per gallon. This is very low, as his type of car is supposed to work out at 30 m.p.g. and I have known instances where 35 m.p.g. have been obtained. As this may be a common experience a few points on the subject may prove of use. It is impossible to give an exact formula for obtaining the most economical petrol consumption. it depends to a very great extent on the driver and his special method of handling his car. There is always a. possibility that the fault may lie in the adjust- ment of the carburetter, too large a jet, weak compression, undue friction in the transmis- sion,_or to the presence of a leak.. If all of these have been taken into consideration and found correct then undoubtedly the fault lies in the driver himself. In a hilly district the manipu- lation of the gear changing is a very important factor. Practice makes perfect, and in time this gear will be managed with better results. The throttle and spark controls can also be handled in a .way that makes for less petrol consumption.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>HOW TO TIME AN ENGINE,.</head>
        <p>HOW TO TIME AN ENGINE,.</p>
        <p>This question invariably crops up when two or three motor cyclists foregather. It is not a difficult operation, and the following should enable anyone to do the work in it .satisfactory Manner. The exhaust valve should be set to close just after the completion of the exhaust , stroke. When the '3lStOll is pbout 1/7 of the length of the stroke from the bottom of the fir- ing stroke it will then begin to open. As the exhaust closes the inlet should commence to open and remain open until the piston is 5 mm. past the dead centre on the compression stroke. In answer to a question which has reached me .from a correspondent v.ith regard to long versus short stroke, it is generally found that the long stroke engine is better on the hills than the short stroke engine, and moreover, it is fast on the level. The short stroke engine has, how- ever, ,been used for speed work, with marked success. It is Slightly easier to balance the short stroke engine, but on the point of flexi- bility there is not much difference.</p>
        <p>TEE lIEB.</p>
      </div>
