      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <p>+4.4.4.4.4.4-4.4.4, +4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4. 4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4. 4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4. &lt;•:•.4.4.•:.4.4.4.4. +++4:4 4:4 ++4:4 4:4.4:4k 44 4:4.4:1•4:••:••:••:4•4,:**4:4,4:4.•:**:••:••:••:•4:4•:•4:44v:44:4. 4:4•4:4.4:.•:4 4:e 4,4:4, .:1 0:41 0:41 •:I1 t) •:. •:• •::. •:* 0:4 •:,* 10&gt; .:.• 4 I:11 •:. 0:6 •:4, f) II:. A:0 I.:. 0 •:.• 0:0 t: i 0 4:0 4:41 •:• •:4 0 :11 .:. .:. .:. .:* ee .:. .4... .:. .:. .:. *4&gt; 4 I.'• .:. .:. .:. .) .:. ..... •:• *4:4 .:. .:. •:* 0:4, •:• .. 41:11 .4.. • •:. •:: • •:. •:* •:• •:* 4:. if 1 muslin is preferred. The fronts are pleated CHATand GOSSIP I and pressed, not 'stitched, and pretty roll-over collars, finely scalloped at the edges, finish the neck. A cravat of dull silk or ribbon is con- sidered a sufficient indication of mourning.</p>
        <p>These simple garments accompany plain. black tailored suits. Black and white materials of all kinds, striped, checked and spotted are also used for this type of blouse.</p>
        <p>Spring and Summer Accessories. Lace collars have lost none of their popu- larity-, and the selections at the big stores and shops are as beautiful as they are varied.. The Quaker Girl shape is shown in Maltese, Guipure, am! Shadow laces. Jabots have taken on a new lease of life, and in Valenciennes lace, fully arranged, are most attractive. Chemisettes of fine pique, prettily embroidered, have double collars, rather high, that flare out at the back, where the side that overlaps is caught by a button. Printed striped ninon most effectively used, for vest and collar schemes. The smartest military designs are in black and white, and grey, whilst spots are favoured also. Never before has such a re- markable variety in neckwear been obtainable. Very bright yellow gloves are making a doubt- ful bid for favour, but white doeskin and white washing kid are far more likely to be generally donned. The most -fashionable gloves have gauntlets, which are often turned back to show a contrasting lining.. para.sols have already appeared and are very fantastic in shape and trimming. Curtailed Mourning.</p>
        <p>Taking into consideration the deadly char- acter and magnitude of the war, the number of people of both sexes to be noted in deep mourning is surprisingly small. Arm-bands and other tokens of bereavement, suggested at the dutbreak of war, do not explain this ab- sence of sable attire, for such signs and tokens are common enough. The true reason may be found in the feeling prevalent among all classes, that an ostentatious display of mourn- ing for men who have died gallantly in tho defence of their country is out of place, and would be so regarded by the dead heroes. The fact that the poor are less disposed to revel in the outward signs and manifestations of sor- row, for their departed relatives, is a.sign that more sensible customs will be adopted in future. Far from evidencing a lack of proper feeling, the curtailment of mourning wear by all classes is a proof of good taste and com- mon-sense.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>By MARJORIE.</head>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>The Greek Royal Family. • The cosmopolitan element enters larpely into the various Courts of EUrope, and most of the monarchs and their consorts have near rela- tives in opposite camps taking part in the war. The King and Queen of Greece are especially awkwardly placed in this respect. The former is the son of a Danish father and a Russian mother. Re is nearly related to the Tsar, and is the nephew of Queen Alexandra. The Queen of Greece is German, one of the sisters of the Emperor William, and she shares to some extent, the energi, ambition, and arbitrary ways that characterise her brother. But for her near relationship to the Kaiser, and her sympathies with the Germans, it is, probable that the Greeks would, before now, have joined the Allies. It is only the Greek Royal Family who have restrained the Government and people of Greece from the desire to take part in the war.</p>
        <p>A Women's InternationAl Conference. A conference of the Women's International Council of Socialist and Labour Organisations has been held at Berne, in Switzerland, despite the difficulties of travel arising out of the war. The International Secretary is a German lady— Frau Klara Zetkin. She is also ow of the leaders of the German Social Democratic party. Twenty-eight delegates attended, Great Britain being represented by four ladies. The rela- tions between the latter and the German repre- sentatives were markedly cordial. The Social- ist women of Belgium wished to send represen- tatives also, but permission to cross the frontier was refused. The German women ran great risk, as did those from neutral countries pass- ing through Germany. The date of the Con- ference was not announced beforehand, for obvious reasons. These courageous women re- turned to their homes with the determination to carry out their propaganda for the termina- tion of the war at the earliest possible moment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>Women and Farm Work.</head>
        <p>Women and Farm Work.</p>
        <p>The employment of women in agricultural operations is common enough in Russia, Hun-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>11051 E NOTES.</head>
        <p>11051 E NOTES.</p>
        <p>Profitable Recipes and Useful</p>
        <p>Sugges-tions.</p>
        <p>Baked Eggs. Cut as many slices of bread as there are persons to be served. The bread should be 3in. thick, the crust removed, and a hole made in the middle, but not all the way through. These arc then fried in melted butter, placed in a shallow, buttered baking-dish, an egg broken in every cavity, the tops strewed' with salt, pepper and bits of butter, and baked live minutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>Macaroni and Veal Pate.</head>
        <p>Macaroni and Veal Pate.</p>
        <p>Take two cupfuls of'cold boiled macaroni and chop it fine; mix with it one egg well beaten, then line a plain mould with the macaroni; chop small the remains of cold roast veal, add a little powdered mace, pepper and salt; mix and 'moisten with gravy, then fill up the mould with the mixture. Spread some more macaroni over the top of the meat; cover the mould tightly, first with buttered paper, then with a pudding cloth. Place it in a pot of boiling water, and boil one hour and a half. Turn on to a dish, and serve with a little gravy or melted butter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>Macaroni and Tomatoes.</head>
        <p>Macaroni and Tomatoes.</p>
        <p>Put a handful of salt in a saucepan on the stove and bring to the boil; when it boils throw in the macaroni, broken into finger-length pieces and boil for twenty minutes, if boiled longer it becomes too soft and pasty. Strata through a colander, standing the colander in an empty saucepan to hold the water that is poured off. While it is boiling prepare the tomatoes. Take two large ripe ones and wipe thoroughly, cut them in half and place them in a little saucepan with a lump of butter about the size of a small walnut, add two or three chopped onions, a bunch of parsley, thyme, and one laurel leaf, tied together, a teaspoonful of salt, and a good dusting of pepper, and boil gently. When cooked pass the mixture through a strainer. In another saucepan place a small lump of butter and a tablespoonful of flour and work together by means of a wooden spoon, then ponr in the tomato mixture, stirring constantly. If too thick add a small quantity of boiling water. Pour the macaroni on to a very hot dish and pour the tomato puree over it, mixing it carefully until it is all red, or, if the saucepan is large enough, mix them in the saucepan. Serve with grated cheese. This is an excellent</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>thumo.lo6-wxeNveo=9,o=c-vv4m,-..0.2du o CHILDREN'S CORNER. Conducted by UNCLE NED. KA2AVYAN2•OVO:t2sOVWXY-640,20N2XWW3K</head>
        <p>thumo.lo6-wxeNveo=9,o=c-vv4m,-..0.2du o CHILDREN'S CORNER. Conducted by UNCLE NED. KA2AVYAN2•OVO:t2sOVWXY-640,20N2XWW3K</p>
        <p>A HEARTY INVITATION to join this Corner is extended to all children with read it regularly. Application for membership, stating name, address and age, should be made to 'UNCLE NED, 'Examiner' Office, Bewsei-street, War- rington." Every girl or boy, whose name is in the Big Book, is entitled to enter the competi- tions held from time to time, to the winners of which useful prizes are awarded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>LEARNING TO BE SOBER.</head>
        <p>LEARNING TO BE SOBER.</p>
        <p>Mr DEAR CHILDREN,-</p>
        <p>As you are all aware, I last week promised to grant the wish of one of my Jittle nephews and tell you how even the children may help i.hu great cause of temperapee during this terrible war.</p>
        <p>You have no vote, nor is it to be expected that -you can influence' the opinions or habits of your elders (except in. one or two cases). Therefore, it is to yourselves that your attention is confined; you yourselves must grow up to sober manhood and womanhood', ever remem- bering why sobriety—like honesty—!' is the best policy." (Of course; if you are honest to yourself you will be. sober.) And the sooner you. learn, the better it will be for you.</p>
        <p>How, then, will you learn? Bands of hope are, of course, excellent insti- tutions. If you attend their meetings you will be told what drink has done and what it is doing—how it is the chief cause of crime and insanity in our good old country. You will also learn what it has not done, nor is doing. But the simplest and best plan is to use your own eyes. Every one of you must at one time or another have seen a drunken man or woman. Would you like ever to become like that—much less to be seen like that? Would there be a single advantage gained (except by the brewers)? Have you -ever known anybody to gain any- thing by it? .• . . BO we know there are many who only take a little liquor ! And yet—have you ever known them to be proud of it? Have they never wished, at one time or another, that nobody knew about it? Never mind what they them- selves declare; draw your own conclusions always.</p>
        <p>Or does it do any harm? Ask a man who has for a long time been in the habit of taking drink to break that habit. Ile cannot! Ile will tell</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH. Useful Information in Popular</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH. Useful Information in Popular</p>
        <p>Avoid Colds.</p>
        <p>To keep off colds, avoid over-heating, over- eating, over-drinking, overwork, exposure, and other persons who have colds. Leaving an overheated room for the open air often gives a cold. Over-eating gives the digestive organs far more than they can accomplish, and over-drink- ing is a sure promoter of any sickness the flesh is heir to.</p>
        <p>If Children's colds were treated as real illnesses, they would be saved; much. ill-health in after life. Many very serious complaints begin with% simple cold. A mother, as soon as she finds that a 'child has taken a chill, should keep it in bed, and give it light, simple food. If this does not cheek the cold send for a doctor, but generally a chill will yield to a few. days in bed.. In any case, a had cough and high tem- perature requires immediate attention, as these may be the forerunner of an attack of bronchitis, pneumonia, or pleurisy.</p>
        <p>Eat Little and Live Long. • Horace Fletcher, the most eminent and per- sistent advocate of simple food, perfect mas- tication and slow eating, said that if it were not for our way of eating the average of human life would be 120 years. Absolute fasting, he says, is Nature's eure for bodily ills. Rheuma- tism, he maintains, can be cured by fasting from 15 to 20 days. Apples as a Tonic.</p>
        <p>The apple is such a common fruit, says a medical writer, that very few persons are familiar with its remarkable efficacious medi- cinal properties. Everybody ought to know that the very best thing they can do is to eat apples just before: retiring for the night.</p>
        <p>Persons uninitiated in the mysteries of the fruit are liable to throw up 'their hands in horror at the visions of dyspepsia which such a suggestion may summon up, but no harm can come even to a delicate system by the eating of the ripe and juicy apple before going to bed. The apple is excellent brain food, because it has more phosphoric acid in easily digested shape than any other fruit. It excites the action of the liver, promotes sound and healthy sleep and thoroughly disinfects the mouth. This is not all; the apple prevents indigestion and throat diseases.</p>
        <p>with a brisk fight now and then. The weather 'here at present is far, better than what it was a couple of months ago, when the snow and frost was on the ground, and if the water was cleared out of the trenches it would seem like Arcady in the trenches. Our soldiers at the Front who receive gifts from friends like you at home are very grateful, and although they do not write and say so they think all the more. The people of England have proved their value and shown how much they appreciate the work our soldiers are doing at the Front, and although I am the father of three children, who are not old enough to knit socks, they do not forget " Tommy " at the Front," but pray for the return of every soldier who is serving at the Front. I ,must close now, thanking you again for your gift and, also .the note.—Believe me to be, your very grateful friend,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>MORE ROOM-MORE RENT.</head>
        <p>MORE ROOM-MORE RENT.</p>
        <p>Tenant: "Our house is in very bad shape. One of the walls has bulged out several inches."</p>
        <p>Landlord: "Is that so? Then as the house is larger, I'll have to raise your rent."</p>
        <p>SING! (By E. IlExronn.) When things in the world about you</p>
        <p>Seem someway going wrong, And clouds shut out life's sunshine, Then sing some cheerful song. Some blithesome and happy measure Will lighten a life of .care, And give us strength for the burden</p>
        <p>That all of us have to bear. Sing and your weary neighbour Will hear the cheerful strain, And his heavy heart grow lighter As the fields grow glad from rain. In the spell of the music's magic His lips repeat the song. And so men help each other And make the weak grow strong. Sing and forget, my children,• The cares that fill the day, There's help in a song, believe it, And prove it as you may. There's nothing gained by sighing, It's ever the wisest plan To sing away One's trouble And be happy if we can.</p>
        <p>No Increase in Price. Reduce Your Meat Bill.</p>
        <p>Puddings 0 made with ATORA shredded Beef Suet are sustaining and digestible-11b. cartons 10d., with recipes—goes much further than raw Suet. Ask your grocer for it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>Lanutiage.</head>
        <p>Lanutiage.</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S WORLD.</p>
        <p>gpry and the Balkan States, but Western women are not fitted for heavy field work, and it is not likely that they will take it up in any considerable numbers in this country. Never- theless the Government is affording assistance to an experiment to train 300 women in agri- culture and farm work. They will be specially selected as suitable for this occupation, and farms have been taken for the purpose of train- ing. It will be interesting to see what is the outcome of this experiment to employ women on the land. There are, of course, many women accustomed to outdoor work who would make very capable farmers, but they are the exception, and for the vast majority of women field labour is pot a desirable occupation, and is only to be justified as an emergency measure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>Khaki In Paris.</head>
        <p>Khaki In Paris.</p>
        <p>Khaki is the most popular colour- in Paris, after black. It has numerous tones, the most charming being deeply tinged with yellow and resembling that worn by officers of our Indian Army. Tailored • suits. of cloth in these shades are made, as far as possible, on military lines. Much tulle and chiffon .are used for khaki- toloured evening dresses, and a favourite con- trast is bright red, which gives an excellent effect. Brilliant roses or poppies-are preferred to other flowers just now. Girls in their early teens are given party frocks in khaki shades, with orange sashes, and the same coloured ribbon bows are worn in the hair. Black and khaki is a favourite contrast for girls, and quite young children. White muslin blouses have khaki collars, cuffs and belts; With these, skirts to match the latter are worn, together with black shoes, stockings and bows for the coiffure. All skirts are fuller for children and young girls, and when not kilted show the fashionable flare.</p>
        <p>Mourning Shirt Blouses. The blouse occupies so important a position in the scheme of dress that there is always something new to say about it. This season's designs are ,recognised at a glance by their neck. One of the latest developments of the latter is known as the high-low, since it may be worn open or closed across the front. Scal- lops finish the fronts of many models, and a small buttbn is frequently placed in each; the frill at the waist is also scalloped. Plain collars have immense ears, which may be turned up or down, and these are, as a rule, made of fine pique.. Fine white organdie mus- lin is extensively used for simple Summer shirts, but in the case of mourning crisp white</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <p>way of using up dry ends of cheese. Do not throw away the water in which the macaroni has been boiled, it will come in for the founda- tion of a white or thick soup.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>HERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>HERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>Men and Women.</p>
        <p>A man's idea of going into politics is not being on speaking ternis with his neighbour over something neither of them understands. A woman gets mad with a Man who can't do anything useful about the house, but she despises him if be can. A woman is willing tO take her husband's judgment, so she can say she told him so When it doesn't come out that way.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <head>More.</head>
        <p>More.</p>
        <p>He had been .calling on the young lady for many moons, but, being bashful, his suit pro- gressed slowly. Finally, according to the "New York Times," she decided it was up to her to start somethtng, so the next time he called she pointed to the rose 'in the buttonhole of his coat, and said:</p>
        <p>"I'll give you a kiss for that rose." A large, open-face blush meandered over his counten- ance, but the exchange was made. Then he grabbed his hat and started to leave the room. "Why, where are you going?" she asked in surprise.</p>
        <p>"To the—er—florist for more roses," he ex- claimed. .</p>
        <p>Fainting is Out of Fashion. A prominent physician who in his younger days used to treat many cases of fainting among females, says the "fainting lady" has gone out of fashion, athletics and a more sane system of living having put an end to the trouble. " Now- adays," says the doctor, " women are ashamed to faint. It is too kittenish. All women want to appear strong, even if they are naturally weak. And as a sex they are considerably more robust and more fascinating than they were a few years back. The reason for it is that women go in .for more outdoor life and recreation than did their mothers or their grandmothers. They may partake of more highly seasoned food and eat more than is good for them at times, but they lead a more athletic life, and for that reason do not have to faint, legitimately or otherwise, im matter what the shock might be. We are going to have a stronger race as a eon- quence."</p>
        <p>you that if be goes without his liquor be be- comes depressed. Do you think that, if the constant use of a thing presently makes a man unable to preserve a joyful outlook upon life without it, that thing can be good in any way? Besides, it has been proved that drink is in- jurious to the system, disorganising to the brain, and numbing to the conscience. And the funny thing is that it doesn't quench your thirst! As a matter of fact, it only aggravates Al Does not that fact alone prose that It cannot be the riget sort of liquor to take? Try to grow a bulb in a glass of intoxicating liquor; an i then write the result to Your loving UNCLE NED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <head>THOUGHT FOR THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>THOUGHT FOR THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>"I see that a man cannot give himself up to drink without being miserable one;half his days and mad the other. . . . Moreover, drinking spoils one's good looks."—ANNE BRONTE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <head>GRATEFUL SOLDIER</head>
        <p>GRATEFUL SOLDIER</p>
        <p>SENDS KINDLY LETTER TO ONE OF MY</p>
        <p>NIECES. VALUE OF DRY SOCKS.</p>
        <p>Miss Janet Dunlop, daughter of Mr. G. A. Dunlop, assistant curator at the Museum, and a scholar in Standard V. at Arpley-street School, and one of my many nieces, recently sent out a pair of socks, along with others from the same school, to be handed to one of .the South Lancashires at the Front. She has re- ceived the following letter of acknowledgment from Corporal S. Shaw, " D " Company, 2nd South Lancashire Regiment : March 22nd, 1915.</p>
        <p>My Dear Little 'Friend,— .• PerhaPs you will ,be surprised at receiving this letter from me, but when I tell you that I have just received a pair of socks with your very nice and thoughtful note inside, I thought the least I could du would be to acknowledge the receipt of Same.' Although a few months have elapsed since .Christmas, when they were intended for, they are just as acceptable now as they would have been then, and after a turn in the trenches a pair of dry socks are as accept- able as a hot meal. I expect you would like to hear a little about the war, but as the censors are very strict we have to leave names of places severely alone, but I can safely tell you that the war at .present is worked (or so it seeing to me) on far different lines than at the commencement, when long marches were the order of the day, with an occasional light to relieve the monotony. NOW it is nothing but trench work for us chap,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0254" n="art0254">
        <head>eljurrij Rehm.</head>
        <p>eljurrij Rehm.</p>
        <p>AUGMENTING A LIVING.</p>
        <p>Writing in the current issue of the St. Barnabas' Parish Magazine, the Vicar (the Rev. J. 11.. O. Bollingworth) says: "In June, 1913, the Committee of the Benefice Augmentation Fund asked the Vicar to raise a sum of £5O, aml they would give a grant itt proportion. In June, 1914, another grant was offered, and in November of last year the sum of £l3B was sent to this fund. This £l3B was increased to OM. The Ecclesiastical Commissioners were then approached with a view to their accepting the £5OO and giving a, grant of like amount from their funds. Last-month they decided to do so. The £1,900 has now .been invested by these Com- missioners, and as long as this money remains with them they will pay to the Vicar of the parish for the time being three, per cent, per annum, or £3O per annum. The value of this living has, therefore, been increased from £,200 to 030 per annum. Our efforts have been to raise and invest £l3B, and this will bring in a sum of £3O yearly."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <head>CALL TO ARMS AM) TO AA ORK.</head>
        <p>THE CALL TO ARMS AM) TO AA ORK.</p>
        <p>Rector lIItI Compulsion.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Canon Willis, preaching at the Parish Church on Sunday morning, spoke, " by special request," on the requirements of the war, and appealed to every physically lit man to put to himself the question: Is it my duty to go? If not, is it not my duty to work and do everything I can so that we may gain the victory for the benefit of mankind?" It was not fair, he said, that sonic should be sacri- ficing their lives while others were holding back from their duty; and, although he had always been in favour of the voluntary sys- tem, he thought it possible that a compulsory system might be introduced. We were fighting for the preservation of our own liberties, for the liberties of the smaller countries on the Continent, and, he believed, for the Church of God and the faith of Jesus Christ. Dr. Ryle, the son of the late Bishop of Liverpool, said the other day, that he had been in Germany studying for some years, and that there was not a single professor in Ger- many, when he was there, who believed in God or in Jesus -Christ. He (the Rector) did not think for a moment that the Germans could have done the horrible things of which we have heard if they believed in God and in our Lord Jesus Christ. •</p>
        <p>We wanted men, not only to fight, but to work. Germany had 5,000,000 or 6,000,000 of lighting men. We had not anything like that number, but the Colonies were responding to the call with large contingents, and new armies had also come into being. Still, we needed more men. As to the industrial aspect of the campaign, we were told by authorities that there were men who. did not work as they should. The majority of the workers on ayma- meats were working satisfactorily, but it ap- peared that a minority ere not.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>GOSPEL OF BROTHERHOOD</head>
        <p>GOSPEL OF BROTHERHOOD</p>
        <p>Preached At Working Men's Mission</p>
        <p>Anniversary.</p>
        <p>The milestones on a traveller's path are indications to him of the progress he is Making On his journey; and when the scholars of the Working Men's Mission assembled • on Sunday morning in the chapel in Thewlis-street to open the day's celebration of the Sunday school anni- versary it was noted .that the 4lth year had been reached in the progress of the work amongst the children. Despite many draw- backs the general advance of the past years had been successfully.. maintained, and the respon- sibility which rests upon the shoulders of the workers can bnly be described as great, when it is mentioned that upwards of Mt scholars are receiving religious instruction every Sabbath day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>Difficulties Surmounted.</head>
        <p>Difficulties Surmounted.</p>
        <p>It has been the custom for many years to hold the afternoon and evening services in the Parr Hall, but this. year the .hall was not at liberty, owing to it being require(' for military pur- poses. This difficulty—which seemed at first an unsurmountable one—Of securing a building whose holding capacity was of such (limensions as to allow of the services being held in com- fort, was overcome by the generous offer, by the trustees, of the Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>War Equalises AU.</head>
        <p>War Equalises AU.</p>
        <p>The preacher was Mr. J. W. Deakin, of North- wich, and at. the morning service, piieachin„f, from -Philemon i. 1, he portrayed the great effort which the aged Apostle Paul made to break down the division which existed between Onesimus, the slave, and Philemon, his master. It Was such an effort that was made by Garri- son Lloyd and others in connection with the slave trade of America. That the gospel of brotherhood was progressing was very forcibly semi to-day, contended the preacher, for, although he .was not going to venture upon the subject of the present war, yet he could not help but notice how all classes were combining, irrespective of rank, in order to work for their country's success, whether their labour was at home or on the battlefield. This gospel of equality was the burden of the Master's mes- sage. He lived it in. His daily life, by examples of love and sympathy, and it was for men to-day to catch His spirit and, in their daily lives, to assist in carrying on the Master's glorious work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>Christian Influence.</head>
        <p>Christian Influence.</p>
        <p>In the afternoon the scholars assembled at the Mission Church and mtirched in procession to. the Bold-street Church to partake in a musical service which was .presided over by Mr. T. Dale, senior superintendent, who, in a, short address, emphasised the importance 'of the work of the teacher in the Sunday school, a work which was not one for material honour, but a labour of love. There was nothing he, desired better nowadays than to see the children brought under the influence of Christian training, it was a pleasing feature to, note, at the present time, the large number of homes in which the Word of God .was read by the parents of the children. It would have a great influence upon the young life of the future. They wanted their Sabbath schools to be the most precious place of resort in the land to-day; and it was the special work of the teacher to be able to make it such. The special speaker was Miss Jessie Reid Crosbie, of the Liverpool Primary Sunday School Union, who gave a very capable and earnest address on the • . word "soldier." Dividing the word into letters, and speaking' principally to the children, Miss Crosbie riveted their attention by the manner in which she illustrated her topic with short up-to-date stories, and as, on her last visit, she showed the adult portion of her audience the methods adopted by the primary schools in better enabling the children to grasp the meaning of the lessons taught in the Sunday school. In addition to musical items rendered by, the children, the choir gave a / splendid rendition of :the anthem. "Sing, 0 Heavens," and also assisted in the chorus ,to the solo "The Words of Life," which was sweetly sung by Miss Eva Tanner.</p>
        <p>• There was a large congregation at the evening service, at which Mr. Deakin delivered an eloquent sermon based on the text 2 Kings, vii.; 17.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>Splendid_ Singing and Collections.</head>
        <p>Splendid_ Singing and Collections.</p>
        <p>The singing throughout the day was well sus- tained by the ehildren and choir,. for which great praise is due to the conductor, Mr. F. Atkinson; Mr. T. Tanner ably assisting at the organ.</p>
        <p>The collections for the day amounted to £l6 17s. 2d.</p>
        <p>At the close of the evening service Mr. A. Tilling (Mission secretary) expressed the thanks of the Mission workers to the trustees of Bold- street Church for their courtesy hi allowing the use of the church for the services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>NO RATE INCREASE AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>NO RATE INCREASE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Urban Council at a meeting on Tuesday-, fixed the general district rate at Is. 3d. in the pound, which is the same amount as last year. Mr. unison said he thought it would be an agreeable surprise to ratepayers to find there was no increase. The Council bail not beeti guilty of any faltie economy, and had just granted a war bonus to its employes equal to a penny rate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>BIG BROTHER'S B. 11) LEAD.</head>
        <p>BIG BROTHER'S B. 11) LEAD.</p>
        <p>Police Court Story of Abused Trust.</p>
        <p>Before Councillor Henry Roberts (in the chair), and Messrs. J. Rymer Young, C. Jackson Holmes, and T. Locker, at the Warrington Police Court •on Tuesday, John William Cross (19) and his young brother, James Arthur Cross (12), of 75, Orford-street, were charged together with stealing from a dwelling-house known as " The ' Poplars," situated in Heaton's Yard, some time during the past four months, various articles—a savings bank home safe, a lady's 18- carat gold ring, a pocket-knife, two pairs of opera glasses, six cut-glass wine glasses, three table knives, one fountaiu pen, and ,t.:6 in cash, together of the value of £l2 12s. Gd., the pro- perty of Annie Jane Heaton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>Thefts Admitted.</head>
        <p>The Thefts Admitted.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said that articles were missed by the prosecutrix for some time. In- quiries were made. and on April 7th the house was locked up at two o'clock. On returning at 4.30, prosecutrix found that a cruet had been stolen from the sideboard. She was advised by the police to make a thorough search, to ascer- tain whether any other property of hers had been stolen. It was then found that the articles mentioned in the chatige were missing. The lads had been in the habit of visiting the house for the purpose of doing small jobs, for which they were paid. Taxed with the thefts. the lads denied any knowledge of the matter, but later, when charged at the Police Station, they. ad- mitted having stolen the articles. Addressing the senior prisoner, the Chairman said: "There's no excuse 'or you. You are old enough to know better. The excuse has been made for you that you have been allowed to have your , own 'sweet will,' but it is a poor excuse. You have abused your opportunities, and will be fined A:2, or in default of payment you will go to Prison for two months' bard labour."</p>
        <p>The other prisoner was ordered to be birched</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>AUEING RAPIDLY.</head>
        <p>AUEING RAPIDLY.</p>
        <p>Prisoner Who Was 32 Six Months Ago • Says lie is 60. Now.</p>
        <p>At the. Borough. Police Court On Saturday, before Mr. R. T. Fairclofigh (in the chair), Alderman Tinnion and. Messrs. A. J. Jolley, T. Pemberton and W. Furness, Win. Walker, a miller, and Edward George Skedgell, a labourer, were charged with wandering abroad in Wilderspool-road without visible .means of sup- port.—They both admitted the offence. Acting-Sergeant Hayes said that at 10.35 on Friday morning, he was in - Arpley- road when he received information from a pawnbroker that Skedgell had been. in his • shop and had offered in pledge a , bracelet., The paw n broker examined the bracelet and found that it was made of brass with a gold coating, and was valued at about Id. A small ticket attached to the bracelet described it as coated with 22 carat rolled gold. Witness followed the men and asked them about the bracelet. Walker produced- it and handed it over to witness, saying that he bought it a an auction in Macclesfield, over • a week pre- viously. - Witness noticed that both prisoners were destitute—that they had no visible means of subsistence—and upon them failing to give a satisfactory account of themselves he took them into custody. . Walker repeated to the Bench that le bought the bracelet in Macclesfield and that,• conse- quently, he had a right to try to pawn it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0265" n="art0265">
        <head>Why He Didn't Work.</head>
        <p>Why He Didn't Work.</p>
        <p>Skedgell said he was an epileptic Subject, and that that was the reason why he was out of work. "I get my living 'where I am able" he added "and I have no fixed place of abode—no home to go to—and consequently I have to wander about the country. I came from Totnes, in Devonshire." The Chief Constable told the Bench that when the men were brought into the Police Station they told different stories. One said that he only met the other the morning they were arrested, and the other said they came across each other a week ago and had been wandering about together since then, sleeping in PreScot and other Workhouses. One admitted a three months' sentence at Exeter, for felony, and that since finishing his time he had been wandering about the country. There was no need for them to do go; there was plenty of work to be had, and one of the men at least, said he had au difficulty in finding a job had he wanted one. Even the errand boys in Warrington were giving up running errands and going into the works, where they earned as much as 18s. and V 1 a week, girls taking their places in the shops,</p>
        <p>Skedgell- said he was formerly in the Royal Navy, but was discharged on account of epilepsy, lie was proceeding to make a further statement when,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>Ple.nty of Work for Willing Mon.</head>
        <p>Ple.nty of Work for Willing Mon.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said that file man who was talking so much was sentenced at that Court to three months' hard labour on November 7th last for begging, after he had "clacked " so much that he had persuaded the Bench to adjourn the case to see whether he could enlist. lie couldn't! lie then gave his age as 32, and now he said he was 60, so he had aged wonder- fully in a very few months. (Laughter.) Skedgell was sent to prison for 21 days' hard labour, and the other man for 11 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
        <head>MIS-SPENT SEPARATION PAY.</head>
        <p>MIS-SPENT SEPARATION PAY.</p>
        <p>How Soldier's Wife Neoleeled tier Home For Drink.</p>
        <p>"NOT FAIR TO YOUR HUSBAND."</p>
        <p>Two cases illustrative of the sad effect of intemperate drinking on home life were heard at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday, when two women—one the wife of a soldier—were accused, of neglecting. their children.</p>
        <p>HUSBAND'S EMPLOYERS' GENEROSITY. Annie Stark, of 8, Robert-street, was sum- moned in respect of her three children. Mr. Browne, prosecuting for the N.S.P.C.C., said hat defendant's husband was in the Army, and defendant was in receipt of an allowance Of 235. per week. The ease .came under .the notice of the N.S.P.C.C. in October last year, when defendant was warned about her drinking habits. She said at the time that she had had one drink that morning as there had been a wedding in the street, and that she would mend her ways. Mr. Wainwright, the society's in- speetor, Visited Messrs. Crosfields', where the defendant's husband had been employed prior to his joining the Army, and the firm gave him .Cl, which enabled defendant to get out of pawn the various household articles she had pledged. However, she returned to her old ways, re- pawned the articles and was found helplessly drunk in her house.</p>
        <p>Miss Sullivan, one of the borough. health nurses, spoke to visiting the house in Robert- street and finding the children in a very neg- lected condition. There was only one blanket on the bed, and defendant said she had pawned the other to get food.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>Found Mother and Another Woman Drunk.</head>
        <p>Found Mother and Another Woman Drunk.</p>
        <p>inspector Wainwright, N.S.P.C.C., said he went to the house and found the defendant and another wothan drunk. She could do better, f she liked. 'Drink was the 'whole trouble. Defendant was originally receiving 2s. a week from Messrs. Crosfield's. but this allowance was stopped n account of her drinking habits. Defeudaat rade a stutemeut, iu fue course of</p>
        <p>which she tearfully pleaded that if she were given another chance she would "never do it again !" _ -</p>
        <p>An Undesirable Companion.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable informed the Bench that the other woman mentioned by inspector Wain- wright was more to blame .for the defendant's relapse than was the defendant herself. They (the police) had had numerous complaints about the woman in question who was a very trouble- some individual and • a most undesirable com- panion for defendant. She also, was a soldier's wife. On the previous Monday the two women drew between them thirty-three shillings, and by the Tuesday evening there was not a penny left, owing to their drinking, if defendant was not careful she would lose her grant. The Bench adjourned the case for four weeks to give defendant a chance of reforming, the Chairman (Councillor Henry -Roberts) inform-</p>
        <p>ing flefendant that if any fresh complaints arose as to her conduct she might expect severe treat- ment "It is not 'fair to your husband," the Chairman concluded.</p>
        <p>LABOURER'S NEGLECTFUL WIFE. From_ Pawnshop to Public-house.</p>
        <p>The :-eeond ease was that of Clara Sharp, of 51, Turner-street.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne stated that in this case the chil- dren were very dirty and on several occasions had had to be sent home from school, as they were unfit to associate with ether children, so verminous were they. Defendant's husband was a labourer and give his wife LI a week, in addition to taking in food and things himself. Tie said that if he gave her more than SA she would only spend it on drink. Jle brought in a pair of clogs for the little girl; aml defendant pawned them at the first opportunity. Miss Phillips, a nurse under the Borough Council, gave evidence as to the condition of the children when they visited the clinic _ _</p>
        <p>Defendant was repentant and promised to amend.</p>
        <p>Given a Chance to Keep Her Word</p>
        <p>The Chairman, stating that the case would be adjourned for four weeks, told defendant that the Bench were taking that course in order to give her an opportunity to Carry out what was her duty and what she had promised to carry out. If there was not a very great improve- ment in the condition of the children at the end of the specified period, they would be taken away from her and she would be put in prison.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0269" n="art0269">
        <head>DRUNKEN HUSBAND.</head>
        <p>A DRUNKEN HUSBAND.</p>
        <p>XX ire Gels a Separation and l'en Shillings a AVeek.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Friday morning, before Mr. S. Wright and other Magistrates, Bernard Lyon, of 21, Old-road, was summoned by his wife, Bridget, who sought a separation from him on the ground that he was an habitual drunkard. The wife told the Court that her husband got drunk nearly every night and "came home</p>
        <p>_ . cursing and swearing." A week last Saturday, whilst she was picking up the children's clogs, she suddenly looked round and saw him ruh- ning at her with a knife in his hand. Recently Ito gave her such a bad kick on the shins that she had to go to the Infirmary, and could not walk for six weeks. The husband said that it was impossible for him to live with his wife; she acted like a woman who was not right when she got into a temper. -</p>
        <p>The Bench granted the wife a separation and ordered her husband to pay her 103. a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
        <head>FRIENDLY SOCIETIES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY SOCIETIES.</p>
        <p>FORESTERS ELECT THEIR HIGH COURT DELEGATE.</p>
        <p>The quarterly district meeting of the War- rington District of the Ancient Order of Fores- ters was held at the horse and Jockey Inn, Martinscroft, on Saturday, the following being present: Councillor Bro. J. Chrimes (in the chair), Bro. B. IL Hollins (in the vice-chair), the Mayor (Alderman P. Peacock), district treasurer; Bros. J. Walsh, H. James and L. Davies, district trustees; together with the undermentioned delegates: Court 138, Bros. W. Hipkiss and A. Morris; Court 210, Bros: G. W. R. Baines, J. Davies and U. Allen; Court 430, Bros. J. tiregory, E. Clayton and H. James, Jun. ;' Court 431, Bros. j. Smith and J. W. Perdue; Court 461, Bro. J. Gatley; Court 475, Bro. J. tiregson; Court 0335, Sisters lt Baines and A. Corradine; banner keepers, -Bros. J. Jeans anti P. Wilding, semi. There were also present Bros. S. B. Healey, 11. Perdue and H. Stafford.</p>
        <p>- The quarterly return of members, together with the cash account, was submitted and passed. Sister Corradine gave the auditors' report, which was confirmed by Bro. Jones, .and con- sidered highly satisfactory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0271" n="art0271">
        <head>Fighting Members' Dues to be Met.</head>
        <p>Fighting Members' Dues to be Met.</p>
        <p>The question of the members serving at the Front was next considered, and it was resolved that the district officers meet all dues and de- mands made by the Executive Council on behalf of members who have joined the colours, which the Courts would loyally support. _</p>
        <p>Councillor Bro. J. Clirimes- was unanimously elected as the delegate to represent the War- Tington district at the High Court meeting, which will be held at Oxford during the first week in August next. Bro. Chrimes thanked the delegates for the honour they had conferred upon him, and assured all present that he would return with a faithful account of his stewardship. Several other matters of 'minor importance were discussed, after which the D.C.R. closed the meeting with the customary ritual.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0272" n="art0272">
        <head>SIR EI)W.‘111) (MIA'S RETURN.</head>
        <p>SIR EI)W.‘111) (MIA'S RETURN.</p>
        <p>Sir Edward Grey returned to duty on Mon- day and was busy all day at the Fureign Office. lie has had ten days' holiday, or only about half the period origivally suggested us pro- bable.</p>
        <p>During his absence Mr. Asquith has had charge of his department, and has been in almost daily attendance at the l'oreien Office. Happily, Sir Edward is in excellent health.</p>
