      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0274" n="art0274">
        <head>WARRINGTON FREE CHURCH COUNCIL.,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON FREE CHURCH COUNCIL.,</p>
        <p>To the Members of the FrO Churches of Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the Warringtet, Free Church Council, reference was made 1, the trying and difficult days through which "lr, churches are passing by reason of this °el' and the opinion expresged. that words .41' encouragement and suggestion from the C 0011;,! as a representative body might be of servic.e the moment. This international crisis testing time, not merely for the nation, hilt ie for our churches and our faith. We need ask that we may be given a faith great ell°lll',r for the occasion. And we need to remind "4 selves of those features of the situation Iyhti. should appeal to us as giving cause for tude. We would therefore venture to tiddrt; the members of our churches with regard 4 some questions of immediate and vital movie'</p>
        <p>RtSPONSE TO DUTY'S CALI' the We must all be profoundly grateful for .uf magnificent response which has been made "o the manhood of our Free Churches to the of their country in the hour of•need. We ha 0 as yet no 'actual figures froth/ which to ro,riltihe full total for the town of Warrington 0 Free Churchmen, who are engaged on sersl,-; but from all our churches a. goodly norti-0 have gone forth. We would suggest that go should forward to the Secretary of the C00°,51 the number connected with your own chu' hie who have thus left you, so that we may be to announce the Free Church total for " whole town. •</p>
        <p>11.—THE CALL TO PRAYER. Amidst all the tragedy of this gi'l3 catastrophe, we must be thankful that iqb'e re-called us to the reality of prayer. ,be monthly intercession service arranged by `b. Council, which has been held regularly throegoi out the war, will be continued to the close 0 the conflict. Arrangements have now bee,,ii made for the service to be held each montibtio the. second Thursday evening, at 7.45, in 'do Bold-street ,Wesleyan Church. We should to well to make this service thoroughly knololibo those of our members who have relatives at Front, for to them it should be particularo.b: helpful occasion. And we trust that it unq a gathering place for large numbers of ChristOd men and women of our churches, where we.° together practise the presence of God..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0275" n="art0275">
        <head>111.-REMEMBER THE ABSENT; COMF°III</head>
        <p>111.-REMEMBER THE ABSENT; COMF°III</p>
        <p>THOSE THAT ARE LEFT. Band in hand with the call to prayer Ow, many fresh opportunities for Christian ser'',l'll We shall all se that every step is taken to Irft, up regular communication with those who away. It means much to the young fellows have gone from our churches and Stl schools to feel that they 'are not forgot-fis Such remembrance will bring cheer I'4 encouragement to them in their hard and-110'in task, will bind them more closely to their 070 church, and ensure their re-consecration to iT service whvn they come home again. The (fey stant visitation of the homes from which tuer. have gone offers a unique opening for the cise of the gift of friendship and Christl'i sympathy to the anxious and the soli stricken, and such opportunity will be reou embraced.</p>
        <p>IV.—CLUB ROOMS SUGGESTED. A We would offer a suggehtion which we thiP" is worthy of the consideration of the „ Churches. We have in our midst a large 110 her of the wives of those who have gone to t,,0 Front. With the absence of the husband tvo whole character of their home life has befo altered, and very many, especially those o'3 have no family ties to engage their thought 011. f activities, must be feeling the lonelines their altered life. Would it not be a wortPJ thing on the part of the Free Churches to 13.1r°:0 vide in some of our buildings something ofoii character of a club at which refreshment collfrot be provided at a nominal cost, and some fre of instruction and entertainment organiseo. We should like to elicit the opinion of the le 0 hers of our churches as to whether such thing is desirable, and if so what practio support the churches would give.</p>
        <p>V.—THE DRINK PERIL.' ,40 An emphatic duty is laid upon our eburcout, to Speak in no uncertain manner about tol necessity for immediate legislation of a nationt character with regard to a substantial curfoig ment of the hours of sale of intoxicatLii;t liquors in war time. Something beyond witr has been. effected by the present military or%, we deem to be necessary.. At all times toi excesses inseparable from the consumption intoxicants, are amongst the chief hindrant.:„3 to the establishment of the Kingdom of Chr'l in Ms world, but in times of war, tile excesses are not only a stumbling-block in p way of the progress of Christianity, but 1114/ grave national danger.</p>
        <p>VI.—THE MARTYRDOM OF BELGIUM' 1 Amongst the possibilities of practic:f Christian service at this time is that 00 rendering succour to the members of 1,1. Belgian race, who in the early days of the took upon themselves the world's burtletij Already some of our churches have prostlper homes for some of the fugitives. We lOU' t stand that a grant in aid is available annoilo jag, we 'believe, to a third of the cire for any of our churches that des,"r to participate in the joy of this P-ho vice. We would therefore ask those chorre where nothing of this nature has yet been to consider the possibility of housing BelVe. refugees, either ,by instituting separate 110 or by arranging for their people to take °neat two Belgians into their own homes. Such 14'id has the, blessing and sanction of Him who t "Inasmuch as ye have done it unto the lv"- of these, ye have done it unto Me." hee In the midst of the strain and stress of trying (lays, days, we trust that the Free. Churchel:o our community may be centres of unbry' faith and loyal service. • , Yours very sincerely, T. T. JAmss, President- OUDEN, Secretary. C. E. PAUKER, • TreaSTirercl2 On behalf of the .Warrington Free Cilu Council. March, 1915.</p>
      </div>
