      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0277" n="art0277">
        <head>LIVING AND THE DEAD</head>
        <p>THE LIVING AND THE DEAD</p>
        <p>Vital Statistics For The Runcorn</p>
        <p>Rural District.</p>
        <p>The. annual health report for the Runcorn Rural District has just- been issued. De: loseph kdams, the. medical'oflicer of health for the dis- trict—who, by the way, i;cas mobilised in August with the battalion of, the Territorial Vorce to which he belongs, and was in conse- quence removed from the sphere of his duties inder the Council—gives some very interesting Information.</p>
        <p>POPULATION INCREASING. „The population has continued to increase, "ough not quite at the rate at which it ad- vanced from the ,time of the 1901 census till 11. During that period the figures rose from 2,3,243 to 28,216—an average of 500 per annum. 'Jr. Adams 'estimates that the 'population half- way through 1914 was 29,170, so that in three -Vears the increase was less than a thousand. uurimi the year 1914, 581 births and 340 deaths were registered 'aS having occurred within the district—compared with. an average for the pre- d,ing five years of 624 births and 325 deaths. • coroner's inquests were held in 22 'cases dur- lag the year. The causes of death, as ascer- lained by these inquiries, were, briefly, as fol- lows: Natural causes 7, shock from falls 3, .con- (mission of brain 1, suicide by drowning 2, in- lluieei from fall 1, ,aecidentally. run over .1, suffo- cation in bed (overlain) 1, fractured skull 1, &amp;Aecidentally drowned 4, injury to head 1.</p>
        <p>' MALE AND FEMALE MORTALITY. Of the 573 births, 268 were those of males, and 405 were those of females; they were distri- '7buted as fid_lowS : Runcorn . sulklistrict, 54 luales, 46 .females; • Frodsbam . sub-district, . 94 ra ales, -107 females; liiutwortli sub-district, 120 ' Males, 152 females. Of the gross total of 340 registered deaths, 180 were those of males and 160 were those of females. The birth-rate for the paotpeu loaf 'holedist•ria during the year 1.914 was at the 19.6 trict ettl7. per 1,000 per annum of estimated The 'death-rate . for the whole dis 11.2 was T Per 1,000 111-; the as fnolill Year 1914 w `lt. the rate of hon. he t tPer annum of 'es'ti'mated popula- hated registered deaths were distri- Males, 59 f °ws : IZuneorn sub-district, •62 males, 24 .felllales; Frodsham sub-district, 32 males, 77 f emales; Budworth sub-district, 86 females. entitles. Totals, 180 males and 160</p>
        <p>DEADLY CANCER. Tubercular diseases caused nineteen deaths, cancer twenty-two, and bronchitis thirty-two. .I‘.lo fatalities were attributed to cirrhosis of the wer (a complaint generally due to alcoholism), 'lnd only two deaths from alcoholism were re- Ported.</p>
        <p>Violent deaths—excluding suicideS—nlimbere thirteen. There were. three suicides.</p>
        <p>VAIN! ‘Nt El. CHURCH AIESTRY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0278" n="art0278">
        <head>Balance in Hand Despile Hem ier</head>
        <p>A Balance in Hand Despile Hem ier</p>
        <p>Calls.</p>
        <p>'The annual vestry- meeting in connection with the Emmanuel Reformed Episcopal 'Church, Warrington, waB held on Thursday ght of last week, in the Institute, Knutsford- 'road. The attendance was not up to the aver- e of previous years. The Incumbent (the 4ver. W. T. Jaye) presided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0279" n="art0279">
        <head>Balance on the Right Side.</head>
        <p>A Balance on the Right Side.</p>
        <p>- —•--- - The church \Nardens' accounts were presented 1?3, Mr. Robert Lees, sen., and showed a balance in hand of 01.11 ss. Bd. Mr. Lees expressed his Pleasure in being able to report that, notwith- '*.anding heavier calls than usual upon the th. arch hinds, he could show a balance on the light side.. Had circumstances been normal, lere would have been a ,balance of at least •t3O,</p>
        <p>v.The meeting accorded a hearty vote of ks to Mr. Lees for his services during the .Year.</p>
        <p>The Select Vestry.</p>
        <p>select vestry for the ensuing year was f3tituted as follows:. Minister's warden, Mr. qt,t• Atherton (who again kindly accepted office he 'invitation of the Incumbent); people's IP 'len. -Mr. E. Lees, sen.; synodsmen, Messrs. R. Jowett and F. Lloyd.; vestrymen, Messrs. 11'4. Armstrong, J. Bibby, A: Dickens, E. J. zlidder, 11., Heald, H. Johnson, T. Lloyd and fl Moon.</p>
        <p>The incumbent's Thanks and Advice. ~rrhe Rev. W. 'P. Jaye,. in thanking the con- V‘,egation for their- continued oval support; that he was sure they had nothing to fear l'3,ng as they fulfilled their special mission ttna",e :town in a spirit of Christian charity relied Upon God for all their needs. The f2tl3,lllillg months might prove to be very difficult .faiall churches of Christ, but faith must never it.eh_ter• • .During the. past. year, lie was happy to 0 the that .two new churches had been added .Reformed Episcopal. ,Church, which arlY Showed that general progress was being tame.</p>
        <p>The Need of a Spring Tonic.</p>
        <p>- •Why at this season of the year, when we are emerging from the long, dull, winter months do hosts of people feel the want of t"ic ? Largely because the digestive „ ..Y§terri (Stomach, .• liver,. •and bowels) has ?ecoine rundown and weakened. If you Pave this feeling of "seediness." these .I.niportant en organs need a -little bracing up . aKt, ‘with 'l 3'u ypened appetite,' and to gain proper ou to Shake off lethargy, to eat ma,tter nourishtnent from your food: The Nvhole is proved solved by the choice of a true Tl.es W tive remedY. Well, during nearly half century, Mother Seig el's Syrup has been tens of thousands to be the ideal •4 Pr..eparat by ion for ensuring good digestion on t,,hicli good health principally depends. By slAendid record of benefits imparted to th.flose sufferin“' from digestive disturbances, severe, it •has won and kept .th.re gl,"oL , layout.. So .be sure you get Mottn. :vlifers SYrup Wh4 you ask for it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0280" n="art0280">
        <head>FIELD OF SPORT.</head>
        <p>FIELD OF SPORT.</p>
        <p>Sound Drubbing From Weakened Wigan Team.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0281" n="art0281">
        <head>WAR-TIME BOWLING.</head>
        <p>WAR-TIME BOWLING.</p>
        <p>‘mateur Association Bold Their Annual Meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0282" n="art0282">
        <head>EW SECRETARY ELECTED.</head>
        <p>EW SECRETARY ELECTED.</p>
        <p>• Although he effect of the war is bound to he felt—because, for one thing there will be fewer players (so many having gone to the Front) and for another thing less inclination and opportunity to indulge in the game, on the part of those who remain behind—the Warrington and District Amateur Bowling Association are nevertheless looking forward to a successful and, on the whole, enjoyable season and to that end, at their annual meeting at the Pelican Hotel, Buttermarket-street on Tuesday night, made the usual preliminary preparations. There was a good attendance of delegates from the affiliated clubs, and 'Mr. Horrahin presided.</p>
        <p>YEAR FINISHES WITH A BALANCE ON THE RIGHT SIDE. The first business was the passing of the accounts. They showed a total income of £5O 3s. 11d., which was mainly made up of entrance fees £l6 10s., subscriptions £l5 15s. and handicap fees £l2 16s. There was a balance in hand at. the close of the year of 15s. Id., the main items of expenditure being .£l2 18s. for prizes for the handicap, and £2O for prizes in the other bowl- ing events held ,_under the auspices of the</p>
        <p>association. Mr. •Geo. F. Freeman, the hon. treasurer, mentioned that the association had been rather lucky in receiving a handsome donation from Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co.</p>
        <p>WAR FUNDS AND THE FINANCES. The Chairman mentioned that it had been found in canvassing for subscriptions for the ensuing season; that there were a number of people who could ilot see their way under the circumstances to, as they put it, dip their hands in their pockets all the time, in view of the fact that in these days there were more needful directions in which their subscriptions were sought; viz., the war funds. However, the association had come out very well indeed, all things considered.</p>
        <p>A LARGER BALANCE IN HAND. The auditors (Messrs. J. Newton and A. E. Burdett) reported that it was pleasing to note that the balance carried forward was slightly larger than it was last season, though, on the other hand, the subscription list revealed a con- siderable falling off. Indeed, the income showed a decrease of 4s. Bd., notwithstanding a handsome donation of tlO 10s. from Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co. The auditors wete accorded a hearty vote of thanks for their services.</p>
        <p>WAR CAUSES A RESIGNATION. A letter was read from the Earlestown Viaduct Institute resigning membership of the association. "Our club," said the letter, "are not taking part in the Warrington Association this year. . . . owing !to a number of our mem- bers having gone on military service." At the same time, with the secretary of the club, the members were desirous of placing on record their opinion that the officials were "very fair, sportsmanlike, businesslike and a credit to the game of bowls."</p>
        <p>SMALLER TEAMS. Mr. Bowers, remarking that many of the clubs have a lot of their members at the Front, or in training for active service, proposed that the association revert to :teams of .12 aside instead of 14. This was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0283" n="art0283">
        <head>CONSOLATION COMPETITION TO BE AGAIN HELD.</head>
        <p>CONSOLATION COMPETITION TO BE AGAIN HELD.</p>
        <p>For some time the meeting debafed as to whether or not it was advisable to run aCon-- solation handicap this year. The lion. Treasurer was of the opinion that there might not be sufficient subscriptions to provide prizes for a consolation handicap; in- deed, it was possible that all the money to pro- vide the prizes in the cup competition would have to be found out of the entrance fees only. The people who usually provided the funds for the prizes had other directions in .which to spend their money in times like the present. Consequently if the association decided on "a. knock-out," he thought that would be quite sufficient; all the players had to "go at" being the entrance fees, the prizes being made up out of 'these, plus, perhaps, one or two sub- scriptions. It was pointed out that last year the entrance fees totalled £l6 10s., which should suffice for Prizes, under all the circumstances; the lion. Treasurer pointing out that, in his opinion, it would be unpatriotic to ask people to subscribe towards the prize funds when their pockets were being already "picked to pieces" through the war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0284" n="art0284">
        <head>FOR THE LOVE OF THE GAME-NOT THE</head>
        <p>FOR THE LOVE OF THE GAME-NOT THE</p>
        <p>PRIZE! A .Member "certainly supported the idea," and said that bowlers did not go in for the com- petitions for the monetary value of the prizes, but for the love of the game. The Chairman said that it appeared to him that it would be a case of trusting to the entrance fees for the prizes this year. Eventually Mr Garnett proposed that a con- solation competition be run this year, as hereto- fore, Mr Ashton seconding. Mr. Hamilton moved as a direct negative that they have no consolation competition this year, and Mr. Watson seconded. The original proposition, carried. Messrs. Bowers, Russell. Whittle, Lawson, Watson, Garnett and Monks were appointed a special committee to go into the question of the rules and report thereon.</p>
        <p>however, was</p>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD RE-ELECTED PRESIDENT. Mr. A. H. Crosfield was unanimously, and with applause, re-elected president of the association. Mr. J. J. Whitley, of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., was added to the list of patrons.</p>
        <p>ORNAMENTAL VICE-PRESIDENTS. The selection of vice-presidents was left to the special sub•committee, the Hon. Treasurer re- marking that the list at present contained "names with ` Esquire ' to them, and the holders of which were as much ' Esquires ' as were threepenny `•bits.' " (Laughter.) It was ridiculous to have on the list vice-presidents who never came near the association's fixtures, and who never .subscribed. "Yet their names appeared as if they were,valuable institutions." The Chairman: A cheap advertisement! (Laughter.) • The names of Messrs. Russell and J. arle were added to the list of vice-presidents to be gone through :by the sub-committee.</p>
        <p>NEW CHAIRMAN—AND OTHERS. , Mr. .Fred Dean was chosen chairman of the council, in place of Mr. Geo. Ball, who was thanked for his past services in that capacity, it being mentioned that he had been chairman for a number of years, and had been a good chairman, but was now unable to give the time he Would like to do to the duties, owing to the nature an 4 .hours of his work, which pre- vented him attending meetings of the council. Mr. T. Garnett was elected vice-chairman. Mr. G. F. Freeman was re-appointed hon. treasurer. Mr. J. Wood resigned the office of hon. secretary owing to the fact that he is now engaged in business in Manchester. -lle was eulogised by the Chairman and accorded the heartiest possible thanks for his past services, and assured the members that he would con- tinue to do all he could •for the Association, in which he was as deeply interested- as ever. Mr. IL Whittle, whose address is 9, Fennell- street, was chosen secretary vice Mr. Wood, and was voted the usual honorarium, payable at the termination of his first year of office. Messrs. Bardett and ~Newton were. re-elected hop. anclitori; .</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 17, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0285" n="art0285">
        <head>TELLING WORK BY THE WINNING FORWARDS.</head>
        <p>TELLING WORK BY THE WINNING FORWARDS.</p>
        <p>Warrington fulfilled an overdue fixture with Wigan on Monday and were badly worsted; indeed, they got their second best—or should not it be worst?—beating of the season. The Wiganites had the game in hand—it was on their own ground !—from first to last, and that notwithstanding the fact that. even they played indifferent football. But, if their form was but mediocre, what shall one say of War- rington's? Indifferent is a mild word to use in describing it, and to say that it was de- cidedly "off" is but tamely expressing it, for it was really deplorably weak and somewhere near the worst they have exhibited this season. Had Wigan shown the least exertion they could with comparative ease have won by twice the _margin of . points that actually divi4ed the teams at the close, but when they fountl that they had the game well in hand they were content to let it Test at a balance of 24 points to the good, the score reading : Wigan, 35 points; Warrington, 11 points. WIGAN TOT UP THEIR BIG TOTAL. The game was only a few minutes old when Coldrick opened the scoring for Wigan with a positively delightful run, and one in which the crowd—neither a large nor an over-enthu- siastic one—revelled. Tries by Francis and Guy followed quickly on the opening points, the last-named player beating several players in most tricky fashion before forcing his way over the line for what was, perhaps, the try of the match. Another brilliant try was scored by Francis, who raced half the length of - the field before he crossed the line: with a breathless ,pack at his heels. Francis set the seal of complete su4ess on his effort by also adding the goal points. Of course, Warrington: were by no means idle whilst all this score was being piled up against them; indeed, to them credit they really made some fine efforts, but unfortunately they were but spasmodic and nearly always lacked that finish without which success is impossible. Their forwards seemed to be too easily discouraged; altogether too irresolute, and lacking in resource when placed, in tight corners. Warrington's tries came per Fearnley, who was what George Robey would describe as a " twicer." His first try Was a dainty piece of work and com- pelled the plaudits of the home supporters, and his second, too, was thoroughly workmanlike. T. Cunliffe also scored a "merry and bright" try, and Taberner landed a goal with a hefty and ,well-directed kick. Coldrick put in two successive tries for Wigan and Seddon im- proved Upon one of them, whilst, later on, he himself got over the line, Francis and Hay- ward registering yet further tries. WARRINGTON'S REPREHENSIBLE . WHACKING. Warrington's defeat. by So big tv margin is all the more reprehensible—just as Wigan's victory . was all the more glorious—from the fact that the home team were only able to field: a weak side. Two or three of their regular men were missing from their ranks, and yet their scratch thirteen was always superior to .the 'Warrington combination. Where the home team scored .most was in their forward line, which was immeasurably superior to the front rank opposed to it. The home sextet not only controlled the scrummages pretty much as they liked, but they were also miles ahead of the opposition when it came to work in the loose. They were so persistent in their efforts that they actually scored seven of the. nine tries with which their side was accredited. Seddon was superb at full-back, while the Warrington "last- liner" appeared to be quite off colour and allowed sothe "soft things" to slip by him. The visiting backs. were perhaps the weakest part of a not over-strong team, but the whole team put up only a mediocre fight, their only redeeming feature being the fine open work of their for- wards, of whom Fearnley and the two Cunliffes were splendid.</p>
        <p>Notes by " Centre."</p>
        <p>they took advantage; a mistake for which the "Saints" have little to thank their "last hope." However, Roberts was by no means a bad asset to his side, his long screw-kicks and his plucky saves from the very feet of his op- ponents going far to redeem his one big mis- take; as, also, to compensate for his somewhat slow fielding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0286" n="art0286">
        <head>EVEN PACK-WORK.</head>
        <p>EVEN PACK-WORK.</p>
        <p>The packs, like the score—which; by the way, was 5 points each—were evenly matched, and, really, much the same may be said with truth of the other departments, for what one lot lacked in speed the corresponding set on the other side, generally speaking, made up for in trickiness and perseverance. If one were called 'upon to choose between the rival three- quarter lines he might, perhaps, feel disposed to award the palm to the " Sainte " quar- tet, for they never flagged in their efforts and lasted noticeably better than their opponents.. Prudence, however, must be left outside the scope of that sweeping statement, for to the bitter end he was always to be found where the fray was thickest, and lie was, indeed, an outshining light. The " Saints' " strongest ' department was their half-back line, Creevey and Trenwith .flatly refusing to give a single advantage away. On the other side E. W. Jones was his old self, but Schofield, although ' lie initiated the movement that gave the Hornets their try, was unaccountably weak. The arrangements for tho big game—and the big "gate" which it drew—were most admirably made by the Warrington Secretary (Mr. T. P. Bradshaw) and his committee, everything going off without a hitch. As one gazed upon the serried rows of faces surrounding the playing pitch and noticed the spacious, and well-built stands he could not help thinking that Wilderspool is almost an ideal venue for a 1 fixture of the kind. THE MAYOR'S ABSENCE. .</p>
        <p>His Worship the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) has written to Mr. T. P. Bradshaw, hon. secretary of the Warrington Football Club, stating that he intended tb be present at the semi-final on the Warrington ground last Saturday, but friendly society duties unavoid- ably prevented him, the quarterly meeting of the Independent Order of Foresters, of which he is district treasurer, having been fixed foz that day. _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0287" n="art0287">
        <head>RE-PLAY.</head>
        <p>THE RE-PLAY.</p>
        <p>At a Northern Union meeting in Manchester on Tuesday night it was decided that the Cup semi-final re-play between St. Helens and Rochdale Hornets should take place at• Wigan on Saturday, April 24th, should Rochdale be beaten in the League semi-final at Hudders- field to-day. If Rochdale win at Huddersfield the cup semi-final to be re-played at Wigan on Wednesday,• April 21st. It was definitely fixed that the 'cup final should be played at Oldham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0288" n="art0288">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S OFF DAY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S OFF DAY.</p>
        <p>Warrington have another off day to-day owing to the re-shuffling of.games to accommo- date Rochdale and St. Helens. The match Withies v. Warrington, at Widnes, which had been re-arranged to be played to-day, has been further postponed by the League Committee to next Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0289" n="art0289">
        <head>SCHOOLBOYS " SOCCER" LEAGUE. St. James' Again Win The Championship.</head>
        <p>SCHOOLBOYS " SOCCER" LEAGUE. St. James' Again Win The Championship.</p>
        <p>The shield and medals in connection with the Warrington and District Teachers' Asso- ciation Schoolboys' Association League were presented to the- St. James' School team, yes- terday by the Rev. H. B. Firth. This is the second year in- succession that St. games' have been School Association champions. Last year they were both Rugby and 'Association cham-</p>
        <p>;iions, and they haven fair .chance of repeat- ng the performance this year, being unde- eatg4l as yet in the Rugby • League. The team's Association record is as follows :</p>
        <p>Played 17, won 16, lost 1; goals—for 57, .gainst 12; total points 12. . _ . . .</p>
        <p>- - - The - following ht. James' boys received medals: Frank Plumb (captain), Ralph .Bibby, Joseph Hough, David Morrison, John L. Munn, George Kershaw, Tom Williams, Osmond Thomas, John Moore, Joseph Davies, William Brierley, and William Meacock. Football and Recruiting.</p>
        <p>The Rev. 11. B. Firth, in making the pre- sentation, congratulated the boys and teachers• on their suCcess, and told the boys that as chairman of the managers he was gratified to know that they took an interest in healthful games as well as in their school work. He also referred to the recruiting in the parish, the number of men from St. James Parish, who have joined the forces, being over three hundred. There was some connection between</p>
        <p>football and recruiting. Mani of the recruits had played football at school, and by so doing had helped to fit themselves to fight their country's battles. The teachers were doing a good work in encouraging the boys.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0290" n="art0290">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Working Men's</p>
        <p>Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0291" n="art0291">
        <head>N.A. CENTRE. THE PIKE PEST.</head>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE. THE PIKE PEST.</p>
        <p>The conditions for close season angling do not improve, and members who have been out for trout have had a very poor time. Last season there were as many trout taken from the Wal- ton Reservoir in one day as there have been during the whole time the trout season has been in. However, we are waiting for better results. The best weighed in is one by E. Brookes, 11b. 90z., and this must not be con- sidered a good one from here. The officials are now considering what kind of bait can be Used by members, as after April 30th, the worm is barred. Taking results this year, it is not such a deadly bait as is sup- posed. However, members will learn, • latier, what they are allowed to fish with, and what- ever the arrangements are, let us hope better results will follow. Several members visited the Gowy during the Easter holidays. One. member .was subcessful in landing a trout of 140 z.; another caught six in one day, but they were not considered big endugh to weigh in. All were taken with the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0292" n="art0292">
        <head>worm</head>
        <p>worm</p>
        <p>We hear that seven pike were hooked by anglers in one day. That goes to prove that something will have to be done by the F.F.P.L., to guard streams infestecf by this class of fish. It is out of all reason to expect any member to put back pike into this favourite stream, when at the same time he knows be is killing his own sport. I wonder if any official of the F.F.P.L. would turn back a pike under the same condi- tions? But we have to go on telling our mem- bers they have not to betaken, and they are not recognised by the society as fish that can be weighed in. It would be a boon to the local member to learn that the League had lifted this narrow ruling policy of serving all socie- ties in all districts, in all waters (which are quite different, and are under conditions that do not require the same ruling) alike. There will be a general meeting at headquar- ters (Gladstone Arms) on Monday. April 19th, at 7.30 p.m. All members who have not re- ceived all their cards for this season must call for them, especially the Weaver and Norton cards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0293" n="art0293">
        <head>" 'T COOP " SEMIFINAL. St. Helens and the Hornets' Vain Struggle for Supremacy.</head>
        <p>" 'T COOP " SEMIFINAL. St. Helens and the Hornets' Vain Struggle for Supremacy.</p>
        <p>A " gate " of nearly 10,000 people, and takings amounting to £288! And this with two stranger-teams playing at Wilderspool. What would it have been if Warrington had been fortunate enough to have climbed into , that semi-final? But they were not, anu Wilderspool for the nonce, was merely the neutral ground upon which the Rochdale Hornets , and St. Helens strove and struggled for the right to enter the final phase in which will be decided the holdership of the Northern Union Cup. The patch attracted thousands of football enthusiasts to the town, and, doubtless the number would have been thousands larger, but for the fact that the war has taken even the whet off the appetite of many football devotees. Still, ten thousand was a fine crowd; a splendid crowd! The people rolled in from Rochdale by special train; from St. Helens they came in all sorts of vehicles—horsed and petrol power- driven—and it was easy to glean from the man- ner in which the applause and the groans were doled out that there were more supporters from Pilltown in evidence than 'there were Rochdale men. And. of course, on top of the purely- partisan section of the crowd there was the huge contingent of "locals," who on this occasion were in the unusual position of being impartial critics, capable of bestowing their verdict without ." favour, affection or ill-will," as they have it in the courts of law. A 110-MINUTE STRUGGLE THAT REMAINED UNDECIDED. The match, though keenly contested and exciting to the last, has, in a football sense, to be written down as one of the most disappoint- ing semi-finals of recent •years, and it also proved to be among the most surprising. For a hundred and ten minutes the Hornets and the Pilltown-men waged war—attacked and defended; defended and attacked; advanced and retreated; rushed and ,counter-ruslied—and when, the extra half-hour 'up, ,the referee blew a loud blast upon his whistle; no decision had been reached, and it was still a moot point as to which side should go to the final, the scores being equal, as were they also when the normal time-limit was reached. Truth to • tell, the football was scarce of cup-tie order; indeed, on Saturday's form it was a matter of opinion as to whether either side deserved to qualify for the final. Rochdale appeared to be more than stale; they were leg-weary after their full Easter programme. And St. Helens seemed quite unable to play their usual game. The net result was much aimless kicking, much crude passing, and a general lack of finish that seemed altogether incOngruous in a Match of such importance. SPORTSMANLIKE SPIRIT.</p>
        <p>An outstanding feature of the game, and one upon which both teams must be complimented, was the sportsmanlike spirit in which it was contested; indeed, it might almost be said that the perfect fairness with which the battle was waged amounted to punctiliousness. In this: respect it was a pattern cup semi-final. Judged on the day's play, St. Helens were fully the measure of their opponents, even if they were not "a little bit of overweight thrown in," and the game left one with the impression that "the men fra' Rochda" were a trifle lucky in making a draw of it. Both sides missed chances galore—cup semi-finals are like to make the best men a trifle "nervy," and a little less venturesome than on• ordinary occasions—and the hulk of such chances fell to the PilltOwn- men. It was only by the gift of a try that the Hornets • stung at all, for Roberts, the St. Helens back, to all intents and purposes, made I them the present of the Opportunity of which</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0294" n="art0294">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The Lure of the Rugby</p>
        <p>Semi-Final.</p>
        <p>WALTON AGAIN ' DISAPPOINTED.</p>
        <p>On Saturday there were four fixtures arranged to be played in the Warrington and District League but, chiefly owing to the attractive fixture on the Wilderspool ground for the Northern Rugby Union Cup, many of the Association players wended their way thither and made other sundry excuses for not fulfilling their own fixtures.</p>
        <p>The only match that was played was between Fairfield United and buns Green, on the Padgate-lane enclosure. The visitors only scored one goal in four, but on the run of the game did not deserve to be beaten by that margin. The first half was keenly contested, and no score was registered. It was in the second portion that the United asserted them- selves and gained the verdict. Cleworth. scored the finest goal of the match.</p>
        <p>It was arranged that Grappenhall should play the Walton team on the latter club's ground, but for some reason or other they failed to appear, and the Walton Club players were appointed for a second time.</p>
        <p>St. Barnabas' were expecting to play the Paddington team on the Lovely-lane ground, which for once in a way was in very good play- ing condition. The Paddington players met as arranged at Market Gate, but as their playing outfits failed to put in an appearance they dis- persed and went their own way instead of Lovely-lane way.</p>
        <p>The Orford White Star team were dis- appointed for the second week in succession by the WarringtonTentral failing to turn up.</p>
        <p>To-day, on the West-avenue ground, a grand football match will be played between Cadis- head Wesleyans, the champions of the North Cheshire League 2nd Division, and Stockton Heath, the runners-up. The gate receipts will be devoted to the League funds. At the con- clusion of the match Mr. W. H. Grounds, the president of the Stockton Heath club; will pre- sent the championship cup to the Cadishead Wesleyans' team. The medals will be presented at a meeting to be arranged at the close of the season.</p>
        <p>To-day, under the auspices of the Widnes and District Alliance, on the Penketh Recreation Ground, the Great Sankey team win meet a team selected from the Rest of the League, and their team will be selected from the following players :—Brindle, E. Glover, B. J. Kelly, J. E. Glover, F. Millington, H. Payne, Banner, .E. Forster, 11. Ditchfield, T. Whitfield, T. Illidge and A. Houghton. The most noticeable absentee will be Poulton, who is at present away from the district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0295" n="art0295">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>FAIRFIELD UNITED v. HOLLINS GREEN. The above teams- met on Saturday on the ground of the former club, in their return League engagement, with the following players: Fairfield United: Potter; Scott and Kelley; Gleave, Bebbington and Cole; Long, Wood- house, Millward, Taylor and Watkin. Hollins Green: Blundell; Clarke and Harri- son; Buckley, Cleworth and Taylor; Wright, Taylor, Muskett, Johnson and Atkinson. Referee;.Mr. B.s.Howell. • Hellins Green won the toss and Millward set the ball rolling against a stiff wind. Cleworth at once sent the ball out to Atkinson, and the latter, with a good centre, placed the home goal in danger. Scott headed the ball away and re- lieved the pressure temporarily. • The visitors quickly returned, when offside spoiled the situation. Good play by Millward and Wood- house transferred play to the visitors' end, where Buckley relieved and Taylor and Wright beating the home half-backs, ran the ball over the goal line. Even exchanges in midfield followed for a time, when Muskett beat the home backs, only for Potter to make a good save. The -United forwards were not com- bining at all well, the movements being erratic, and the visitors' backs cleared easily. Half- I time: No score.</p>
        <p>The second portion was opened very briskly by the visitas, and a splendid shot by Muskett was put over the bar by Potter. The ensuing corner was cleared by Kelley and Gleave passed nicely to his forwards, but cleworth and Buckley relieved. The United forwards re- turned to the attack and Taylor scored with a good shot. A few minutes later a penalty kick was given against Cleworth for pulling Mill- ward down. The latter took the kick and scored</p>
        <p>the second goal. Johnson and Atkinson com- bining nicely together, beat Gleave, _ and the</p>
        <p>former centred to Cleworth, who scored the best goal of the match, Millward scoring again close on time. Result: Fairfield United, 3 goals; Hollins Green, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0296" n="art0296">
        <head>CHESTER AND RUNCORN LEAGUE</head>
        <p>CHESTER AND RUNCORN LEAGUE</p>
        <p>BALFOUR-ROAD OLD BOYS v. KINGSLEY. The above teams met on the Volunteer ground Wore a moderate attendance. The Old Boys won the toss and played with the wind in their favour, and the game opened very evenly. 'After both ends had been visited, Kingsley opened the score, Alderman beating Holt with a fast shot. The Old Boy, from the centre kick, beat all opposition, and Able equalised. Directly after Sefton gained the lead for the Old Boys. The ensuing play was chiefly in midfield, and in the occasional raids which were made on each goal the custodians were not seriously troubled. Half-time: 01(1. Boys, 2; Kingsley, 1. On resuming the visitors, with the wind in their favour, at once attacked, but the home basks were equal to the demands made upon them, and drove the visitors back to their own half. After the homesterfi had been penalised several times for offside, Green got through and scored the third . point, and added a fourth a few minutes later After .these reverses the visitors were several times within an ace of lowering the home citadel. A splendid effort by the Kingsley centre-half hit the cross-bar and re- bounded into play. The visitors gained several corner kicks, but failed to improve upon them_ and time was called with play in midfield, the score being: Balfour-road Old Boys, 4 goals; Kingsley, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0297" n="art0297">
        <head>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>STARKEY CUP COMPETITION, Semi-final Tie. • At the King and Queen ground:—Paddington v. Walton; referee, M. J. Kite.</p>
        <p>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. Stockton Heath v. Cadishead Wesleyans; Mr. Goddard.</p>
        <p>WIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE. Champions v. Rest of League. At Penketh Recreation :—Great Sankey (Champions); D. F. James..</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Fairfield United v. Longford United. St. Barnabas' v. Earlestown Mission. Orford White Star v. Warrington Central.</p>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>EMMANUEL INSTITUTE v. PADGATE UNITED.</p>
        <p>At Padgate:— Emmanuel T. Knight; E. Crozier and S. Hughes; T. Hughes, E. Hardy and W. Ayres; Walter Eaton, A. Oakes, S. Roberts (Capt.), R. Rates and William Eaton: Team meet at Market Gate at 2.20:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0298" n="art0298">
        <head>FOR CONVALESCENT WARRIORS.</head>
        <p>FOR CONVALESCENT WARRIORS.</p>
        <p>New Auxiliary Home Hospital At Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>The care of our wounded soldiers returned from the scene of activity on the other side ofl the Channel is a matter in which everyone naturally takes a strong interest, and where assistance can be given the Government in ministering to the gallant fellows stricken by the way, it is readily given. A public meeting was held in the Large Room, Grappenhall, on Tuesday evening, with the object of forming a committee to control an auxiliary home hospital for wounded soldiers, at Thelwall Heyes, which has been kindly lent by Mr. and Mrs. W. M. Shipman for the purpose. Mr. W. 'Peter Rylands bresided, and was supported .by Mr. F. ..kylmer Frost. There was not a very large attendance, but those who did put in an appear- ance showed themselves to be in earnest, and the scheme was quickly put on a business foot- ing. Although the hospital is to be established in the Thelwall-Grappenhall district, the pro- moters do not wish the matter to be regarded as of concern' only to the residents of the im- mediate locality. Subscriptions and other offers of help will be weleorne, no matter from what quarter. The most acceptable -form that sup- ' port can take is the certain sum per week form. It is anticipated that the local farmers will make a generous response. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0299" n="art0299">
        <head>NEED FOR THE HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>THE NEED FOR THE HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that. a few weeks ago a movement was put on foot to offer the military authorities a hospital in the district of Grappenhall and Thelwall. There was ,some question as to whether the authorities required any more hospitals of that kind. It was felt that those two districts, lying a long distance away from the nearest hospital, should be pre- pared to offer to provide such an institution if one were required. He was informed that the matter had been carefully considered by the military authorities, who expressed the view that the hospital would be badly needed,, and that the proposal was a very proper one. It was extremely good of Mr. and Mrs. Shipman to place Thelwall Heyes at the disoosal of the promoters. The house was peculiarly suited to the purpose. It had been approved by a repre- sentative of the military authorities. That meeting had been, called with the joint idea of obtaining public sanction for the scheme and appointing a committee of administration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0300" n="art0300">
        <head>GOVERNMENT'S GRANT.</head>
        <p>THE GOVERNMENT'S GRANT.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. A. Frost said that it was felt thatl every section of society should take a part in the maintenance of the hospital. The Gov- ernment had agreed to make a grant of 2s. per Patient per day, and the full complement of patients would be twenty-three. The cost to the public would not be so much if the hospital was filled, instead of being only half-filled. It might cost them something like £4OO a ,year. Be thought there would be no difficulty about obtaining the necessary money. One hundred and eight pounds had already been Promised, and £6 collected, and no serious attempt to get subscriptions had yet been made. Allud- ing to the possibilities as to a -balance in hand at the end of the war, the speaker said the disposal of any such surplus would be left to the discretion of the committee. Probably it would be utilised for the helping of men who ! had been Maimed, or in other ways seriously handicapped for the industrial struggle. In conclusion, the Chairman said that the best way to help the hospital scheme was to under- take to give a subscription weekly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0301" n="art0301">
        <head>WORKING MEN'S COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>A WORKING MEN'S COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. 'l'. Nicholls proposed that a Working Alen's Committee be formed for the purpose of collecting money amonkst their mates at the different workshops. . The meeting empowered Mr. Nicholls to form such a committee.</p>
        <p>Votes of thanks were accorded the Chairman and the Rector (the Rev. H. V. Pigot) for the loan of the room.</p>
        <p>, A letter from Mr. H. P. Depsham, expressing regret at his inability to be present, was read to the meeting.</p>
        <p>THE GENERAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The following were elected to serve on the General Committee: The Revs. H. V. Pigot and W. S. Stanton, Drs. Henry Lang.'lle and C. Robinson, Messrs. Arthur Bleckly, W. Breward, John Coson, H. P. Densham, R. T. Fairclough, F. A. Frost, T. B. Handley, H. Holden, Graham Hutchings, Robert Hutton, Lister-Kaye, William Lomas, R. C. Parr, R. Poulton, W. Peter Rylands, W. M. Shipman, William Trantom, C. H. Tinsley, R. 'W. Wilde and W. Walker.</p>
        <p>FINDING THE FUNDS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0302" n="art0302">
        <head>Successful Whist Drive and Dance At Parr Hall.</head>
        <p>Successful Whist Drive and Dance At Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>A very successful whist drive and dance in aid of the funds of the proposed new military auxiliary-hospital, was held at the Parr Hall, Warrington, on Thursday evening. over four hundred people were present, and a most enjoy- able evening was spent. Congratulations are due to Mrs. F. A. Frost and Mrs. Kerfoot, who organised the large undertaking, to the M.C.'s of the evening—Messrs. J. H. Houghton, A. Jowett and H. McQueen, for the important part they played in bringing the proceedings to a. thoroughly satisfactory completion, to the indefatigable committee, and to all who in any way contributed to the excellent result attained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0303" n="art0303">
        <head>NINETY .TABLES FOR WHIST.</head>
        <p>NINETY .TABLES FOR WHIST.</p>
        <p>Three hundred and sixty players participated in. the whist drive, the M.C.'s for which were Messrs. J. H. Houghton and A. Jowett. The drive commenced about eight, and ended shortly after 1030, when there was an interval before the presentation of the prizes by the Mayor (Alderman P. Peacock). At the conclusion of this part of the programme, •dancing corn- xnenced, and was kept up till two o'clock in the morning. to the strains of the Latchford orchestra, conducted by Mr. Alfred Grounds.</p>
        <p>The M.C.'s for the dancing were Messrs. A. Jowett and H. McQueen. Tasty refreshments were daintily served by a staff under Mrs. Kerfoot. The halls was adorned with -plants lent by Mr. Whitfield, head gardener to the Cor- poration.</p>
        <p>The Mayor presented the prizes for the whist to the following ladies and gentlemen:—Ladies: 1, Mrs. A. Shakeshaft (180); 2, Mrs. W. Martin (178) ; 3, Miss Thornley (178); 4, Mrs. Lightfoot (174), Mrs. Lockerby (174). Gents: 1, Mrs. A. Baker (playing as gent) (183); 2, Mr. A. Darbyshire (181); 3, Mrs. E. Power (178); 4,s Miss Gleave (176); 5, Mr. Bothwell (175). Mrs. Earwaker was the winner of a quilt, which was raffled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0304" n="art0304">
        <head>MRS. FROST'S THANKS.</head>
        <p>MRS. FROST'S THANKS.</p>
        <p>Mrs. F. A. Frost, who was supported on the platform by Mrs. Harrison and Miss Atkinson. as well as the Mayor, expressed, in a few aptly chosen words, her thanks to all who had helped in making.the effort a success—those who had worked so assiduously and heartily in the organisation and execution of the evening's scheme, and those who had done their share by purchasing tickets. She hoped everyone was satisfied, and, in any case, they had the gratifi- cation of knowing that they had done some- thing for our wounded soldiers. More especially, she would like to thank the Mayor for his kindness in lending that hall for the occasion and in presenting the prizes. ,(Applause.) The Mayor briefly replied, modestly depre- cating the part he had played in the attainment of the evening's signal success, and observing that anything he had done was only what it was his duty to do, considering it was a case of doing it for our wounded soldiers. None could but admirß the splendid efforts of Mrs. Frost and Mrs. Kerfoot in the cause of the new hospital. It was a great work, in which he was proud to be afforded the opportunity of taking. part, however insignificant. In conclusion, the Mayor called for three cheers for Mrs. Frost and Mrs. Kerfoot.</p>
        <p>The cheers were heartily given and were followed by others for Mr. Frost and for the Mayor, on the call of Mrs. Frost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0305" n="art0305">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>WORKS' BOWLING ASSOCIATION GETTING TO WORK.</p>
        <p>The draw for ;the " Finlayson." Cup Com- petition in connection with the Warrington Works' Amateur Bowling Association will take place on Monday next, and the games will commence the following Saturday, April 24th. The green at the association's headquarters, the Horse and Jockey, Winwick-road, will be open to the public to-day, for the first games of the season. Experts who have had a trial trundle pronounce the turf to be in excellent condition.</p>
        <p>• Despite the war no fewer than 41 teams have entered the Cup Competition, a total that is regarded as highly satisfactory and which augurs a successful and interesting season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0306" n="art0306">
        <head>SHOOTING MATCH.</head>
        <p>SHOOTING MATCH.</p>
        <p>FLETCHER, RUSSELL'S BEAT RYLANDS'. Teams representing the rifle clubs in connec- tion with Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co.'s and Messrs. Rylands' Works met on the range of the former club on Tuesday evening in a friendly match, the conditions being ten rounds each at 50 yards. The scores were as follows : Fletcher, Russell's: Buchanan 95, Fleming 87, Legge 89, Towers 90, Mason 84, McPhie 85, Gleave 83, Dennett 93, Daintith 87, Gamble 84-- 877.</p>
        <p>Rylands' : Newton (1) 65, Bailey 77, Peters 76, Gibson, jun., 66, Garnett 82, Balmer 57, Allman 76, Williams 68, Gibson, sen., 66, Newton (2) 64 —697.</p>
