      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0326" n="art0326">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAX, APRIL 17, 1915. WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAX, APRIL 17, 1915. WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Highly Satisfactory Result of Official Test.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0327" n="art0327">
        <head>TINY DEFLECTION UNDER HUGE WEIGHTS.</head>
        <p>TINY DEFLECTION UNDER HUGE WEIGHTS.</p>
        <p>Two interesting events have marked the com- pletion of the new Warrington Bridge. Of these, the first was the public 'testing, which took place in the presence of a large number of spectators at eight o'clock on Sunday morn- ing; whilst the latter was held on Tuesday after- noon, and consisted of the unveiling by the Mayor of a second commemoration tablet, followed by tea at the Town Hall.</p>
        <p>Commemoratory Tablet Unveiled.</p>
        <p>of the resident engineers—first, Mr. J. Mansfield Cobb, and, more latterly, Mr. G. H. Hodgson— and ,to congratulate them upon the success which had attended their efforts. Great credit was due to them. The Mayor concluded by ex- pressing the confident hope that the people of Warrington would be satisfied with what they had got for the £23,370 they had already spent on the -bridge. "THEY LAUGH BEST WHO LAUGH LAST." Alderman TINNION proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayor, and in doing so said that the Council had had a good deal of fun poked- at them over the matter of the bridge; they had had gratuitous advice from many amateur bridge builders—(laughter)—as well as much criticism, but, as, usual from such quarters, very little help. However, he believed that if the critics and fault-finders would look at the fin- ished bridge they would—if they told the truth —be perfectly satisfied with the work that had been done. It was a happy thing that that day's ceremony should have taken place on the Mayor's birthday, and they all wished his Wor- ship "many happy returns of the day."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0328" n="art0328">
        <head>OFFICIAL TESTING. Bridge Bears Great Strain Splendidly.</head>
        <p>OFFICIAL TESTING. Bridge Bears Great Strain Splendidly.</p>
        <p>The 'great interest taken by Warringtonians in their new bridge was strikingly manifested at the official testing on Sunday morning, for despite the early hour, well over two hundred assembled to witness the proceedings and (literally) to "add their weight" thereto. Amongst the spectators were Aldermen J. Smethurst (chairman of the Bridges Com- mittee), Bennett, James Evans and John Evans; and Councillor Osborne. Mr. A. M. Ker (borough surveyor) and Mr. Jas. Cook, together with other members of the borough engineer's department, conducted the test. Five tramcars—each weighing twelve tons, and carrying about two dozen passengers (journey gratis !)—and two steam rollers were used for weights, one of the steam rollers was heavily loaded to bring its weight up to 20 tons, and the second roller weiglied 15 tons. The total strain which the bridge had to bear was, therefore, 95 tons. The five tramcars were first formed up, so as to cover the east side of the bridge, and the steam rollers were then stationed alongside them as near as the centre of the bridge as possible. Next all the cars and rollers were backed up Bridge-street, and (as there was only one engine driver) the lighter engine was left in Mersey-street. The 20-ton steam roller then headed a procession over the bridge, the cars being driven as fast as the steam roller would. permit. Afterwards the same tests—stationary and moving—were re- peated on the other side of the structure. There were in addition to the weighty rollers and cars about eighty people standing on the bridge, and several carts passed over during the teNts.</p>
        <p>BRIDGE "GIVES" 32nd PART OF INCH UNDER 95 TONS WEIGHT.</p>
        <p>The results are rightly considered highly satisfactory. The deflection of the bridge under the weight of the stationary, load was the 32nd part of an inch, while the pressure of the moving load caused it to deflect only the 64th part of an inch. The vibration of the bridge was .the 32nd part of an inch. The deflection was recorded by means of a staff held against the under-side of the ribs of the bridge from a boat moored in the centre of the river, the minute measurements being observed by using 'a theottoliie.- ‘," .</p>
        <p>Judging from the conversation of the spec- tators, the townspeople in general never doubted the stability and strength of the structure. The .tendency, indeed, was to treat the whole affair as a joke; and as the crowd dispersed one worthy was heard to exclaim: "My word; there'll be some big breakfasses eaten this morning!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0329" n="art0329">
        <head>UNVEILING THE TABLET. Putting The Finishing Touch To The Bridge.</head>
        <p>UNVEILING THE TABLET. Putting The Finishing Touch To The Bridge.</p>
        <p>The brief al fresco unveiling ceremony on Tuesday afternoon opened with prayer by the Rector (the Rev. Canon Willis). CONSUMMATION OF THIRTY YEARS'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0330" n="art0330">
        <head>AGITATION.</head>
        <p>AGITATION.</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST. as the chairman of the committee that had been mainly responsible for bringing about this fine addition to Warring- ton's public structures, viz., the Bridges Com- mittee, fittingly had the first words. That day's ceremony, he said, might be said to put the finishing • touch to Warrington Bridge; and he then proceeded to detail the history of the move- ment in the Council which culminated in the decision to build the present fine structure. It was, he mentioned, over thirty years since the agitation for the widening of the old bridge began; it began, in fact, just about six years before he entered the Council. Several alterna- tive schemes to the one that had actually been adopted were suggested, but he thought they would all agree that the one upon which the Council had acted was quite the best of the lot.</p>
        <p>Council Did The Best And Cheapest Thing.</p>
        <p>The time came when the Council were called upon to make a choice between iron and ferro- concrete, as the materials out of which the bridge should be constructed, and, in his opinion, the Council did quite the best and cheapest thing in deciding upon ferro-concrete. There had, he continued, been a great deal of fighting about the bridge—(laughter)---in fact, at one time one could not go to a dinner or tea party without being twitted on the subject. (More laughter.) It had even provided material for jokes on the pantomime stage—(laughter)— .comedians being apt to say that so-and-so (some- thing very remote) would happen when War- rington Bridge was built. (Laughter.) The Council had been scapegoats for a long time. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0331" n="art0331">
        <head>TABLET.</head>
        <p>THE TABLET.</p>
        <p>The Mayor, who was received with a hearty rovind of applause, then pulled the cord which held in position the green curtain which tem- porarily hid from view the chastely-ornate memorial tablet. .</p>
        <p>The tablet is wrought in bronze, and in general design is the counterpart of the one placed on the opposite pylon to record the visit of. their Majesties, King George V. and Queen Mary. The inscription reads: This is the sixth bridge erected here, and was built during the mayoralties of Alderman William J. Forshaw, Council- lor G. W. Joseph, M.D., J.P., Alderman Peter Peacock. Chairman of Bridges Committee: Alderman James Smethurst, J.P. Town Clerk: J. Lyon Whittle. Engineer: John J. Webster, M.lnst., C.E. Borough Engineers: Thomas Longdin, A. M. Ker, B.Sc., A.M.lnst. C.E. Contractors: Alfred Thorne and Sons. 1304 1915.</p>
        <p>When the (nrtain had been drawn back, what time the crowd stood bare-headed, his WORSHIP Went on to express the pleasure it afforded him to have had the great honour and privilege of being- asked to unveil the tablet. He trusted that the structure would fulfil all the require- ments that Were expected of it, and added that when they contrasted the new bridge with the first structure-on that site, which was erected in -1304, they must, he said, feel gratified that the Corporation had had the courage to build a bridge which was almost the widest in Great Britain. The Westminster Bridge beat it by only itwo feet, but he thofight the Warrington Bridge was the most handsome in Great Britain. Great praise was due to the bridge builders, and the more' so from the fact that not one .serious accident had taken place during the whole of what must have been difficult and dan- gerous work. They all knew that the work had been delayed, but they could not control the tides or foresee all things that might happen to the best undertakings. The only cause for regret was that Mr. Webster, one of the most eminent engineers in the world, was no longer with them, as it: would have gladdened' his heart to have seen the completion of a project in which he had taken particularly keen interest. ,</p>
        <p>The present war would pass away; they be- lieved England would again emerge triumphant, and that God would still give the increase to the old town. The war had drawn together all classes of the community, as well as linking Great Britain more closely to the Colonies and to our Allies, and it occurred to him that the new bridge, which joined north and south, would be emblematic of the new spirit forged amid the horrors of war and of the closer union existing between Lancashire and Cheshire and all parts of the kingdom and of the Empire. (Applause.) Councillor Loom seconded, and the proposi- tion having been carried, Alderman SMETHITRST briefly acknowledged the compliment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0332" n="art0332">
        <head>Warringtonians Proud of Their Bridge.</head>
        <p>Warringtonians Proud of Their Bridge.</p>
        <p>Even, if the whole of the members of the -Town Council were not fully satisfied with the brido, the people of Warrington were very proud of —(loud applause)—and he was quite sure that it would fulfil all expectations. His Worship went cra to pay,g.tribute to the excellent work</p>
        <p>DON'T LOOK Wax—Keep the ravages of time at bay by darkening the grey streaks with LOCKYER'S SULPHUR HAIR REsTonsit, which deepens to • natnral colour in a few day.. Lockyer's maintains a youthful appearance. Large bot's. everywhere. Post free 1/6 Bedford Laboratory, London, 8.E.,</p>
        <p>At the second benefit "house" several well- known local artistes have promised their assist- ance, and the result will be an entertainment of the highest and most enjoyable order. Mr. Thorpe Mayne and his company will present a dramatic sketch from the pen of their leader himself, and entitled "The Love of Leslie Heseltine;" and other contributions to the pro- gramme will be given by Madame Sara Silcock, Mrs. J. Smallshaw, Miss Elsie Hewitt; Mr. H. M. Ashton, Mr. Fred Fieldhouse, and Mr. W. Booth, the last five appearing in a cycle, of old English melodies—" Flora's Holiday," with Mr. W. H. Payton as conductor. Artistes from the Palace and Hippodrome have also kindly prom- ised their assistance, and the Warrington Dramatic Society will stage the one-act farcical comedy, "A Little 'll6wl' Play." Altogether two splendid programmes are promised, and if the lover of play and music misses either—well, he is making a miss he will regret.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0333" n="art0333">
        <head>TOWN'S FOUR FINE APPROACHES.</head>
        <p>TOWN'S FOUR FINE APPROACHES.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. FURNESS, the chairman of the War- rington Rural District Council, seconded the Alderman's proposition,, and paid tribute to "the 'consistent pluck and courage" which Alderman Smethurst had shown in guiding his committee and other colleagues in the matter of the building of the bridge. He went on to say that Warrington would shortly have four of the finest approaches to be found in any town, for, in addition to Warrington Bridge, . there were the spacious Bruche Bridge on the east side; Longford Bridge, which would take all the traffic on the north side for generations to come; and the new Sankey Bridge, which would shortly be in use on the west side. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The vote of thanks was accorded with cheers, and in acknowledgment his WORSHIP said he would have a unique record as Mayor of War- rington, for most important things had hap- pened during his term of office. Indeed, during his Mayoralty the most serious happening in history had occurred, viz., the war. He onlSl wished he were in a position to give an fitssur- anee that the war, which had made his term of office so memorable, would be completed before his tenure of the position closed.</p>
        <p>DAYS OF DARKNESS AND HORROR.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT next proposed a vote of thanks to Alderman Smethurst for his services in connection with the bridge, remarking that that was a great day in the history of Warring- ton. It was, the Alderman said, possible to derive a lesson from the bridge and its previous history, which would be applicable to the cir- cumstances of the day. The first bridge, as they had heard, was erected, in 1304, and since that far-Off day the country had passed through times which in their darkness and horror were almost paralleled by the times through which the land was now passing. But the river had flowed on and Silt successive bridges had stood while England had,-through the centuries, risen to greater heights and Warrington to more abundant prosperity. Links the War has Forged 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0334" n="art0334">
        <head>MAYOR'S COMMEMORATIVE PARTY.</head>
        <p>MAYOR'S COMMEMORATIVE PARTY.</p>
        <p>King Toasted in Tea.</p>
        <p>Subsequently the Mayor entertained a numerous company to tea in the Town Hall, the catering being in the hands of Mr. Mitchell, who discharged the task devolving upon him in his usual excellent manner, a most attractive and delectable menu having been prepared. Over the teacups seVeral toasts were-sub- mitted, that of "The King," of course, coming first, and being enthusiastically drunk, most appropriately, as the Mayor remarked, in tea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0335" n="art0335">
        <head>CITIZENS PROUD OF THE BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>CITIZENS PROUD OF THE BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR proposed the toast of "Success to the Bridge,"' and, in doing. so, said that the town now possessed a means of communication across the river of which they were all proud; indeed, if it , were possible; the citizens were even more proud of the bridge than were the Council, and that was a point well worth bear- ing in mind in these days of high rates and high-everything-else. With the toast his Wor- ship coupled the names of Mr. Fitz Simons, the partner of the late Mr. Webster, and Mr. Alfred Thorne, the head of the firm responsible for carrying out Mr. Webster's noble. design. His Worship and Recruiting in the Municipal</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0336" n="art0336">
        <head>Offices.</head>
        <p>Offices.</p>
        <p>The Mayor Went on to refer to the fact that three of Mr. Thorne's sons were gallantly doing their duty with the military forces, and he wished he could say that every firm and under- taking was diving of its best on the same lines in furtherance of the Imperial cause. "As head of this borough,"- his Worship went on, "I cannot say I am personally proud of the Warrington Corporation, in the extent to which, so far, they are giving the best of their servants to further the successful issue of the war. When I look round the various departments and see the single young men in the employ of the War- rington Town Council, I don't think I can con- gratulate our borough, our Corporation and our officials on giving the proper and only sensible lead in the work of our great Empire:"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0337" n="art0337">
        <head>ALL-ROUND MAYOR.</head>
        <p>THE ALL-ROUND MAYOR.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Fru SIMONS, and THORNE suitably re- sponded, after which Alderman TINNION pro- posed the health of the Mayor, remarking that he was sure that all must admire the way in which Alderman Peacock had done his work. He had tackled anything and everything, and had tackled it fearlessly.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. FURNESS, as chairman of the Warring- ton Rural District Council, also spoke to the toast, commenting eulogistically upon the Mayor's energy, tact and initiative as a public man, and commending him as one whose work had done much towards improving and benefit- ing the town with which he was proud to be so prominently associated. The toast having been cordially received, the MAYOR briefly expressed his thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0338" n="art0338">
        <head>OTHER TOASTS.</head>
        <p>OTHER TOASTS.</p>
        <p>The health of the Chairman of the Bridges Committee (Alderman Smethurst) was also drunk, at the call of Alderman BENNETT and Councillor LOCKER, who warmly eulogised the Alderman's services in connection with the bridge. Before separating the company toasted in silence the memory of the late Engineer, Mr. Webster, the MAYOR remarking that they all profoundly regretted that he—one of the most distinguished sons of Warrington-,had not lived to see the consummation of his efforts. Three cheers were then given for our soldiers and sailors, after which the National Anthem was sung.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0339" n="art0339">
        <head>BRIDGES COMMITTEE AND CONTRACTORS.</head>
        <p>BRIDGES COMMITTEE AND CONTRACTORS.</p>
        <p>At a recently held meeting of the Bridges Committee it was resolved that the Town Clerk be -instructed .to convey to Alessrs. Alfred Thorne and Sons an expression of opinion from the members of the coMmittee of the, effi- cient manner in which they have carried out the construction of the Bridge, and the con- gratulations of the committee on the fact that the work had been carried out without loss of life or serious accident.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0340" n="art0340">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>ROLLICKING REVUE AT THE</p>
        <p>ROYAL COURT.</p>
        <p>" All the Nice Girls" is the title of a madly- merry revue which is, this week, being played to big business at the Royal Court. It is full of harmless fun and is played by a host of pretty girls and a handful of real mirth-pro- voking comedians. The scenic effects are lavish and the music is of exactly the order best adapted to a production of the kind. And what more would you have? A kind of hybrid between musical comedy and pantomime, the production not only con- tains plenty of mirth and melody,, but through it there also runs a sentimental vein in which the usual charming girl and the manly man figure, their little romance providing the excuse for dragging in one or two sentimental ballads and duets, the latter including a particularly effective number entitled "Kentucky Days" It is most effectively sung- by Miss Viola Rene (who makes a charming heroine) and Mr. Kenneth Seymour, who is a dashing hero. Miss Joy Phillis makes a vivacious servant girl, and little Elsie Young, as a Boy Scout, is to be complimented on the clearness and natural- ness of her singing The comedians work hard, and their wheezes never fail to "get there." Mr. Geo. West ex- tracts every ounce of fun out of the part of an impossible Scotsman, and Mr t Sid Kearns also contributes lavishly to the laughter-raising department. The chorus girls are dainty and they do their work well, and altogether lovers of the light- some and blithesome could hardly do better than pay the show a visit.</p>
        <p>A Shaw Play—and Others—Next Week.</p>
        <p>Devotees of the higher drama are to have a rich treat on Tuesday and Saturday evenings of next week, when Mr. N. Thorpe-Mayne and Miss Beryl Hamilton and Co. will stage Bernard Shaw's scintillating masterpiece_"Arms and the Man "—the play on which was founded the wonderfully successful light opera "The Chocolate Soldier." A Shaw play is a rara avis in Warrington, and should prove an irresistible attraction, even if one only goes out of curiosity. On Monday afternoon and evening Hubert Henry Davies' "Captain Drew on Leave" will be staged, on Wednesday evening " Don," and on Friday the famous old Robertsonian comedy "Ours." Each of the plays named will be given twice nightly—at 6.50 and nine.</p>
        <p>POPULAR MANAGER'S BENEFIT.</p>
        <p>Two Splendid Programmes for Next Thursday. • The courteous and popular acting manager of the Royal Court Theatre, Mr. R. L. Warburton, is to take his annual complimentary benefit on Thursday evening .of next week. Uusually the benefit performance has been limited to one house," but so great has been the demand for seats in the past that this year it has been decided to have two performances the same night, the first at 6.50 and the second at 9. Mr. Warburton deserves well of local lovers of the drama, for not only does he do every- thing in his power to ensure that the fare pro- vided at the theatre which he so ably directs shall be just of the kind that is attuned to the varying tastes of his patrons, but he has also seen to it that the house itself from the point of view of comfort and cleanliness is second to none.</p>
        <p>Two excellent and entirely different pro- grammes. have been drawn up for the benefit performances. At the fiist. " house " the cellent company which next week is to appear at the house in a repertoite of excellent plays, will give Hubert Davies's "'Doormats," which is quite one of the most brilliant and fascinating plays that ever eal3lo out of London, and is certainly the best play of its type that has visited Warrington for more than one moon. In addition Miss Minnie Williams (soprano) and Mr. Cyril Cooke (violinist)_ will appear._</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0341" n="art0341">
        <head>LAUGHTER AND TEARS AT THE HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>LAUGHTER AND TEARS AT THE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Many novelties are included in the first-rate vaudeville menu presented this week at the Hippodrome. Chief of these is a semi-dramatic playlet—" A Fairy Story." This, presented by George Hudson, has a very pretty "laughter and tears" theme, and also contains the topical touch of the soldier's heroism.</p>
        <p>Fred Ward, the popular Yorkshire comedian, and the beautiful dancer, Miss Selina, bring the "house" down with their burlesque coon scena, which includes a speciality dancing act. Alf. Chester, a versatile comedian, earns Very hearty applause with selections from his capital repertoire. Agda and Company (in "The Lady of the Future") show remarkable nerve, skill and strength in their ~comedy acrobatics; whilst Woodward and Page's remarkable variety of acrobatic and musical specialities constitute an- other "tip-top" turn. Mr. Fred Lindsay, "the famous Australian ranchman and stock-whip expert," is in great form with his adaptation of sensational feats of swordsmanship applied to his different stock- whips. 'Amidst lovely scenic effects, he success- fully performs all sorts of thrilling feats of muscle and dexterity with the co-operation of the members of his company. To F. Alfred Swift, "the boy entertainer," the .production of beautifully coloured rhalk landscapes and very amusing cartoons is but a matter of moments. "The Gaumont Graphic" PrCtures up-to-date war news upon the screen. Sparkling Comedian Next Week.</p>
        <p>The specially attractive bill of fare prepared for next week will be topped by `fatten Hall, in his latest comedy. creation, "`Patten Hall," in which he will have the as4stance. of Miss Donna: Haydon. Cromo and Hamilton will appear in a burlesque scena, entitled "Fashions," and another strong- turn will be provided by David Poole, one of the smartest ventriloquists- on the vaudeville stage. Mani other turns will go to the making up of a first- rate two-hour programme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0342" n="art0342">
        <head>DRAMA, COMEDY AND FARCE AT</head>
        <p>DRAMA, COMEDY AND FARCE AT</p>
        <p>TIIE GRAND.</p>
        <p>Truly "Grand" programmes are the order this Week at the popular Bridge Foot picture- lovers' resort. During tile first half of the week the firstklass programme included "Peter's Patent Fertiliser," a hilarious st" comic." "Regan's Daughter" was the title of a stirring drama of love and hate. "Those Love Pangs," featuring Chas. Chaplin. and Other famous comedians, was too funny fbr words. "A Page From. Life," proved to be a finely-filmed and powerfully acted series of thrilling adventures. " Too • Much Game" was an uproarious . absurdity _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>To-day' bills-a-fare will doubtless prove a great attraction, for in them Premier places are allotted to "The . Mill Stream Z--another of those fine dramas for which the Grand is already becoming •so noted—and • "The :Wonders," a comedy subject, again featuring the inimitable Chaplin. A " Grand " Programme for Next Week.</p>
        <p>Another splendid programme Irs been arranged for The Grand next week, and picture- lovers will have an opportunity of spending a tip-top couple of hours any evening from Mcn- day to Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0343" n="art0343">
        <head>IN THE HANDS OF THE HUNS.</head>
        <p>IN THE HANDS OF THE HUNS.</p>
        <p>Corporal E. Jones, of the South Lancashire Regiment, whose home is in Elizabeth.street, and who was previously reported as missing, is now reported to be a prisoner in the hands of the Germans. Private Barnes, also of the South Lancashire Regiment, and whose borne is in Friar'-lane, is reported as being wounded an-d .a prisoner.</p>
