      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0344" n="art0344">
        <head>TERRITORIAL CASUALTIES</head>
        <p>TERRITORIAL CASUALTIES</p>
        <p>6 Killed, 9 Died of Wounds,</p>
        <p>And 40 Wounded.</p>
        <p>FIRST OFFICIAL LIST.</p>
        <p>The first official list of casualties suffered by the Warrington Territorials was published this week, and it is of such length as to demonstrate beyond all doubt, the fact that the citizen- soldiers of whom the town is so justly proud, have borne their fair share of the recent fierce fighting. An analysis of the list shows that up to the date on which it was made out, presumably April 2nd, one officer and five non-commissioned officers and men had been killed in action; nine had died of wounds, and one officer and 36 non- commissioned officers and men had been wounded. In addition two men had received slight wounds, but were not seriously enough hurt to be admitted to hospital. _</p>
        <p>The list was forwarded to Lieutenant C. L. Williamson, of the 4th South Lancs., who has charge of the recruiting at the Drill Hall, and who, in turn, has communicated it to the Press. His communication reads: Below is appended a complete official list of all casualties of the 4th South Lancashire Regiment sustained by the Battalion up to April sth. Further lists will be sent to me after the completion of each turn in the trenches. or after any action .at which many casualties occur. On being received lists will be posted up in the Drill Hall and can be seen any hour on week days from 10 am. to 7 p.m. The list referred to is as follows :•</p>
        <p>OFFICERS. KILLED. Lieutenant A. Seymour-Jones, "A" Company. —Bullet wound in head; March 27th.</p>
        <p>WOUNDED. Captain H. G. Roberts, " B " Company. Bullet wound in the lower right leg; \larch 26th</p>
        <p>NON-CONIMISSIONED OFFH:ERS .X\D MEN. KILLED.</p>
        <p>2316, Private T. Watson, " B " Company.— Bullet wound in the head whilst on observation duty; February 28th. .</p>
        <p>2267, Private A. Hughes, "A " Company.— Bullet wound in the neck; March 3rd.</p>
        <p>1438, Private E. Warburton, " B " Company. —Bullet wound in abdomen; March 10th. • 1287, Acting-Corporal R. Humphries, "B " Company.—Bullet wound in the eye; March 16th.</p>
        <p>2499, Lance-Corporal W. Ball, " C " Company. —Bullet wound in the heart; March 31st.</p>
        <p>DIED OF WOUNDS.</p>
        <p>2491, Private J. Welldon, "B" Company.— Bullet wound in the neck; February 28th; died March 3rd</p>
        <p>IT Private W. Brisce, "C" Company.— Bullet wound in the abdomen; March sth; died March 6th.</p>
        <p>1990, Corporal Geo. Higham, " A " Company. —Bullet wound in the abdomen; March 11th; died the same day.</p>
        <p>1684, Private W. G. Rawlinson, "A " Corn- pany.—Wounded, March 11th; died !March 12th. 1821, Private A. B. Lawless, "D" Company.— Wounded. March 12th; multiple injuries from shrapnel; died the same day. 1350, Private, T. Smith, "B" Company.— Bullet wound in the temple; March 17th; died the same day. 2402,• Private W. Etridgen, " A" Company.— Bullet wound in the abdomen; March 27th; died March 28th.</p>
        <p>1460. Private F. L. larvki, "B" Company.— Bullet wound in the head; March 30th; died the same day. 226 g. Private J. Bernett, "A" Company.— Bullet wound in the head; March 31st; died April let.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0345" n="art0345">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>RETURNED TO DUTY.</p>
        <p>The under-mentioned N.C.O.'s and men re- turned to duty on the dates stated. after having either being injured or on the sick list Sergeant Ford, March 3rd. Private Rowlands, March 4th. Private Rusciale, March. sth. • Private G. Chadwick, March sth. Private W. 'Hardman. March 6th. Private J. Laycock, larch 6th. Sergeant Sollery, A pril 4th. Private J. Fillingham, March 30th. Private W. Howells has also returned to active service.' %</p>
