      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0348" n="art0348">
        <p>I P.J</p>
        <p>THE DRINK PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>Free Churches Will Support Government Up To Prohibition</p>
        <p>The, Warrington Free Church Council on Thursday forwarded the following to Mr. McKenna :--</p>
        <p>fit. Bon. Sir,—</p>
        <p>At the annual Free Church Council meeting the following resoluticrn was passed unani- mously:—</p>
        <p>That this Council being deeply impressed by the moral and economic evils arising from: the use of strong drink, which evils impair the national efficiency required dur- ing the present crisis, begs to assure the Government of its support in any drastic steps it may take to secure national sobriety up to the point of prohibition, and urges that any legislation shall be of a national and nut of a local or permissive c ha ra cter. Yours on behalt of the council, H. OGDEN, Secretary.</p>
        <p>Rt. Bon. ft,. McKenna.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0349" n="art0349">
        <head>DRINK AND AGRICULTURE. Valuable Land Wasted For Barley GPONS</head>
        <p>DRINK AND AGRICULTURE. Valuable Land Wasted For Barley GPONS</p>
        <p>The workers of the various Band ,of Rope Societies affiliated with the Warrington Band of Hope tnion, assembled in Cairo-street School- room on Wednesday evening, at the special in- vitation of the Temperance Queen (Miss Eva Tanner). In the unavoidable absence of the President (Mrs. Dodds), Mr. T. Dale, chairman of the Executive Committee, occupied the chair:</p>
        <p>In his opening remarks the Chairman referred to the hopeful outlook of the movement brought about by the previa crisis and the effects of drink on our nation. Mr. A. F. Edwards, of Manchester, gave an experimental address showing .the ill-effects of alcohol on the system, and proved to the- audience that total abstainers had been able to accomplish more arduous labour mentally and physically than drinkers. He also pointed out how the drink traffic involved the wastage of a large quantity of agricultural land, owing to its being used for growing barley.. That land might be put to better advantage in growing corn for the nation.</p>
        <p>Several members took part in the discussion which followed. refreshments were provided and songs were given by Misa. Eva Tanner and Mr. J. Hannan, whose ,efforts were Much enjoyed. A hearty Vote of thanks was passed to Miss Tanner. who, 'since she became Temperance Queen, has been very active in her endeavour4 to ,help forward the movement, and has per- sonally obtained over a hundred pledges to total' abstinence. A very pleasant and profitable even- ing concluded with the singing of the National' Anthem. Mr. T. Tanner ably acted as accom- panist. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0350" n="art0350">
        <head>IN JEOPARDY.</head>
        <p>IN JEOPARDY.</p>
        <p>Rev. 11. Ouden And The Menace To</p>
        <p>National Life.</p>
        <p>• Last Sunday evening the Rev. H. Ogden, preached in Golborne-street Baptist Church, when his theme was "The Example of King George." For his text he turned to IL Samuel : " Men that went in jeopardy .of their lives." .The preacher instanced David's concern -- about these men's lives-as real patriotism. He loved Bethlehem, and the water from the well - by the gate revived old memories, but;when the water .was obtained. he remembered the risk at whieh it was secured, andithen poured out the water as an offering unto the Lord. . The jeopardy of then's lives, said Mr. Ogden,. was what has weighed with our noble King. The Chancellor of the Exchequer has made known the peril that drink has brought the nation in this hour of trial. There is no doubt that drink has meant less munitions for the, - men in the field. When allowances have been made for men who are suffering from over- strain—seven. days to the week of.work, or even six days and overtime strains, the body and Mind to a state of collapse—when allowance hag been made for long hours, there is doubtlessY proof beyond diSPute,tbat drink has been, and is, a menace in the hour of the national peril. But one rather suspects that drink has imperilled even the Army and the men in it. Officers of all grades have been its victims, and efficiency has been sacrificed .because of its influence. The King will know the social" side of the Army in a fashion that only those in the inner counsels can know.</p>
        <p>The King's Attitude.</p>
        <p>The King's attitude is horn of the knowledge that men are in jeopardy of their lives. Drink wasted lives at Ntuve Chapelle. Munitions. says Sir John French, will spare the lives of our brave soldiers. - The Chancellor says AC° have three foes—Germany, Austria and drink. When drink curtails ammunition- the order should he inverted. . Drink is easily a premier foe of England. It was an enemy to these islands before a German menace was thought of, and I am pessimist enough to fear it will be a menace when Germans and Britons are friends again. :</p>
        <p>Printed and published by John Walker and Co.• (Warrington), Limited, at the Exastuall General Printing Works, Bewsey-street, WO" rington ; also published by them at 02 EXAMINER Offices, 2, Victoria-road, Widnes; at' George-street, St. Helens; 3, Market-strew. Earleetown, all in the County of Lanosstar,_s and at Church-street, Runcorn. in the/C*ll*W of Chester.</p>
      </div>
