        <p>HINTS AND INTERESTING FACTS AND CHAT FOR AMATELIRS. 4., keepers. The leaflet furnishes some suggestions as to the methods to be adopted, including the distribution over the land of portable . houses of suitable size, the selection and mating of hens, selection of eggs for hatching, &amp;c. As regards the raising of ducks, turkeys, and geese, with a few exceptions, if is pointed out, farmers have hitherto failed to realise the profitable nature of turkey raising, which is not suited to small holdings, as the birds require an abund- ance of range. Farmers might take the oppor- tunity of increasing the general stock of turkeys throughout the country. As to geese, as these are unprofitable unless they can be fed cheaply, they are not recommended for ordinary farms, but much might be done on common or rough grazing laid, where these birds would find their food during the growing stages, and provide a cheap supply for the market. Home production would be greatly increased if agricultural labourers 'were encouraged to keep a few fowls, and if farmers gave permission for these to run over pastures or arable lands after the crops are harvested. If in this way each agricultural labourer were enabled to keep, say, ten fowls, production would be increased to a very considerable extent.</p>
