        <p>I A LENGTHENING LIST OF KILLED AND WOUNDED KILLED. Private J. P. Mitchell. Lance-Corporal Wallace Leslie. Private B. Homer. Lance-Corporal W. Ball. Private A. B. Shaw. Private J. Sixsmith. Private W. Milsom. , Private E. B. Dooney. WOUNDED. Private T. Rusdale. Sergeant J. H. Sollery. Private W. Naylor. . Lance-Corporal H. Hewitt, Private Rimmer (slightly). Lance-Corporal J. Smith. Private R. C. Bradbury. Private T. Shovelton. Private J. Bond (slightly). Private A. Barker. Private J. Reeves. Private J. H. Riley. Private J. Dale. Private W. Robinson. Private H. Hemmings. Private J. Broadhurst. Private W. Mairs. Private L. Gough. KILLED IN ACTION. - , • LANCE-CORPL. WALLACE interested follower of the doings of the War- rington Rugby Football Club. LESLIE. Mr. George Sixsmith, brother of the deceased _ soldier, received ' the first intimation of the ...____ Bandsman Who Turned Fighter death of the gallant warrior. This consisted of a message on a postcard from Sergeant Thomas Lowe. It read : , ' " News was received by Mr. and Mrs. Leslie, of I am very sorry to tell you that your 50, Mersey-street, Warrington, on Thursday brother was shot yesterday and has since morning, of the death of their son, Wallace, . died. You may be assured that his company who was killed in action on April 12th. Deep officer, Captain Smith, did all that was sympathy is felt with Mr. and Mrs. Leslie in possible for him. He was in my platoon, their loss, and we shall all miss him. You will be Lance-Corporal Leslie was 24 years of age. He consoled by knowing that he gave his life was in the Territorials as a bandsman some in a noble cause and' died 'doing his bit.' while ago, but resigned, only to join again when Please accept thb deep sympathy of his com- the war broke out, but ,not, this time, as a patsy and myself." musician. He was educated at the People's College, and on leaving school, was engaged, KILLED BY A SNIPER. with a view to being articled, in the office of The following m