        <p>it is hoped to obtain at least one penny per head of the population. This collection is to be made in all the g parishes in the United Kingdom. It is claimed that if only half of the population • gave the small sum named it would result in the grand total of XlOO,OOO being raised. The appeal was referred to the Parish Council to deal with. , NO SUNDAY LETTERS. Postmaster-General's Decision. The annual Parish Council meeting was held on Tuesday evening, in the Sankey School- room, when the members present were Messrs.- W. Furness, J. Pownall, W Taylors S. • Fir, and A. H. 'Lowe, with Mr. J. Smith (clerk). Those In Authority. It is proposed by Mr. S. Fair, seconded hi Mr. A. H. Lowe, and carried unanimously, - that Mr. W. Furness be elected chairman and Mr. J. G. Pownall vice-chairman. Messrs. W. Wright and W. H. Ireland were re-elected Overseers, and the Rev. J. R. Jones was appointed the Council's repr&amp;entative the Earlestown Education Committee. The Cause of the Delay. With reference to the Sankey Canal Bridge. it was stated that no definite statement had yet been received relative to the completion of the work in progress. This delay was understood to be partially caused by staff changes through enlistments. No Sunda ir Letters. As to the question of a Sunday postal delivery. it was reported that a letter had been received from the Postmaster-General stating that the matter had undergone official consideration, but it was found that circumstances did not at present justify the increase of expenditure 'which would be involved. _ PADGATE. Wesley Guild.—The monthly devotional meet- ing of the Wesley Guild was held on Monday' evening, and was presided over by the Rev. A. J. Morton. Short interesting papers were given by Miss Ada Egerton of Stockton Heath, and Miss L. Brough, of Fearnhead, their subjects being "The Pharisees"' and "Prayer" respectively. Miss B. London. who was tho soloist, gave a sweet rendition of Jude's "Peace be still," which was much appreciated. OR FORD. ,• Whist Drive for Organ Fund.—A very plea- sant evening was spent in Orford School 00 Friday of last week, when a whist drive wa4 held in aid of the organ fund, and as a result of which nearly ,R. 3 was handed over for that purpose. The prizes were won by the follow- ing :—Ladies: 1, Miss • Taylor; 2, Miss Gilbert; ,3, Mrs. liankey.. Gentlemen: 1, Mr. Hankey; 2, Mr. R. Cuppage; 3; Mr. •J.-GibSo'n. The last- named gentleman also officiated as Lc. I** freshments were provided. FITS CAN BE CURED. Every sufferer from the weakness that is the root cause of the trouble is in a continual state of suspense as to when his or her next attack will take place. Whether it will be in the safety of the home or in public, or in some dangerous place, where the sudden deprivation of consciousness may have a tragic sequel. All such sufferers have a certain remedy irt FITZKURE. It has cured hundreds of cases* of Epilepsy, Hysteria., St. Vitus' Dance, ano kindred nervous diseases. A parent writes :--- " I am proud to tell you that my son is going on splendid. Twenty-three weeks now and not a fit. For four years he was unable to earn a penny." Another parent writes :—" My son has had as many as 15 fits in one day. He onlY took four bottles Of your, medicine, and these cured him. You may publish this or refer anyone to me." . A bottle of this famous remedy, which con' tains no. bromide of potassium or any poi6on• and so can be taken with perfect safety by the most delicate, will be sent post free to anY sufferer for 2s. 9d. if addressed to The Londo' Medicine Co., 37, College-street, Bromley, Kent. SOOT WATER FOR PLANTS IN ROOMSi Many plants used in rooms are apt to assunle a yellow appearance. To bring them back to their normal colour there is nothing to equal soot water, which may be prepared as follows: Tie up about a peck of soot in a rough canvas bag and sink it in a tub holding from gallons to 40 gallons of water, stirring it occasionallY until the water has become impregnated with the Soot. If the soot water is too strong, dilute it with clear water until it is in the right con' dition.-If used too strong, the soot water will tn some extent render the surface of the soil inv. pervious to air, and give it a dirty black appear- ance. The best way is to add half a pint of soot water to every gallon of clear water that is used for watering the plants. Many plants will take it stronger than this, but it is always best tc' be on the safe side and use it weak. It i''' wonderful how soon the plants respond to this treatment. BILLIARDS. ADULT SCHOLARS AGAIN MEET ROUNO THE GREEN CLOTH. A return visit from the Stockton Heath Adult School cueists was paid to the Bewiey Adult School on Tuesday last, where an enjoyable evening was spent in a billiard tournament, the scores being as 'follows : Bewsey: H. Perks 75, Stockton 75, 17' Healey 75, G. Boyle 75, F. 4111111bert 68, C. StarkeY 75, D. Elwell 30, W. Boucher 75-548. Stockton Heath:. T. Norbury 54, A. Albinsol 52, W. Hankey 52, A. Spencer 54, R. Lamoar" 75, F. Lynch 51, A. Gamble 75, A. Morrison 71--" 484. DEATH OF A CROYDON CENTENARIAN, The burial took place on Saturday of Mrs' Elizabeth Deacon, the oldest inhabitant of Col" don, , who has reached her 103rd year. She enjoyed the use of all her faculties until month ago, and she had never worn glasses. Till quite recently she supplemented her old age pension by hawking flowers. Her seven childreo . are. all alive, and three are old age pensioners.</p>
